What is memoization in JavaScript?

Know it:

Memoization is an optimization technique that saves (caches) the result of a function for a given input.
If the function is called again with the same input, it returns the saved result instead of calculating again.
It saves time for heavy or repeated calculations.
It is usually done with an object or a Map as the cache, where the key is the input and the value is the result.
It works best with pure functions, which always give the same output for the same input.
It trades memory for speed, because the cache keeps growing.
It is not the same as "memorization" (remembering). The correct word is memoization.
Angular has similar ideas: pure pipe (re-runs only when the input changes) and computed() signal (re-calculates only when dependencies change).


What is a Pure Function in JavaScript?
A pure function is a function that follows two rules:
1. Same input → Same output: When you pass the same arguments, it always returns the same result.
2. No side effects: It does not modify external variables, update the DOM, make API calls, or change data outside the function.
1. Example of a pure function
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

console.log(add(2, 3)); // 5
console.log(add(2, 3)); // 5
console.log(add(4, 3)); // 7

 impure function?
Same input can give a different output (uses Math.random(), Date.now(), or an outside variable).
Or it has side effects: it changes outside data, calls an API, updates the DOM, or writes to storage.
Memoization works only with pure functions.
In Angular, a pure pipe should use a pure function, so it re-runs only when the input changes.

let count = 10;

function increaseCount() {
  count++;
  return count;
}
console.log(increaseCount()); // 11
console.log(increaseCount()); // 12




Question: What is the Event Loop in JavaScript?

Know it:

JavaScript is single-threaded. It runs only one task at a time.
The Event Loop lets JavaScript handle async work (timers, API calls, clicks) without blocking the page.
It keeps checking: "Is the Call Stack empty? If yes, take the next waiting task and run it."
Parts:
Call Stack: runs the code, one function at a time.
Web APIs: the browser handles setTimeout, fetch and events in the background.
Microtask Queue: Promise callbacks (.then, await). Higher priority.
Macrotask Queue: setTimeout, setInterval, event callbacks. Lower priority.
Order of running: Sync code, then all Microtasks, then one Macrotask. Then repeat.
So a Promise always runs before setTimeout, even when the delay is 0.
A long task blocks the Call Stack and freezes the UI. For heavy work use async code or a Web Worker.
Example:


console.log('1');                                  // sync, runs now

setTimeout(() => console.log('2'), 0);             // goes to Macrotask Queue

Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('3'));   // goes to Microtask Queue

console.log('4');                                  // sync, runs now

// Output: 1, 4, 3, 2
// 1 and 4: sync code first
// 3: Promise (microtask) next
// 2: setTimeout (macrotask) last





 What is a Web Worker in JavaScript?

JavaScript is single-threaded. The same main thread runs the code, updates the UI and handles clicks.
When heavy code runs on the main thread (a big loop, parsing a big file), the UI freezes.
A Web Worker runs JavaScript on a separate background thread, so the UI stays smooth.
The main thread and the worker talk using messages:
postMessage(data): send data.
onmessage: receive data.
A worker cannot access the DOM (no document, no window). It only does the work and sends the result back.
Data is copied when it is sent, not shared.
Stop a worker with worker.terminate().




 What is the Spread Operator in JavaScript?
The spread operator is three dots: ...
It expands an array or object into its single items.
It works with arrays, objects, strings and function calls.
Uses: copy, merge, add items, update one property, pass items as function arguments.
It makes a shallow copy. Nested objects are still shared.
When merging objects, if the same key appears twice, the last one wins.
In Angular with OnPush, spread creates a new reference, so the UI updates.

// 1. Copy an array
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
const copy = [...arr];                    // [1, 2, 3]

// 2. Merge arrays
const q1 = ['CH001', 'CH002'];
const q2 = ['CH003'];
const all = [...q1, ...q2];               // ['CH001', 'CH002', 'CH003']

// 3. Add an item
const list = [...arr, 4];                 // [1, 2, 3, 4]

// 4. Merge objects
const user = { name: 'Aniket' };
const role = { role: 'Admin' };
const full = { ...user, ...role };        // { name: 'Aniket', role: 'Admin' }

// 5. Update one property (the last one wins)
const deductor = { tan: 'PNEA12345B', status: 'Pending' };
const updated = { ...deductor, status: 'Filed' };
// { tan: 'PNEA12345B', status: 'Filed' }

// 6. Pass items to a function
const amounts = [5000, 12000, 800];
console.log(Math.max(...amounts));        // 12000

// 7. String to array
console.log([...'PAN']);                  // ['P', 'A', 'N']

// 8. Remove duplicates
const sections = ['194C', '194J', '194C'];
console.log([...new Set(sections)]);      // ['194C', '194J']




Question: What is the Rest Operator in JavaScript?
The rest operator is also three dots: ...
It collects many values into one array or object.
It is used in function parameters and in destructuring.
It must always be the last item.
You can use only one rest per function or destructuring.
It gives a real array, unlike the old arguments object.
Common uses: a function with any number of arguments, taking the first item and keeping the rest, removing a key from an object.


// 1. Function with any number of arguments
function sum(...numbers) {                 // numbers = [10, 20, 30]
  return numbers.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
}
console.log(sum(10, 20, 30));              // 60
console.log(sum(5, 5));                    // 10

// 2. First arguments fixed, rest collected
function log(level, ...messages) {
  console.log(level, messages);
}
log('ERROR', 'File missing', 'Retry'); // ERROR ['File missing', 'Retry']

// 3. Array destructuring
const [first, ...others] = ['Q1', 'Q2', 'Q3', 'Q4'];
console.log(first);                        // 'Q1'
console.log(others);                       // ['Q2', 'Q3', 'Q4']

// 4. Object destructuring: remove a key
const user = { name: 'Aniket', password: '123', city: 'Pune' };
const { password, ...safeUser } = user;
console.log(safeUser);                     // { name: 'Aniket', city: 'Pune' }

// 5. Rest must be last
// function wrong(...a, b) {}              // ❌ SyntaxError




