What is Destructuring in JavaScript?

Destructuring takes values out of an array or object and puts them into separate variables, in one line.
It makes code shorter and cleaner. You don't need to write obj.name, obj.city again and again.
There are two types:
Array destructuring uses [ ] and works by position (order matters).
Object destructuring uses { } and works by key name (order does not matter).
You can give a default value in case the value is undefined.
You can rename a variable: { name: userName }.
You can skip array items by leaving an empty comma.
It works with nested objects and arrays.
It works directly in function parameters.
It does not change the original array or object.



// 1. Basic: by position
const quarters = ['Q1', 'Q2', 'Q3'];
const [a, b, c] = quarters;
console.log(a, b, c);          // Q1 Q2 Q3

// 2. Skip items
const [first, , third] = quarters;
console.log(first, third);     // Q1 Q3

// 3. Default value
const [x, y = 'N/A'] = ['Q1'];
console.log(y);                // N/A

// 4. Swap two variables (no temp variable needed)
let m = 1, n = 2;
[m, n] = [n, m];
console.log(m, n);             // 2 1



const deductor = { tan: 'PNEA12345B', name: 'ABC Ltd', city: 'Pune' };

// 1. Basic: by key name
const { tan, name } = deductor;
console.log(tan, name);        // PNEA12345B ABC Ltd

// 2. Rename
const { name: companyName } = deductor;
console.log(companyName);      // ABC Ltd

// 3. Default value
const { status = 'Pending' } = deductor;
console.log(status);           // Pending (the key does not exist)

// 4. Rename + default together
