What is Optional Chaining (?.) in JavaScript?

Optional chaining is written as ?.
It safely reads a property even when the value before it is null or undefined.
If the value is null or undefined, it stops and returns undefined instead of throwing an error.
Without it you get the error: Cannot read properties of undefined.
It replaces long checks like user && user.address && user.address.city.

// ❌ Old
const city = user && user.address && user.address.city;
// ✅ New
const city = user?.address?.city;

What is ?? (Nullish Coalescing) in JavaScript?
?? means: "If the left value is empty, use the right value."

let name = null;
console.log(name ?? 'Guest');      // Guest   (name is null, so use 'Guest')

let name2 = undefined;
console.log(name2 ?? 'Guest');     // Guest   (undefined, so use 'Guest')

let name3 = 'Aniket';
console.log(name3 ?? 'Guest');     // Aniket  (has a value, so keep it)


let tdsAmount = 0;                     // real value: tax is zero

console.log(tdsAmount || 'No data');   // 'No data'  ❌ wrong, 0 was replaced
console.log(tdsAmount ?? 'No data');   // 0          ✅ right, 0 is kept
























