            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Dark mode &amp; theming</td>
              <td>CSS</td>
              <td>Define colors as variables, switch them with prefers-color-scheme or a data-theme attribute.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">@layer, @supports &amp; nesting</td>
              <td>CSS</td>
              <td>Control the cascade with layers, test for features, and nest rules.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">:focus-within, logical properties &amp; gap</td>
              <td>CSS</td>
              <td>Style a parent when a child is focused, and write direction-friendly CSS.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">scroll-snap, sticky &amp; scroll behavior</td>
              <td>CSS</td>
              <td>Native carousels and smooth scrolling without JavaScript.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">@font-face &amp; font loading</td>
              <td>CSS</td>
              <td>Load custom fonts without invisible or jumping text.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">filter, backdrop-filter, clip-path &amp; shadows</td>
              <td>CSS</td>
              <td>Visual effects with no images.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">contain &amp; content-visibility</td>
              <td>CSS</td>
              <td>Skip rendering work for off-screen content.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">pointer-events, user-select, cursor &amp; appearance</td>
              <td>CSS</td>
              <td>Small properties that control how an element reacts to the user.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Text wrapping &amp; typography properties</td>
              <td>CSS</td>
              <td>word-break, overflow-wrap, white-space, line-height, and text-wrap.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">vertical-align &amp; inline gaps</td>
              <td>CSS</td>
              <td>Why there is a gap under images and between inline-blocks.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">3D transforms &amp; perspective</td>
              <td>CSS</td>
              <td>Flip cards and depth effects.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">CSS reset, critical CSS &amp; architecture</td>
              <td>CSS</td>
              <td>Start from a consistent base, and keep the first paint fast.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">A page with a 5,000-row table is slow. What do you do?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Measure first, then cut what is rendered: pagination or virtual scroll, OnPush, trackBy, and fewer bindings.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The app gets slower the longer a user keeps it open. How do you find the cause?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Take heap snapshots to find what keeps growing, then fix subscriptions, timers, listeners, and detached DOM.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The first load of the app takes 12 seconds. How do you reduce it?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Analyze the bundle, lazy load routes, remove heavy libraries, enable compression and caching, and use @defer.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The same API is called 5 times when a page loads. How do you fix it?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Find who subscribes, then share one request with shareReplay, a service cache, or a signal-based store.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The search box sometimes shows the results of an older search. Why, and what is the fix?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>It is a race condition between responses; switchMap cancels the previous request.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Users click Save twice and get duplicate records. How do you stop it?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Disable the button while saving, use exhaustMap, and make the server idempotent.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The browser freezes when a component renders, and the console shows NG0100 or an endless loop. What do you check?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>A binding is changing during change detection, or a getter or effect keeps changing its own input.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Users upload Excel files with 50,000 rows and the browser hangs. What do you do?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Do not parse on the main thread: upload to the server, or parse in a Web Worker and send in chunks, with progress.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">A user is filling a long form and the token expires. What should happen?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Refresh the token silently in an interceptor, replay the request, and never lose the form data.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Different roles must see different menus, pages, and buttons. How do you design it?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Permissions from the token or API, a guard per route, a directive for UI, and the server as the real check.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The backend changes an API response and your screen breaks. How do you protect against it?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Map API data in one place, type it, use versioned endpoints, and add contract checks.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">A security audit found an XSS issue. How do you fix it and prevent more?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Stop rendering untrusted HTML, sanitize, and add a CSP and review rules.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The business wants live notifications and offline support. What would you use?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>WebSocket or SSE for live data, a service worker for offline, and a queue for offline writes.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How would you cache API data in an Angular app?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Match the cache to how often data changes: in-memory with shareReplay, HTTP caching headers, an interceptor, or a store.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The same build must run in dev, QA, and production with different API URLs. How?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Load a config.json at startup (APP_INITIALIZER), so the build is not rebuilt per environment.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">After a deployment, some users still see the old version. Why?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>index.html or the service worker was cached; fix cache headers and prompt for refresh.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you decide between a service with signals, NgRx, and local component state?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Start with the smallest tool; add NgRx only for large, shared, complex state.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Your project still uses NgModules. How would you move to standalone components?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Use the CLI migration in steps, with tests passing after each step.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">You must build a form with 200 fields that changes by customer type. How?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>A config-driven dynamic form, split into sections, with reusable field components and validators.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you design a reusable table or dropdown that many teams will use?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>A small, typed API with inputs and outputs, content projection, accessibility, and documentation.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Would you use micro frontends for this project?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Only when separate teams must deploy independently; otherwise a modular monolith is simpler.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The marketing pages in your Angular app must be found on Google. What do you do?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Use SSR or prerendering, set titles and meta tags per route, and keep content out of client-only code.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">You inherit a large JavaScript project. How do you move it to TypeScript?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Move gradually: allowJs, then convert file by file, then tighten strictness.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Production shows "Cannot read properties of undefined". How do you handle it?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Reproduce with the logged data, find the missing guard, fix the cause, and add monitoring and a test.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The layout works on desktop, but breaks on phones. How do you debug and fix it?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Check the viewport tag, find overflowing elements, and make layouts fluid with grid or flex and relative units.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">A dropdown is hidden behind another element, even with a huge z-index. Why?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Stacking contexts: the dropdown's ancestor has a lower stacking order; render it at the body level.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">A callback logs "undefined" for this.name, though the method worked a minute ago. What happened?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>The method was passed as a callback and lost its this; use an arrow function or bind.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">A critical bug is reported in production on a Friday evening. What do you do?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Confirm and size the impact, mitigate first (rollback or flag), then fix properly and follow up.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you give code review feedback, and how do you take it?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Be specific and kind, separate must-fix from preferences, and treat feedback as learning.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">You are asked for an estimate, and later you will miss the deadline. What do you do?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Break the work down when estimating, and raise risk early with options.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">You must change old, untested code that nobody understands. How do you do it safely?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Add characterization tests first, make small safe changes, and avoid a big rewrite.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The requirements are unclear and the product owner is not available. What do you do?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>List assumptions and questions, work on what is clear, and confirm early with a small demo.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Tell me about yourself.</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Present, past, future in about 90 seconds: your role now, key experience, and why this role.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are your strengths?</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Pick two or three that match the job, and back each one with a real example.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is your weakness?</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Name a real, fixable weakness, and show what you are doing about it.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Why are you leaving your current company?</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Talk about growth and what you are moving toward, and never criticize your employer.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Why do you want to join us?</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Show you researched the company: product, tech, and how your skills fit.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Where do you see yourself in five years?</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>A realistic path of growing skills and responsibility, in a way that fits the role.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is the STAR method?</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Situation, Task, Action, Result: a way to tell a short, clear story.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Tell me about a conflict with a teammate, and how you handled it.</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Stay calm and professional: listen, find the real issue, agree on a fix, and focus on the result.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Tell me about a mistake or a failure.</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Own it, explain the fix and what you changed so it does not happen again.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you handle pressure and tight deadlines?</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Prioritize, communicate early, break down the work, and protect quality on the critical parts.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you work with a difficult team member or manager?</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Understand their point of view, communicate directly and respectfully, and stay professional.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you keep your skills up to date?</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Name specific habits: official docs, side projects, courses, and applying new things at work.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Have you led or mentored anyone?</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Even without a title: reviews, onboarding, knowledge sharing, and taking ownership of a module.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are your salary expectations?</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Give a researched range, link it to the role and market, and stay flexible on the total package.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is your notice period? Can you join earlier?</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>State it honestly, and say what you can negotiate in a professional way.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Do you have any questions for us?</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Always ask two or three thoughtful questions about the work, team, and growth.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Why should we hire you?</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Connect your skills and results to what the job needs, in a confident and short answer.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is the difference between const, readonly and as const?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td><code>const</code> locks the variable, <code>readonly</code> locks the type, <code>as const</code> locks the literal value; none freeze at runtime.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do optional chaining and nullish coalescing work with strictNullChecks?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td>Use <code>?.</code> to read safely and <code>??</code> for defaults; unlike <code>||</code>, it keeps <code>0</code> and empty strings.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is the difference between unknown[] and any[]?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td><code>unknown[]</code> is the safe choice for untrusted arrays: it makes you check each item before use.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you handle unknown in catch blocks and type API responses safely?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td>Narrow <code>unknown</code> errors with <code>instanceof</code>, and validate API data at runtime (e.g. Zod), because generics are not checks.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is the this parameter, and what are call signatures?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td><code>this: T</code> types the calling context and costs nothing at runtime; call signatures type callable objects.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you use type guards for API responses that can have different shapes?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td>Model varying responses as a union, then narrow with a field check or an <code>is</code> guard.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">When should you use function overloads instead of a union?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td>Union when the result type is the same, overloads when the result type depends on the input type.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you type HttpClient, Observables, signals and typed forms in Angular?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td>Give HttpClient, signals and forms a generic type and it flows to the template; HTTP types are not checked at runtime.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are generic defaults, and how do you build a typed repository service?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td>Constraints set the minimum shape and defaults make type arguments optional: ideal for generic CRUD services.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you use Partial and Pick for DTOs versus the model?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td>Derive Create (Omit), Update (Partial) and view (Pick) types from one model instead of copying fields.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Why use Readonly&lt;T&gt; for NgRx state?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td>Mark state <code>readonly</code> so mutations fail at compile time; always return a new object from reducers.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do abstract classes and InjectionToken give type-safe DI in Angular?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td>Interfaces cannot be DI tokens; use an abstract class or a typed <code>InjectionToken&lt;T&gt;</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Why do Angular classes use the ! operator with strictPropertyInitialization?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td><code>!</code> promises Angular will set the field later; prefer <code>input.required()</code> and signal queries.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is the difference between Record and Map?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td>Use <code>Record</code> for fixed or JSON-like keys, and <code>Map</code> for dynamic keys, object keys or frequent add and delete.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Why does Object.keys return string[] and not keyof T?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td><code>Object.keys</code> returns <code>string[]</code> because objects may have extra keys; cast only when you are sure.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is the difference between a TypeScript namespace and ES modules?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td>Use ES modules, not namespaces; namespaces are a legacy way to avoid globals.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are tsconfig paths aliases, and what do you watch out for?</td>
              <td>TypeScript</td>
              <td><code>paths</code> gives clean import aliases; other tools such as Jest need the same mapping set up separately.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is tap() really for, and what can go wrong with it?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>tap is for side effects only (log, loader, analytics); it never changes values, and the observer form covers subscribe, unsubscribe and finalize.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are skip, skipWhile, skipUntil and skipLast?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>skip(1) hides a BehaviorSubject's starting value; skipWhile stops skipping once, and skipUntil waits for a notifier.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">take, takeWhile, takeLast: how do they differ?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>takeWhile stops at the first false and drops that value unless you pass inclusive = true; takeLast needs the source to complete.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">first vs. take(1) vs. last vs. single: what is the difference?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>take(1) is quiet on empty, first() errors on empty, and single() demands exactly one match.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">defaultIfEmpty, throwIfEmpty and isEmpty: how do you handle empty streams?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>defaultIfEmpty gives a fallback, throwIfEmpty gives a clear error, isEmpty gives true or false when the stream completes.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">count, min, max, every, find, findIndex, elementAt: what do they do?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>These are array methods for streams; on infinite streams use only the early-finishing ones (find, every, elementAt).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What does pairwise() do and when is it useful?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>pairwise gives [previous, current] for diff logic; it skips the first value unless you use startWith.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">distinctUntilChanged with a comparator vs. distinct: which and when?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>distinctUntilChanged removes consecutive repeats (use a comparator for objects); distinct removes all repeats but remembers everything.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is ignoreElements() used for?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>ignoreElements keeps only complete and error, so use it when the end of the stream is the signal.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is a higher-order observable, and what are mergeAll, switchAll, concatAll, exhaustAll?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>xxxMap is map plus xxxAll; pick merge (parallel), concat (queue), switch (latest wins) or exhaust (ignore while busy).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you limit parallel requests (max N at a time)?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>mergeMap(fn, N) runs at most N inner requests in parallel and queues the rest; N = 1 equals concatMap.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">repeat vs. retry, and what about repeatWhen?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>retry reacts to errors, repeat reacts to completion; repeat({ delay }) is the simple polling loop.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you retry an HTTP call with a delay or backoff?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>retry({ count, delay }) does backoff; return throwError from delay to stop retrying on errors that will never succeed.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">timeout, delayWhen and timeInterval: what do they do?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>timeout turns silence into an error or a fallback, delayWhen delays per value, timeInterval measures gaps between values.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">expand and mergeScan: how do you load paged data recursively?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>expand repeats a call using the last result until you return EMPTY; mergeScan is scan with an observable accumulator.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">window, windowTime, bufferTime, bufferWhen: what is the difference?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>buffer gives arrays, window gives inner observables; bufferTime is the usual way to batch events before sending them.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are materialize and dematerialize?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>materialize wraps next, error and complete as plain values; dematerialize unwraps them.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">throttleTime with leading and trailing, and audit vs. throttle?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>throttle takes the first (or last, with trailing) of a window; audit always takes the last; set both flags to get both ends.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">debounce(durationSelector): when is it better than debounceTime?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>debounceTime has a fixed wait; debounce(fn) computes the wait for each value.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you create an Observable by hand, and what is the teardown function?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>new Observable runs per subscriber, and the function you return is the cleanup that runs on unsubscribe, error or complete.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you write a custom RxJS operator?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>A custom operator is just (source) =&gt; Observable; reuse it by composing existing operators with pipe().</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are schedulers, observeOn and subscribeOn?</td>
              <td>RxJS</td>
              <td>Schedulers control when values are delivered; observeOn changes where values come out, subscribeOn changes where the subscription starts.</td>
