              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Use IndexedDB for large offline data; use localStorage only for tiny sync values. See <a href="#js-web-storage">web storage</a>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are JavaScript source maps and minification?</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Minify for size, source maps for debugging; in production keep maps private and upload them to your error tracker.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do polyfills work in a real project? (core-js, browserslist, feature detection)</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Set browserslist, load core-js only for missing features found by feature detection, and avoid polyfilling what your users do not need.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Webpack vs Vite vs esbuild vs Rollup: what is the difference?</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Webpack is flexible but slow, esbuild is fast, Rollup suits libraries, Vite gives fast dev; Angular now uses esbuild plus Vite.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is tree shaking and the sideEffects flag?</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Tree shaking drops unused ES-module exports; use named imports and mark side-effect-free packages with sideEffects.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is code splitting and dynamic import()?</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Dynamic import() creates a lazy chunk, so users download only the code the current screen needs.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Explain npm semver (^ and ~), package-lock, npm ci, npm audit and pnpm/yarn</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>^ allows minor updates, ~ only patches, the lock file pins the tree, and CI should run npm ci.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">ESM vs CommonJS: what interop problems happen?</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Prefer ESM for tree shaking; when mixing with CJS, use dynamic import() and watch the default-export shape.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are the types of XSS and how do you prevent them?</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Never put untrusted data in innerHTML; escape or sanitize output and add a CSP as a second wall.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is CSRF and how does SameSite help?</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>CSRF abuses automatic cookies; defend with SameSite cookies plus a CSRF token on write requests.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is clickjacking and how do you stop it?</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Block framing with CSP frame-ancestors (or X-Frame-Options) so your pages cannot be hidden in another site's iframe.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is Content Security Policy (CSP)?</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>CSP is a browser allow-list that limits damage from XSS; roll it out in report-only mode first.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Where do you store a JWT: localStorage, memory or cookie?</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Safest common choice is an access token in memory plus a refresh token in an HttpOnly SameSite cookie; localStorage is XSS-exposed.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Which OWASP Top 10 items should a frontend developer know?</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>The frontend is untrusted; security lives on the server, and the frontend avoids XSS, leaked secrets and vulnerable packages.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How does HTTP caching work: Cache-Control, ETag and 304?</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Cache hashed assets for a year, revalidate index.html with ETag, and a 304 means "use your copy".</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Same-origin vs same-site, and the CORS preflight in short</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Origin includes port, site does not; CORS is enforced by the browser and fixed on the server with preflight answers.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Implement an LRU cache</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Map plus delete-and-reinsert gives O(1) LRU; the first key is always the least recently used.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Implement deep equal</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Deep equal is a recursive check of identity, type, key count and each value; call out Dates and cycles as edge cases.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Run promises with a concurrency limit (promise pool)</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Start N workers that each pull the next task from a shared index; RxJS mergeMap with a concurrent argument does it too.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Debounce with cancel and flush</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Keep the timer and last arguments in the closure; cancel clears the timer and flush runs the pending call now.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Throttle with leading and trailing calls</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Leading runs at the start, trailing runs the last call at the end of the window; use both for scroll or resize so the final state is handled.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Implement Promise.race, allSettled and any</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>race settles with the first result, allSettled never rejects, any resolves on first success and rejects with AggregateError.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Implement an EventEmitter with on, off, once and emit</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Store handlers in a Map of Sets; once wraps the handler so it removes itself, and copy the set before emit.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Binary tree traversal: DFS and BFS</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>DFS uses recursion or a stack, BFS uses a queue; both visit every node in O(n).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Memoize with a TTL (expiry time)</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Store value plus timestamp, and treat an entry older than the TTL as a miss.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Array helpers: chunk, zip, groupBy and uniqueBy</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>reduce builds groups, Map removes duplicates by key, and Array.from with a length builds chunks and zips.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Longest substring without repeating characters and valid parentheses</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Sliding window with a last-seen Map gives O(n); a stack matches brackets in O(n).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Output questions: promise chain, finally, getters and this</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>A throw skips to catch, finally runs and passes values on; var shares one binding, let makes one per loop turn; extracted methods lose this.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Output questions: array holes, equality and references</td>
              <td>JavaScript</td>
              <td>Holes are skipped by map, objects as keys become "[object Object]", and the order is sync, then microtasks, then timers.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are the HTTP methods, and which are safe or idempotent?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Safe = no change; idempotent = repeat is harmless; GET/PUT/DELETE can be retried, POST cannot without a key.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Which HTTP status codes must you know?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>401 = who are you, 403 = not allowed, 422 = invalid data, 429 = slow down, 5xx = server problem, safe to retry GETs.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are REST principles and good URL design?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>REST = resources as nouns, HTTP methods as verbs, stateless requests, correct status codes.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">REST vs GraphQL vs gRPC: when to choose which?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>REST for simple and cacheable, GraphQL for flexible client queries, gRPC for fast internal service calls.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 vs HTTP/3: what changes for a frontend developer?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>HTTP/2 multiplexes requests on one connection, HTTP/3 uses QUIC; sprites and domain sharding are no longer needed.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How does HTTP caching work (Cache-Control, ETag, stale-while-revalidate)?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Cache hashed assets forever, never cache index.html, and use ETag with 304 for data that may change.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is the difference between authentication and authorization?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Authn = who are you (401), authz = what can you do (403); the real check always lives on the server.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Session cookie vs JWT vs OAuth2/OIDC: how do they differ?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Cookie = server session, JWT = stateless token, OAuth2/OIDC = delegated login; SPAs use code flow with PKCE, never implicit.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are common API conventions for pagination, filtering and sorting?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Paginate every list; cursor pagination beats offset for large or live data.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you handle API versioning and backward compatibility?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Add, do not break; ship breaking changes under a new version and give clients time to migrate.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is rate limiting and how do you handle a 429 in the frontend?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>On 429 honor Retry-After, back off with jitter, and cut needless requests.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is an idempotency key and why use it?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>An idempotency key makes a POST safe to retry because the server treats the same key as the same request.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How does a WebSocket connection start?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>WebSocket = HTTP Upgrade, 101 response, then full-duplex messages; you must code reconnects.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Merge vs rebase vs squash: what is the difference?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Rebase your own branch to stay current, squash or merge into main, and never rewrite shared history.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Which Git commands help in real life (stash, cherry-pick, reset, revert, reflog, bisect)?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Reset rewrites your local history, revert is safe on shared branches, reflog saves you after mistakes.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you resolve a merge conflict?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Understand both changes, merge by hand, run tests, and keep branches short so conflicts stay small.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">GitFlow vs trunk-based development, and what makes a good PR?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Prefer short-lived branches and small focused PRs with a clear what, why and how to test.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are semantic versioning and conventional commits?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>SemVer says what kind of change a version holds; conventional commits let tools automate versions and changelogs.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are Scrum ceremonies and what is your role as a developer?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>I refine stories early, estimate honestly, raise blockers daily, and call a story done only by the Definition of Done.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you keep Jira tickets clean and estimate work?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Clean tickets have criteria, links and honest status; estimates are ranges with stated assumptions.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What stages does a CI/CD pipeline have?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Pipeline = lint, test, build, scan, deploy, verify, with a fast and tested rollback.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are environments, blue-green, canary and feature flags?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Blue-green and canary cut deploy risk; feature flags separate deploying code from releasing it.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you use Docker for an Angular app?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Multi-stage Docker: Node builds, nginx serves a small image, and the same image runs in every environment.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you monitor and log a frontend in production?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Track errors with source maps, measure real users, log safely, and alert on trends someone owns.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What does "production ready" mean?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Production ready = tested, secure, accessible, observable and reversible, not just "works on my machine".</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is SOLID, with Angular examples?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>SOLID = small focused classes, extend without editing, and depend on abstractions that Angular DI can swap.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What do DRY, KISS and YAGNI mean, and why composition over inheritance?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Keep it simple, build only what is needed, avoid wrong duplication, and compose instead of inheriting.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Which design patterns appear in Angular apps?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Angular already gives singleton, observer, decorator and factory; you add facade, adapter and strategy yourself.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is clean code: naming and small functions?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Clean code reads like prose: clear names, small single-purpose functions, early returns.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is separation of concerns and layered architecture in a frontend?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Keep UI, state and data access apart so you can test and change each without touching the others.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is the testing pyramid, and what are TDD and BDD?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Many fast unit tests, some integration, few E2E; TDD is red-green-refactor, BDD is Given-When-Then.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Mocking vs stubbing vs spying, and what does code coverage mean?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Stubs feed data, spies and mocks verify calls, and coverage finds gaps but never proves quality.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is technical debt and how do you handle refactoring?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Make debt visible, pay it in small tested steps, and keep refactor PRs separate from features.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is a performance mindset?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Measure, find the bottleneck, fix it, measure again, and guard it with budgets.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What accessibility basics must you know (WCAG)?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Aim for WCAG AA: semantic HTML, keyboard access, good contrast, and test with real tools.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What is a frontend security checklist?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Escape output, restrict scripts with CSP, protect tokens, hide no secrets in code, and audit dependencies.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Which browser DevTools skills do you need?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>Network for requests, Performance for slow rendering, Memory for leaks, Coverage for dead code, Lighthouse for audits.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you document a project: README and ADR?</td>
              <td>Engineering</td>
              <td>A README says how to run the project; an ADR says why a decision was made, and both live in the repo.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The app is on Angular 12 and you must move to a current version. Third-party libraries block the upgrade. How do you plan it?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Upgrade one major at a time with tests in place, ship after each step, and deal with blocking libraries as separate tracked tasks.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Management wants to move a big app from Zone.js to zoneless. How do you do it gradually?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Go OnPush first, then signals, then remove Zone.js; the risky part is old code and libraries that depended on zone triggers.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">A library used in 40 places is deprecated and no longer maintained. What do you do?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Wrap the library behind your own API, migrate step by step, then ban the old import so it cannot come back.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">SharedModule has 300 declarations, everything imports it, and you have circular dependencies. How do you break it up?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Standalone pieces plus a one-way dependency rule (features use shared, never the reverse) remove both the giant module and the cycles.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The production build takes 15 minutes. How do you bring it down?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Time each stage, then cache, parallelize, use the esbuild builder, and build only what changed.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Tests fail randomly in CI but pass on your machine. How do you handle flaky tests?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Flaky usually means time, shared state, or real network; fix the root cause, and quarantine with a deadline, never ignore.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">A long form must autosave drafts, and warn users who leave with unsaved changes. How do you build it?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Autosave with debounce plus a status label, protect navigation with canDeactivate and beforeunload, and plan for save failures.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Users want filters, sort, and page to be kept in the URL, so they can share links and use the back button. How?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Keep list state in query params and load data from them; you get shareable links and a working back button for free.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Two users edit the same record. The last save silently overwrites the first. How do you prevent it?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Send the version with every update, let the server reject stale writes with 409, and give the user a merge choice.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Users need to export a large report (100,000 rows) to Excel or PDF. Server or client?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Export what is on screen on the client, but use a server job with progress and a download link for big or permissioned data.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">How do you design loading and error handling for the whole app, so every page is consistent?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Handle cross-cutting errors in one interceptor and handler, and design every screen for loading, empty, error, and data states.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">One app must serve many customers, each with its own colors, logo, and name (multi-tenant white label). How?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>One build, CSS variable tokens, and tenant config loaded at startup give you white labeling without forking the app.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Users in different countries see the wrong dates, sometimes one day off. How do you fix and prevent it?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Store moments in UTC, show them local, and treat date-only values as plain strings that you never convert.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">A table gets 100 WebSocket messages per second, and the UI freezes. What do you do?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Buffer messages into batches, update rows by id, and render only what is visible, so the UI redraws a few times a second, not 100.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">You must release a big feature gradually, with the option to turn it off fast. How?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Flags let you ship code dark, roll out by percentage, and switch off without a deploy; remove them after rollout.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Your team needs a grid or chart library. How do you choose a third-party library?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Judge health, fit, cost, and exit plan, prove it with a small spike, and record the decision.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The Lighthouse performance score is 45. How do you raise it?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Fix by metric: LCP with images and blocking resources, TBT with less JS, CLS with reserved space, and guard it in CI.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">An accessibility audit failed. Where do you start, and how do you stop it from coming back?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Start with keyboard, semantic HTML, labels, and contrast, then add automated checks to CI so it stays fixed.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">A backend API is slow or down. How should the Angular app behave?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Use timeouts, limited retries, stale cache, and per-widget errors, so a failing API degrades one feature, not the app.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Design a dashboard with 15 widgets, each with its own data. How do you keep it fast and stable?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Independent widgets with their own state, deferred loading, shared caches, and isolated errors keep a busy dashboard fast.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">You start a new large Angular app from scratch. How do you structure the folders and architecture?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Organize by feature with core and shared layers, enforce import rules with tooling, and add heavy tools only when needed.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">You join a new team that has no testing strategy. How do you set one up?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Use a testing pyramid (many unit, some component, few e2e), enforce it in CI, and add a regression test for every bug.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Files must be downloaded only by logged-in users. A plain link does not send the token. What do you do?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Download through HttpClient as a blob for normal files, or use a short-lived signed URL for big ones, and never put the token in a URL.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">A junior developer's pull requests always have many issues. How do you help them?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Find the root cause, give kind and specific feedback, pair on real tasks, and automate repeated mistakes.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The product owner adds new features in the middle of the sprint and keeps the same deadline. How do you push back?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Do not refuse; show the cost in days, offer options, and let the owner choose the priority, then write it down.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Your manager wants only new features, but tech debt is slowing the team. How do you make the case?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Sell tech debt as lost speed and higher risk with real numbers, and ask for a small fixed share of every sprint.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">You join a company with a big unknown codebase. How do you spend your first two weeks?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Run it, map it, ship a small fix early, and write down what you learn; do not start a big rewrite.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">There is an old, fragile module that nobody wants to touch. You are asked to own it. What do you do?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Take ownership by adding tests and docs first, improving in small steps, and sharing the knowledge.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">The designer wants a pixel-perfect result, but the deadline is close. How do you handle it?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Agree on tokens and what really matters, show the time cost of details, and schedule polish as a follow-up.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">A teammate keeps pushing code without tests. How do you deal with it?</td>
              <td>Scenario</td>
              <td>Talk first and help, then fix it with team rules and CI checks, so quality does not depend on one person's habit.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">Explain your current project and its architecture</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Go product, architecture, then your role with one number; practise it until it takes under three minutes.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="name">What are your daily responsibilities and what do you own?</td>
              <td>HR</td>
              <td>Describe a normal day, then stress the parts you own from requirement to release.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
