    <!-- ================= ANGULAR FUNDAMENTALS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="chapter-divider first">
        <span class="chapter-label">Part A · Angular core fundamentals</span>
        <div class="chapter-title">Angular core fundamentals</div>
        <p class="chapter-dek">The building blocks every Angular app is made of.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">01</span>
        <h2>Angular fundamentals</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="what-is-angular" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Overview</span>
        <h3>What is Angular?</h3>
        <p>Angular is a frontend framework developed by Google, used to build single page web applications.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Uses components, modules, services, and dependency injection to build scalable applications.</li>
          <li>Supports TypeScript, and provides built-in features like routing, forms, and HTTP API integration.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A Google frontend framework for building single page web applications.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="building-blocks" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Overview</span>
        <h3>What are the main building blocks of Angular?</h3>
        <p>Angular is built on five main building blocks that work together to create an application:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Components</b> — define the UI and logic.</li>
          <li><b>Templates</b> — define the HTML structure.</li>
          <li><b>Modules</b> — organize the parts of the app.</li>
          <li><b>Services &amp; Dependency Injection</b> — the reusable logic / business layer.</li>
          <li><b>Routing</b> — navigation between views.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Components, templates, modules, services/DI, and routing.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="angular-component" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Building block</span>
        <h3>What is a Component in Angular?</h3>
        <p>A component is the building block of an Angular application. It controls the part of the UI shown on the
          screen.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A TypeScript class for logic.</li>
          <li>An HTML template for the UI.</li>
          <li>A CSS file for styling.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> The building block that controls one part of the UI: class, template, and styles.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="component-architecture" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Architecture</span>
        <h3>Component-based architecture</h3>
        <p>Component-based architecture means the application is divided into small, reusable, and independent
          components. Each one has its own HTML template, TypeScript logic, and CSS styles.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Improves code reusability, maintainability, and scalability.</li>
          <li>Makes the application easier to maintain and scale.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> The app is split into small, reusable, independent components.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="spa" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Concept</span>
        <h3>What is a Single Page Application?</h3>
        <p>A Single Page Application (SPA) loads only one HTML page once, and updates content dynamically without
          refreshing the full page.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The whole page does not reload — only the component or content changes dynamically.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> One HTML page loads once; content updates without a full page refresh.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="angular-architecture" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Architecture</span>
        <h3>Angular architecture</h3>
        <p>Angular architecture means how an Angular application is structured internally — mainly using components,
          services, modules, routing, and data binding.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Components</b> — control the UI. Contain HTML, CSS, and TypeScript.</li>
          <li><b>Services</b> — contain business logic and API calls, injected using Dependency Injection.</li>
          <li><b>Modules</b> (or standalone components in Angular 17+) — organize features.</li>
          <li><b>Routing</b> — used to navigate between pages.</li>
          <li><b>Dependency Injection</b> — Angular creates and provides service instances automatically.</li>
          <li><b>Data Binding</b> — Interpolation, Property Binding, Event Binding, and Two-way Binding. See
            <a href="#data-binding">Data binding</a>.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> How components, services, DI, modules, and routing fit together.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="angular-cli" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Tooling</span>
        <h3>What is Angular CLI?</h3>
        <p>Angular CLI is a command-line tool used to create, run, build, and manage Angular applications using
          commands, instead of writing everything manually.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Automates development tasks like generating components, services, and modules, and building the
            project.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A command-line tool to create, run, build, and generate parts of an app.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ngmodule" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Organization</span>
        <h3>What is NgModule?</h3>
        <p>NgModule is a container that organizes an Angular application by grouping components, directives, pipes,
          and services in one place. It tells Angular how the different parts of the app work together.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Declares components, directives, and pipes.</li>
          <li>Imports other modules.</li>
          <li>Defines which component starts the app (bootstrap).</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Groups components, directives, pipes, and services, and tells Angular how they fit together.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="standalone-components" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Angular 15+/17+</span>
        <h3>Standalone Components</h3>
        <p>Standalone Components are components that don't need an NgModule. They are self-contained, and can be used
          directly.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Makes the project simpler, and reduces extra code.</li>
          <li>Makes lazy loading easier, and improves performance.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>With standalone components, modules are no longer required in many cases, because a component can manage
          its own dependencies directly. Standalone APIs let us build directives and pipes the same way, without
          declaring them inside a module.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Components that don't need an NgModule, manage their own dependencies (Angular 15+/17+).</div>
      </article>

      <article id="bootstrapping" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Startup</span>
        <h3>How does Angular work internally?</h3>
        <p>Angular starts from <code>main.ts</code>, and loads the root module and root component — this is called
          bootstrapping.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Angular builds the UI using a component tree.</li>
          <li>It converts HTML templates into JavaScript code, and creates the DOM.</li>
          <li>It binds data between the template and TypeScript, and uses change detection to update the UI
            automatically when data changes.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In modern Angular (17+), we usually use standalone components, so <code>app.module.ts</code> is often not
          required — instead of <code>bootstrapModule(AppModule)</code>, we use
          <code>bootstrapApplication(AppComponent)</code>.
        </p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Bootstraps from main.ts, builds the component tree, binds data, runs change detection.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= PROJECT & CONFIG FILES ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">02</span>
        <h2>Project &amp; configuration files</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="index-html" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Entry file</span>
        <h3>Role of index.html in Angular</h3>
        <p>index.html is the main entry HTML file of an Angular application — the first file the browser loads. It
          contains the root selector, like <code>&lt;app-root&gt;</code>, which is where Angular starts.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Holds the page title, favicon, meta tags, and SEO tags.</li>
          <li>Loads external scripts and styles.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> The entry HTML file the browser loads; holds the root selector.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="package-json" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Configuration</span>
        <h3>What is package.json?</h3>
        <p>package.json is the main configuration file of an Angular project. It contains project information,
          dependencies, dev dependencies, and npm scripts used to run and manage the application.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Project information</b> — name, version, and similar metadata.</li>
          <li><b>Dependencies</b> — libraries required to run the application.</li>
          <li><b>Dev dependencies</b> — libraries used only during development.</li>
          <li><b>Scripts</b> — commands we execute, like start and build.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>It's what lets <code>npm install</code> install every required package for the project.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Project metadata, dependencies, devDependencies, and npm scripts.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="package-lock-json" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Configuration</span>
        <h3>What is package-lock.json?</h3>
        <p>package-lock.json is an automatically generated file that stores the exact versions of every installed
          package and its dependencies. It ensures every developer, and every environment, installs the same
          versions — avoiding compatibility issues.</p>
        <p><b>Should we commit it to Git?</b> Yes, always. It:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Ensures consistent builds.</li>
          <li>Prevents "works on my machine" problems.</li>
          <li>Makes deployments reproducible.</li>
          <li>Keeps team environments synchronized.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Locks the exact installed versions of every package, for consistent builds.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="angular-json" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Configuration</span>
        <h3>What is angular.json?</h3>
        <p>angular.json is the main configuration file of an Angular project. It controls how the application is
          built, served, tested, and configured — Angular CLI uses it while building and running the app.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Build configuration</b> — how Angular builds the application.</li>
          <li><b>Serve configuration</b> — controls <code>ng serve</code>.</li>
          <li><b>Global styles</b> — SCSS, Bootstrap, global CSS files.</li>
          <li><b>Global scripts.</b></li>
          <li><b>Environment configurations.</b></li>
          <li><b>Assets</b> — images, icons, fonts, JSON files.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> The Angular CLI config for building, serving, and testing the app.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="tsconfig-json" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Configuration</span>
        <h3>What is tsconfig.json?</h3>
        <p>tsconfig.json is the TypeScript configuration file — the instruction file for the TypeScript compiler,
          telling it how to compile TypeScript code into JavaScript.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Settings include the target JavaScript version, module type, strict type checking, path aliases, and
            other compiler options.</li>
          <li>In Angular projects, it helps maintain type safety and project structure.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Tells the TypeScript compiler how to compile the project.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="main-ts" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Entry point</span>
        <h3>What is main.ts?</h3>
        <p>main.ts is the entry point of an Angular application — the first file that executes, responsible for
          bootstrapping (starting) the app. In modern Angular, it uses <code>bootstrapApplication()</code> to start
          the root component and configure application providers.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Starting Angular, and loading the root component.</li>
          <li>Registering HTTP services and routes.</li>
          <li>Configuring providers, and handling startup errors.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Without main.ts, no Angular application starts.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> The entry point that bootstraps the Angular application.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="app-module-ts" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Root module</span>
        <h3>What is app.module.ts?</h3>
        <p>app.module.ts is the root module of a traditional Angular application. It groups together the components,
          directives, pipes, services, and other modules the application needs.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Declarations</b> — registers components, directives, and pipes.</li>
          <li><b>Imports</b> — imports other Angular modules.</li>
          <li><b>Providers</b> — registers services.</li>
          <li><b>Bootstrap</b> — defines the first component Angular loads.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In newer Angular versions, <a href="#standalone-components">standalone components</a> can be used instead
          of AppModule.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> The root module grouping components, directives, pipes, and services.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="dependencies-vs-devdependencies" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">package.json</span>
        <h3>dependencies vs. devDependencies</h3>
        <p><b>dependencies</b> — packages required to run the application. Without these, the app can't run.</p>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Example:</b> in an ERP application, dependencies like Angular, RxJS, and Angular
          Router are needed to display pages, call APIs, and navigate between screens.</div>
        <p><b>devDependencies</b> — packages needed only during development, to write, build, and test the app.
          Users of the built application don't need these.</p>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Example:</b> dev dependencies like Angular CLI, TypeScript, ESLint, and Karma help
          write code, compile TypeScript, and run tests — but aren't needed once the app is built.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Packages the app needs to run, vs. packages needed only to develop it.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

<!-- ================= DATA BINDING ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">03</span>
        <h2>Data binding</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="data-binding" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Template feature</span>
        <h3>What is Data Binding?</h3>
        <p>Data binding connects component data (TypeScript) with the HTML template (view) in Angular. It is used to
          show data and keep the UI in sync with the component, and the component in sync with the UI.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>There are 4 types of data binding: Interpolation, Property Binding, Event Binding, and Two-way Binding.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Connects component data (TypeScript) with the HTML template.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="interpolation" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Component → HTML</span>
        <h3>Interpolation</h3>
        <p>Interpolation is used to display dynamic values inside HTML, using <code>{{ }}</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>&lt;p&gt;{{ name }}&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Displays a dynamic value inside HTML, using double curly braces.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="property-binding" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Component → HTML</span>
        <h3>Property Binding</h3>
        <p>Property Binding is used to bind an HTML element's property dynamically. It sends data from TypeScript to
          HTML, using <code>[property]="value"</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>&lt;img [src]="imageUrl"&gt;</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Sends data from TypeScript to an HTML element's property, using [property].</div>
      </article>

      <article id="event-binding" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">HTML → Component</span>
        <h3>Event Binding</h3>
        <p>Event Binding is used to respond to user actions like click, input, or submit. It sends data from HTML to
          TypeScript, using <code>(event)="method()"</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>&lt;button (click)="save()"&gt;Save&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Sends data from HTML to TypeScript when a user acts, using (event).</div>
      </article>

      <article id="two-way-binding" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Both directions</span>
        <h3>Two-way Data Binding</h3>
        <p>Two-way data binding keeps data synchronized between the UI and the component, in both directions. If the
          value changes in TypeScript, the UI updates automatically. If the user changes the value in the UI, the
          component's value updates too. We use <code>[(ngModel)]</code> for this.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Data flows both ways between HTML and TypeScript.</li>
          <li>Value changes in TypeScript → UI updates.</li>
          <li>User changes value in UI → TypeScript updates.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>&lt;input [(ngModel)]="name"&gt;</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Syncs data both ways between component and view, using [(ngModel)].</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= COMPONENT COMMUNICATION ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">04</span>
        <h2>Component communication</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="constructor" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">TypeScript</span>
        <h3>Constructor in Angular</h3>
        <p>The constructor is a TypeScript feature used to initialize the class and inject dependencies, like
          services. It runs when the component is created, before Angular's lifecycle hooks.</p>

        <p>constructor is a special method of a class that is automatically called
          when an object of that class is created. It is mainly used to initialize the object's data.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Initializes the class and injects dependencies; runs before lifecycle hooks.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ngoninit" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Lifecycle hook</span>
        <h3>ngOnInit() in Angular</h3>
        <p>ngOnInit() is a lifecycle hook that runs after Angular creates and initializes the component. It is mainly
          used for API calls and initialization logic.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Lifecycle hook that runs after the component is created — used for API calls and setup.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="share-data-unrelated" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Communication</span>
        <h3>How do you share data between unrelated components?</h3>
        <p>The best way is a shared service with an RxJS Observable or BehaviorSubject. The service stores the data,
          and multiple components subscribe to it.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A shared service with an Observable or BehaviorSubject that components subscribe to.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="pass-data" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Communication</span>
        <h3>How to pass data to components</h3>
        <p>There are a few common ways to pass data between components, depending on how they're related.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Parent → Child (@Input()):</b> define @Input() in the child, and bind the data from the parent using
            property binding. Angular updates the child automatically when the parent's data changes — internally
            handled through the <code>ngOnChanges</code> lifecycle hook.</li>
          <li><b>Child → Parent (@Output() + EventEmitter):</b> create an @Output() with an EventEmitter, emit a value
            using <code>.emit()</code>, and the parent listens using event binding.</li>
          <li><b>Shared Service + RxJS:</b> a service with a Subject or BehaviorSubject — one sibling sends data, the
            other subscribes. Works for real-time communication, even if components are far apart.</li>
          <li><b>Router parameters:</b> pass data during navigation.</li>
          <li><b>Global State (NgRx / Signals):</b> centralized state management, used in larger apps like ERPs and
            dashboards.</li>
          <li><b>Angular Signals (modern):</b> signals inside a service — cleaner than RxJS, and better performance.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> @Input()/@Output(), a shared service, router params, or global state (NgRx/Signals).</div>
      </article>

      <article id="smart-dumb-components" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pattern</span>
        <h3>Smart Components &amp; Dumb Components</h3>
        <p><b>Smart components</b> (container / parent components) handle logic, API calls, and state management —
          they control the application's data and business logic.</p>
        <p><b>Dumb components</b> (presentational / child components) only display data and emit events to the
          parent. They have no API calls or business logic, which makes them reusable and easy to test — they receive
          data through inputs, and send events through outputs.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Smart handles logic and API calls; dumb only displays data and emits events.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="state-management" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Architecture</span>
        <h3>State Management</h3>
        <p>State management is how we store, update, and share data across the application. Examples of state: user
          data, API responses, form data, or UI state like a loading flag or a modal being open.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>In small Angular apps, a service with BehaviorSubject or Signals is usually enough.</li>
          <li>In large apps, libraries like NgRx, NGXS, or Akita manage state in a predictable way.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> How data is stored, updated, and shared — services, Signals, or NgRx/NGXS/Akita.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= VIEW & CONTENT QUERIES ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">05</span>
        <h2>View &amp; content queries</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="viewchild" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Query</span>
        <h3>@ViewChild() in Angular</h3>
        <p>@ViewChild() is used to access a child component, DOM element, or directive inside the parent component.
          It becomes available in <code>ngAfterViewInit()</code>.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Call a child component's methods, or access its properties.</li>
          <li>Access DOM elements, like an input or a button.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Use cases:</b> focus an input field, trigger a child form's validation, expand or
          collapse a child component, scroll to an element, read a child's data.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Accesses a child component, DOM element, or directive from the parent.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="viewchildren" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Query</span>
        <h3>@ViewChildren() in Angular</h3>
        <p>@ViewChildren() is used to access multiple child components or DOM elements. It returns a
          <code>QueryList</code>.
        </p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Accesses multiple child components or elements, returned as a QueryList.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="contentchild" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Content projection</span>
        <h3>@ContentChild()</h3>
        <p>@ContentChild() is used to get a reference to content the parent projects into a component using
          <code>&lt;ng-content&gt;</code>.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>The parent puts something inside the child component.</li>
          <li>The child displays it with <code>&lt;ng-content&gt;</code>.</li>
          <li>The child uses @ContentChild to access and control that content.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>// Parent (usage)
&lt;app-card&gt;
  &lt;button #actionBtn&gt;Click Me&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/app-card&gt;</code></pre>
        <pre><code>// Child (Card)
&lt;div class="card"&gt;
  &lt;ng-content&gt;&lt;/ng-content&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
        <pre><code>import { Component, ContentChild, ElementRef, AfterContentInit } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-card',
  templateUrl: './card.component.html'
})
export class CardComponent implements AfterContentInit {
  @ContentChild('actionBtn') button!: ElementRef;

  ngAfterContentInit() {
    this.button.nativeElement.style.background = 'blue';
  }
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Common use:</b> reusable UI components with content projection, like cards, modals, or
          custom wrappers.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Accesses content a parent projected into the component via ng-content.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="templateref" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Dynamic views</span>
        <h3>TemplateRef</h3>
        <p>TemplateRef is a reference to an Angular template (<code>&lt;ng-template&gt;</code>). It acts as a
          blueprint, and is not rendered until Angular creates a view from it.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Used for dynamic view creation.</li>
          <li>Used internally by <code>*ngIf</code> and <code>*ngFor</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A reference to an &lt;ng-template&gt;, not rendered until a view is created from it.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="viewcontainerref" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Dynamic views</span>
        <h3>ViewContainerRef</h3>
        <p>ViewContainerRef is a place in the DOM where Angular can add or remove views dynamically. It works with
          TemplateRef to render templates at runtime, and is used internally by features like <code>*ngIf</code> and
          <code>*ngFor</code>.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Used to load UI at runtime, and show or hide content dynamically.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A DOM location where Angular can add or remove views dynamically.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="dynamic-component-loading" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Runtime rendering</span>
        <h3>Dynamic Component Loading</h3>
        <p>Dynamic component loading means creating and rendering a component at runtime, instead of defining it
          directly in the template — based on conditions or user actions.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Use cases: dynamic forms, modals / popups, role-based UI, dashboards.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>@ViewChild('container', { read: ViewContainerRef })
container!: ViewContainerRef;

loadComponent() {
  this.container.clear();
  this.container.createComponent(LoginComponent);
}</code></pre>
        <pre><code>&lt;ng-template #container&gt;&lt;/ng-template&gt;</code></pre>
        <p><code>ViewContainerRef.createComponent()</code> is the method behind this. In real projects, it's a common
          way to build a reusable modal system or dynamic dashboard widgets — instead of hardcoding components in the
          HTML, different components load dynamically based on user action or an API response.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Creates and renders a component at runtime, e.g. for modals or dashboards.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-container" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Template</span>
        <h3>ng-container</h3>
        <p><code>&lt;ng-container&gt;</code> is used to group elements without creating an extra HTML element in the
          DOM. It's useful when you want a structural directive like <code>*ngIf</code> or <code>*ngFor</code>
          without adding an unnecessary <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>&lt;ng-container *ngIf="isLoggedIn"&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;Welcome&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/ng-container&gt;</code></pre>
        <p>No extra <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> is created in the rendered DOM.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Groups elements with a structural directive, without adding an extra DOM element.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= LIFECYCLE HOOKS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="chapter-divider">
        <span class="chapter-label">Part B · Lifecycle &amp; change detection</span>
        <div class="chapter-title">Lifecycle &amp; change detection</div>
        <p class="chapter-dek">How a component is born, updated, and torn down.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">06</span>
        <h2>Lifecycle hooks</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="lifecycle-overview" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Overview</span>
        <h3>Angular Lifecycle Hooks</h3>
        <p>Lifecycle hooks are special methods that Angular calls automatically at different stages of a component
          or directive's life, from creation to destruction.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#constructor">constructor()</a> — the class is created, dependencies are injected.</li>
          <li><a href="#ngonchanges">ngOnChanges()</a></li>
          <li><a href="#ngoninit">ngOnInit()</a></li>
          <li><a href="#ngdocheck">ngDoCheck()</a></li>
          <li><a href="#ngaftercontentinit">ngAfterContentInit()</a></li>
          <li><a href="#ngaftercontentchecked">ngAfterContentChecked()</a></li>
          <li><a href="#ngafterviewinit">ngAfterViewInit()</a></li>
          <li><a href="#ngafterviewchecked">ngAfterViewChecked()</a></li>
          <li><a href="#ngondestroy">ngOnDestroy()</a></li>
        </ol>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> The order Angular calls its hooks in, from constructor to ngOnDestroy.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ngonchanges" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Lifecycle hook</span>
        <h3>ngOnChanges()</h3>
        <p>ngOnChanges() runs whenever an <code>@Input()</code> property's value changes. It works only with
          <code>@Input()</code> properties, and runs before <a href="#ngoninit">ngOnInit()</a> on the very first
          change.
        </p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Runs whenever an @Input() property's value changes.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ngdocheck" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Lifecycle hook</span>
        <h3>ngDoCheck()</h3>
        <p>ngDoCheck() runs on every Angular change detection cycle — even when Angular doesn't detect a change
          automatically. It's used for custom, manual change detection, mainly to catch changes Angular can't track
          on its own.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Runs on every change detection cycle — for custom, manual change checks.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ngaftercontentinit" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Lifecycle hook</span>
        <h3>ngAfterContentInit()</h3>
        <p>ngAfterContentInit() runs once, after Angular projects external content into a component using
          <code>&lt;ng-content&gt;</code>. It's mainly used with <a href="#contentchild">@ContentChild</a> to access
          that projected content.
        </p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Runs once after content is projected in via ng-content.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ngaftercontentchecked" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Lifecycle hook</span>
        <h3>ngAfterContentChecked()</h3>
        <p>ngAfterContentChecked() runs after every change detection cycle for the projected content
          (<code>ng-content</code>). Used for debugging, or tracking changes in projected content.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Runs after every check of the projected content.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ngafterviewinit" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Lifecycle hook</span>
        <h3>ngAfterViewInit()</h3>
        <p>ngAfterViewInit() runs once, after the component's view and child views are fully initialized. It's
          mainly used to access DOM elements and <a href="#viewchild">@ViewChild</a> /
          <a href="#viewchildren">@ViewChildren</a> references, and to perform DOM-related operations.
        </p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Runs once the component's view and child views are initialized.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ngafterviewchecked" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Lifecycle hook</span>
        <h3>ngAfterViewChecked()</h3>
        <p>ngAfterViewChecked() runs after every change detection cycle for the component's view and child views.
          Used for tracking view updates or debugging — carefully, since it runs very often and can affect
          performance.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Runs after every check of the component's view and child views.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ngondestroy" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Lifecycle hook</span>
        <h3>ngOnDestroy()</h3>
        <p>ngOnDestroy() runs just before a component is destroyed. It's mainly used for cleanup — unsubscribing
          from Observables, removing event listeners, and clearing <code>setInterval</code> / <code>setTimeout</code>
          — to prevent memory leaks. See <a href="#memory-leaks">Avoiding memory leaks</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Runs just before the component is destroyed — the place to clean up.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= CHANGE DETECTION & ZONE.JS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">07</span>
        <h2>Change detection &amp; Zone.js</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="change-detection" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Core concept</span>
        <h3>What is Change Detection in Angular?</h3>
        <p>Change detection is the process by which Angular checks component data and updates the DOM when something
          has changed. It keeps the UI in sync with the data — when data changes, Angular updates the view.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> How Angular checks component data and updates the DOM when it changes.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="default-vs-onpush" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Strategies</span>
        <h3>Default vs. OnPush change detection</h3>
        <p>Angular provides two change detection strategies.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Default</b> — checks the entire component tree on every change detection cycle, even if nothing
            changed. This can cause performance issues in large apps.</li>
          <li><b>OnPush</b> — tells Angular "don't check this component on every cycle — check it only when its
            inputs actually change, an event occurs, or an Observable emits." OnPush works on reference change, not
            value mutation.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Default checks the whole tree every cycle; OnPush checks only on input reference change.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="why-onpush-faster" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Performance</span>
        <h3>Why is OnPush faster?</h3>
        <p>Because it reduces unnecessary checks by relying on immutable data and input reference changes, instead of
          checking the whole component tree every cycle.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> It skips unnecessary checks by relying on input reference changes, not value mutation.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="zonejs" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Under the hood</span>
        <h3>What is zone.js?</h3>
        <p>Zone.js is a library that helps Angular automatically detect and update the view when something changes
          in the app — like when data is fetched, a timer finishes, or a user clicks a button.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Tracks asynchronous operations like HTTP calls and <code>setTimeout</code>.</li>
          <li>Lets Angular know when these tasks are done, so it can run change detection and update the view.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Basically, zone.js saves developers from having to manually refresh the UI after an async action.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Tracks async operations so Angular knows when to run change detection.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ngzone" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Service</span>
        <h3>What is NgZone?</h3>
        <p>NgZone is an Angular service, built on Zone.js, that tracks asynchronous operations — API calls, events,
          timers — and automatically triggers change detection to update the UI.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Something async happens (API call, click, timeout) → NgZone tells Angular "data changed, update the
            UI."</li>
          <li>Also lets us run code outside Angular's zone, to improve performance.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Developers mostly use NgZone directly for performance optimization, working with third-party libraries,
          and avoiding unnecessary change detection.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> The Angular service built on Zone.js that triggers change detection for async work.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="run-outside-angular" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Performance</span>
        <h3>runOutsideAngular()</h3>
        <p>runOutsideAngular() runs code outside Angular's zone, so it does not trigger change detection or UI
          updates. Good for performance-heavy tasks that don't need to update the view.</p>
        <pre><code>this.zone.runOutsideAngular(() => {
  // heavy work that shouldn't trigger change detection
});</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Runs code without triggering Angular's change detection.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ngzone-run" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Performance</span>
        <h3>ngZone.run()</h3>
        <p>ngZone.run() executes code back inside Angular's zone, so change detection runs and the UI updates. Use it
          when code is running outside Angular and you need to update the UI or navigate.</p>
        <pre><code>this.zone.run(() => {
  // update UI or navigate here
});</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Brings code back inside Angular's zone so change detection runs again.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="zoneless" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Modern Angular</span>
        <h3>What is Zone-less Angular?</h3>
        <p>Zone-less Angular means running Angular without Zone.js — without automatic change detection. Instead of
          Angular updating the UI automatically, you decide when to update it.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Used for better performance, and to avoid unnecessary change detection.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Runs without Zone.js — change detection is triggered manually instead of automatically.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="detectchanges-vs-markforcheck" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">OnPush tools</span>
        <h3>detectChanges() vs. markForCheck()</h3>
        <p>Both are used with <code>ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush</code>, to update the UI manually.</p>
        <p><b>detectChanges()</b> immediately triggers change detection and updates the UI instantly. Use it when
          data comes from outside Angular — like <code>setTimeout</code>, a third-party library callback, or a manual
          DOM event — and you want an immediate UI update.</p>
        <pre><code>constructor(private cd: ChangeDetectorRef) {}

updateData() {
  this.name = 'Saurabh';
  this.cd.detectChanges();
}</code></pre>
        <p><b>markForCheck()</b> does not update immediately — it only tells Angular "check this component in the
          next change detection cycle." Use it in OnPush components when data changes asynchronously, and you want
          Angular to recheck efficiently without forcing an immediate update.</p>
        <pre><code>constructor(private cd: ChangeDetectorRef) {}

updateData() {
  this.name = 'Saurabh';
  this.cd.markForCheck();
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> detectChanges updates the view immediately; markForCheck queues it for the next cycle.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= TEMPLATES, RENDERING & COMPILATION ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="chapter-divider">
        <span class="chapter-label">Part C · Templates, directives, pipes &amp; forms</span>
        <div class="chapter-title">Templates, directives, pipes &amp; forms</div>
        <p class="chapter-dek">Everything that shapes what ends up in the DOM.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">08</span>
        <h2>Templates, rendering &amp; compilation</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="selector" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Component metadata</span>
        <h3>What is a Selector?</h3>
        <p>A selector is used to identify and load a component in HTML. It acts as a custom HTML tag through which
          Angular renders the component — the selector is the component's HTML tag name.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> The custom HTML tag name Angular uses to render a component.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="template-vs-templateurl" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Component metadata</span>
        <h3>template vs. templateUrl</h3>
        <p>Both define a component's HTML.</p>
        <p><b>template</b> — the HTML is written directly inside the component.</p>
        <pre><code>@Component({
  selector: 'app-home',
  template: `&lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;`
})</code></pre>
        <p><b>templateUrl</b> — the HTML is written in a separate file.</p>
        <pre><code>@Component({
  selector: 'app-home',
  templateUrl: './home.component.html'
})</code></pre>
        <p>In real projects, <code>templateUrl</code> is more common, because it's easier to maintain.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Inline HTML in the decorator, versus HTML loaded from a separate file.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="view-encapsulation" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Styling</span>
        <h3>What is View Encapsulation?</h3>
        <p>View Encapsulation controls how a component's CSS is applied — whether its styles stay inside it, or
          affect other components too. By default, Angular uses <b>Emulated</b> encapsulation, which keeps styles
          scoped to the component and prevents conflicts.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Emulated (default)</b> — Angular adds unique attributes to scope styles; styles stay inside the
            component, preventing conflicts.</li>
          <li><b>None</b> — no encapsulation; styles become global, and can affect other components.</li>
          <li><b>ShadowDom</b> — uses the browser's Shadow DOM for full, strong style isolation.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>@Component({
  selector: 'app-demo',
  templateUrl: './demo.html',
  styleUrls: ['./demo.css'],
  encapsulation: ViewEncapsulation.Emulated
})</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Controls whether a component's styles stay scoped to it or become global.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="defer" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Angular 17+</span>
        <h3>@defer in Angular</h3>
        <p>@defer is used to lazy-load parts of a template only when needed, instead of loading everything at once —
          reducing initial bundle size and improving performance.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>on viewport</code> — load when the block becomes visible.</li>
          <li><code>on interaction</code> — load on click / hover.</li>
          <li><code>on timer</code> — load after a set time.</li>
          <li><code>on immediate</code> — load right away, but defer execution.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>@defer (on viewport) {
  &lt;app-chart /&gt;
} @placeholder {
  &lt;p&gt;Loading...&lt;/p&gt;
} @loading {
  &lt;p&gt;Please wait...&lt;/p&gt;
} @error {
  &lt;p&gt;Error loading component&lt;/p&gt;
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Example:</b> a homepage with a navbar, dashboard, charts, and a heavy analytics
          component. Without deferred loading, everything loads immediately and the page becomes slow. With it, the
          navbar and dashboard load first, and the heavy parts load later — on scroll, after a delay, or on a button
          click.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Lazy-loads part of a template only when a trigger (viewport, interaction, timer) fires.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="forwardref" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Advanced DI</span>
        <h3>What is forwardRef?</h3>
        <p>forwardRef tells Angular to resolve a dependency later, once the referenced class is fully available.
          It's mostly used when building custom form controls with <code>ControlValueAccessor</code>.</p>
        <p>Imagine Angular reading your code top to bottom: it sees <code>useExisting: MyInputComponent</code>, but
          <code>MyInputComponent</code> isn't fully created yet. <code>forwardRef(() => MyInputComponent)</code> tells
          Angular: don't look for it now — look for it later, once everything is created.
        </p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Tells Angular to resolve a class reference later, once it exists.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="jit-aot" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Compilation</span>
        <h3>JIT vs. AOT compilation</h3>
        <p>Angular code (HTML + TypeScript) has to be converted into JavaScript the browser understands — this
          conversion is called compilation.</p>
        <p><b>JIT (Just-In-Time)</b> — code is compiled in the browser, at runtime. Angular uses JIT by default in
          development.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Good for: development, debugging, testing.</li>
          <li>Downsides: slower app load, larger bundle size, less secure (the template is exposed).</li>
        </ul>
        <p><b>AOT (Ahead-Of-Time)</b> — code is compiled during the build, before the app runs. The browser gets
          ready-to-run JavaScript, with no compilation in the browser.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Good for: production builds, performance-sensitive and large apps.</li>
          <li>Benefits: faster load, better performance, smaller bundle.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Angular needs AOT because it removes runtime compilation, improves performance, catches errors at build
          time, and makes the app more secure.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> JIT compiles templates in the browser; AOT compiles them ahead of time, at build.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ivy" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Compiler</span>
        <h3>What is Ivy?</h3>
        <p>Ivy is Angular's modern compiler and rendering engine — faster, smaller, and a better Angular engine. It
          converts Angular code into efficient JavaScript instructions to render the UI.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Makes Angular apps faster, and reduces bundle size.</li>
          <li>Improves debugging and build performance, without changing how developers write Angular code.</li>
          <li>Enabled by default since Angular 12.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Angular's modern compiler and rendering engine — faster, smaller, easier to debug.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="tree-shaking" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Build optimization</span>
        <h3>What is Tree Shaking?</h3>
        <p>Tree shaking is a build optimization that automatically removes unused code from the final bundle —
          Angular only keeps the code you actually use.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Reduces bundle size, and improves Angular app performance.</li>
          <li>Makes applications smaller and faster to load.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Removes unused code from the build to shrink the bundle.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= PIPES & DIRECTIVES ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">09</span>
        <h2>Pipes &amp; directives</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="pipe" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Template feature</span>
        <h3>What is a Pipe?</h3>
        <p>A Pipe transforms or formats data before showing it in the UI. Pipes are used only in templates, and they
          change how the data is displayed — not the original value.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Built-in examples: <code>date</code>, <code>currency</code>, <code>uppercase</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Transforms data for display in a template, without changing the original value.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="pure-impure-pipe" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Template feature</span>
        <h3>Pure Pipe vs. Impure Pipe</h3>
        <p>A <b>pure pipe</b> runs only when its input value changes, so it gives better performance — most built-in
          pipes (<code>date</code>, <code>currency</code>, <code>uppercase</code>) are pure by default.</p>
        <p>An <b>impure pipe</b> runs on every change detection cycle, even if the input value didn't change. Custom
          pipes can be marked impure, and should be used carefully since they can affect performance.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Pure runs only when input changes; impure runs on every change detection cycle.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="pipe-before-subscribe" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">RxJS</span>
        <h3>Why use pipe() before subscribe()?</h3>
        <p>pipe() lets us apply RxJS operators — like <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>,
          <code>debounceTime</code>, or <code>distinctUntilChanged</code> — to process the observable's data before
          it reaches <code>subscribe()</code>.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Filter data, delay data, transform data, avoid duplicate API calls, and handle errors — all before
            subscribe().</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Applies RxJS operators to process data before it reaches subscribe().</div>
      </article>

      <article id="directives" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Template feature</span>
        <h3>What are Directives in Angular?</h3>
        <p>Directives change the behavior or appearance of DOM elements. Angular provides built-in directives like
          <code>ngIf</code>, <code>ngFor</code>, and <code>ngClass</code>.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Component directive</b> — every Angular component is a directive (<code>@Component()</code>).</li>
          <li><b>Structural directive</b> — changes the DOM structure, like <code>*ngIf</code> and
            <code>*ngFor</code>.
          </li>
          <li><b>Attribute directive</b> — changes an element's style or behavior, like <code>ngClass</code> and
            <code>ngStyle</code>.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Change a DOM element's behavior or appearance — component, structural, or attribute.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="structural-directives" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Directive</span>
        <h3>Structural Directives</h3>
        <p>Structural directives change the structure of the DOM — adding or removing elements — like
          <code>*ngIf</code> and <code>*ngFor</code>.
        </p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Add or remove elements from the DOM, like *ngIf and *ngFor.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="attribute-directives" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Directive</span>
        <h3>Attribute Directives</h3>
        <p>Attribute directives change the appearance or behavior of an existing element, without adding or removing
          it — like <code>ngClass</code>, <code>ngStyle</code>, and <code>ngModel</code>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Change an existing element's style or behavior, like ngClass and ngStyle.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="custom-directive" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Example</span>
        <h3>Custom Directive example</h3>
        <p>A custom directive can add its own behavior to an element — for example, a highlight directive that turns
          the background yellow when the mouse hovers over it.</p>
        <pre><code>import { Directive, ElementRef, HostListener } from '@angular/core';

@Directive({
  selector: '[appHighlight]'
})
export class HighlightDirective {
  constructor(private el: ElementRef) {}

  @HostListener('mouseenter')
  onMouseEnter() {
    this.el.nativeElement.style.backgroundColor = 'yellow';
  }

  @HostListener('mouseleave')
  onMouseLeave() {
    this.el.nativeElement.style.backgroundColor = '';
  }
}</code></pre>
        <pre><code>&lt;p appHighlight&gt;Move mouse here&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A hand-written directive, e.g. highlighting an element on hover.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= FORMS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">10</span>
        <h2>Forms</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="reactive-forms" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Forms</span>
        <h3>Reactive Forms vs. Template-driven Forms</h3>
        <p><b>Reactive forms</b> build form inputs using TypeScript code — <code>FormGroup</code>,
          <code>FormControl</code>, and <code>Validators</code> — giving better control and validation for complex,
          dynamic forms.
        </p>
        <p><b>Template-driven forms</b> write the logic in HTML — Angular creates the form model, and validation
          lives in the template. Suitable for simple forms.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Logic in TypeScript with FormGroup, vs. logic written in the HTML template.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="formgroup" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Reactive Forms</span>
        <h3>FormGroup</h3>
        <p>FormGroup groups multiple form controls together, and represents the form as one object. Use it for a
          fixed set of fields — like a login or registration form.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Groups a fixed set of form controls together as one object.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="formarray" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Reactive Forms</span>
        <h3>FormArray</h3>
        <p>FormArray manages a dynamic list of controls as an array — for fields that repeat, like multiple skills,
          multiple addresses, or dynamic table rows.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Manages a dynamic list of form controls as an array.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="dynamic-forms" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Reactive Forms</span>
        <h3>Dynamic Forms</h3>
        <p>Dynamic forms are generated at runtime from a configuration object, JSON, or API data, instead of
          hardcoding fields in the HTML. FormControls are created dynamically, and the template loops over them to
          render the inputs.</p>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Use case:</b> in enterprise apps like an ERP, this is more scalable when different
          forms have different fields, or the structure can change without touching the UI code.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Builds form fields at runtime from a config object or API data.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= FORMS DEEP DIVE ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">11</span>
        <h2>Forms deep dive</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="custom-validators" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Reactive Forms</span>
        <h3>Custom Validators</h3>
        <p>A custom validator is a function that checks a form control's value and returns an error object if it's
          invalid, or <code>null</code> if it's valid. Use one when the built-in validators — <code>required</code>,
          <code>minLength</code>, <code>pattern</code> — aren't enough.</p>
        <pre><code>export function noSpecialChars(control: AbstractControl): ValidationErrors | null {
  const valid = /^[a-zA-Z0-9]*$/.test(control.value);
  return valid ? null : { specialChars: true };
}</code></pre>
        <pre><code>name = new FormControl('', [Validators.required, noSpecialChars]);</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A function that checks a control's value and returns an error object, or null.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="async-validators" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Reactive Forms</span>
        <h3>Async Validators</h3>
        <p>An async validator runs an asynchronous check — like calling an API to see if a username is already taken
          — and returns an Observable or Promise of the validation result, instead of a plain object.</p>
        <pre><code>export function uniqueUsernameValidator(userService: UserService): AsyncValidatorFn {
  return (control: AbstractControl) =>
    userService.checkUsername(control.value).pipe(
      map(isTaken => (isTaken ? { usernameTaken: true } : null))
    );
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Common use:</b> checking whether an email or username already exists, before letting
          the user submit.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A validator that checks a value against an API, returning an Observable/Promise.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="cross-field-validation" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Reactive Forms</span>
        <h3>Cross-field validation</h3>
        <p>Cross-field validation compares two or more fields together — like confirming that <code>password</code>
          and <code>confirmPassword</code> match. It's added at the FormGroup level, not on a single control.</p>
        <pre><code>export function passwordsMatch(group: AbstractControl): ValidationErrors | null {
  const pass = group.get('password')?.value;
  const confirm = group.get('confirmPassword')?.value;
  return pass === confirm ? null : { passwordsMismatch: true };
}</code></pre>
        <pre><code>this.form = new FormGroup({
  password: new FormControl(''),
  confirmPassword: new FormControl('')
}, { validators: passwordsMatch });</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Compares multiple fields together, like matching password fields.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="formbuilder" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Reactive Forms</span>
        <h3>FormBuilder</h3>
        <p>FormBuilder is a helper service that cuts down the boilerplate of creating FormGroups, FormControls, and
          FormArrays by hand.</p>
        <pre><code>constructor(private fb: FormBuilder) {}

form = this.fb.group({
  name: ['', Validators.required],
  email: ['', [Validators.required, Validators.email]]
});</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A helper service that reduces the boilerplate of building reactive forms.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="control-value-accessor" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Custom form controls</span>
        <h3>ControlValueAccessor</h3>
        <p>ControlValueAccessor is the interface Angular uses to let a custom component work with
          <code>[(ngModel)]</code> or <code>formControlName</code>, just like a native input. Implementing it lets a
          component — like a star rating or a custom date picker — plug directly into Reactive or Template-driven
          forms.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>writeValue()</code> — Angular calls this to set the value from outside.</li>
          <li><code>registerOnChange()</code> / <code>registerOnTouched()</code> — register the callbacks Angular
            uses to know when the value or touched state changes.</li>
          <li><code>setDisabledState()</code> — called when the form disables or enables the control.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>It's commonly paired with <a href="#forwardref">forwardRef()</a>, since Angular needs to reference the
          component's own class before it's fully defined.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Lets a custom component work like a native input inside Angular forms.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

<!-- ================= ROUTING & NAVIGATION ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="chapter-divider">
        <span class="chapter-label">Part D · Routing &amp; dependency injection</span>
        <div class="chapter-title">Routing &amp; dependency injection</div>
        <p class="chapter-dek">Navigating between views, and wiring services together.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">12</span>
        <h2>Routing &amp; navigation</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="angular-routing" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Navigation</span>
        <h3>What is Angular Routing?</h3>
        <p>Angular routing allows navigation between different components in a single page application. Each route
          maps a URL path to a specific component.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Supports lazy loading, guards, and parameters.</li>
          <li>Navigation can be done using <code>routerLink</code>, or the Router service.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Maps URL paths to components, for navigation inside a single page app.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="lazy-loading" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Performance</span>
        <h3>Lazy Loading, Eager Loading &amp; Preloading</h3>
        <p><b>Lazy loading</b> means loading a feature module only when the user navigates to that route, instead of
          loading the entire application at once. It reduces the initial bundle size, and improves the first load
          time.</p>
        <p><b>Eager loading</b> loads everything at startup. It's simpler, but leads to a slower initial load — fine
          for small apps.</p>
        <p><b>Preloading strategies</b> load lazy modules in the background, after the app has already started, so
          there's no delay the next time the user navigates there.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Load a module on demand, at startup, or in the background after startup.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="route-guards" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Navigation</span>
        <h3>Route Guards in Angular</h3>
        <p>Route guards are functions that control whether navigation to — or away from — a route is allowed.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>CanActivate</b> — allows or blocks navigation to a route.</li>
          <li><b>CanActivateChild</b> — protects child routes.</li>
          <li><b>CanMatch</b> — decides whether a route should match, before Angular loads it.</li>
          <li><b>CanDeactivate</b> — asks for confirmation before leaving a form or page.</li>
          <li><b>Resolve</b> — pre-fetches data before the route loads.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> CanActivate, CanActivateChild, CanMatch, CanDeactivate, and Resolve.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="authguard" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Guard</span>
        <h3>AuthGuard</h3>
        <p>AuthGuard is a route guard that checks whether a user is allowed to access a route. It protects private
          pages, prevents unauthorized access, and improves security.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Checks whether a user is allowed to access a route.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="resolver" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Guard</span>
        <h3>What is a Resolver in Angular?</h3>
        <p>A resolver is a route guard that preloads the data a route needs, before navigating to it. Angular waits
          for the data first, then loads the component — instead of showing an empty screen or a loading state.</p>
        <p><b>Step 1: create the resolver</b></p>
        <pre><code>import { ResolveFn } from '@angular/router';
import { inject } from '@angular/core';
import { EmployeeService } from './employee.service';

export const employeeResolver: ResolveFn&lt;any&gt; = (route) => {
  const service = inject(EmployeeService);
  return service.getEmployee(route.paramMap.get('id')!);
};</code></pre>
        <p><b>Step 2: add it to the route</b></p>
        <pre><code>{
  path: 'employee/:id',
  component: EmployeeComponent,
  resolve: {
    employee: employeeResolver
  }
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Preloads a route's data before the component opens.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="canmatch" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Guard</span>
        <h3>What is CanMatch?</h3>
        <p>CanMatch decides whether a route should even match, before Angular loads it. It's commonly used with
          lazy-loaded routes for role-based access, and is the recommended replacement for the older CanLoad.</p>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Example:</b> in an ERP application, only Admin users should access the /admin route.
          CanMatch checks the user's role first — if they're an Admin, Angular loads the route; otherwise it redirects
          to another page, like /login.</div>
        <pre><code>// AdminGuard.ts
import { CanMatchFn, Router } from '@angular/router';
import { inject } from '@angular/core';
import { AuthService } from './auth.service';

export const adminGuard: CanMatchFn = () => {
  const authService = inject(AuthService);
  const router = inject(Router);

  if (authService.isAdmin()) {
    return true;
  }
  return router.createUrlTree(['/login']);
};</code></pre>
        <pre><code>// route.ts
{
  path: 'admin',
  loadChildren: () =>
    import('./admin/admin.routes').then(m => m.routes),
  canMatch: [adminGuard]
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Decides if a route should match at all, before Angular loads it.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="candeactivate" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Guard</span>
        <h3>CanDeactivate</h3>
        <p>CanDeactivate controls whether a user can leave the current page — commonly used to warn about unsaved
          changes.</p>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Example:</b> in an ERP application, a user edits a form and tries to navigate away
          without saving. CanDeactivate shows a confirmation dialog — "Are you sure you want to leave?" If the user
          confirms, Angular navigates away; otherwise, it keeps them on the current page.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Confirms before letting a user leave a page, e.g. with unsaved changes.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="canactivatechild" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Guard</span>
        <h3>CanActivateChild</h3>
        <p>CanActivateChild protects every child route of a parent route at once, instead of adding CanActivate to
          each child route individually.</p>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Example:</b> in an ERP application, all pages under /admin — /admin/users,
          /admin/reports, /admin/settings — can be protected with a single CanActivateChild, so only authorized users
          can access any of them.</div>
        <pre><code>// admin-route.ts
{
  path: 'admin',
  canActivateChild: [adminGuard],
  children: [
    { path: 'dashboard', component: DashboardComponent },
    { path: 'users', component: UsersComponent },
    { path: 'reports', component: ReportsComponent },
    { path: 'settings', component: SettingsComponent }
  ]
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Protects every child route of a parent route at once.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="query-params" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Navigation</span>
        <h3>Query Parameters</h3>
        <p>Query parameters are values passed in the URL, commonly used for pagination, filters, search, sorting, or
          tab state — e.g. <code>/users?page=1&amp;role=admin</code>.</p>
        <p><b>Setting query params</b></p>
        <pre><code>this.router.navigate(
  ['/users'],
  {
    queryParams: {
      page: 1,
      role: 'admin'
    }
  }
);</code></pre>
        <p><b>Getting query params</b></p>
        <pre><code>this.route.queryParams.subscribe(params => {
  console.log(params['page']);
  console.log(params['role']);
});</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Values passed in the URL, e.g. for pagination or filters.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="role-based-access" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Security</span>
        <h3>Role-based access in Angular</h3>
        <p>Role-based access is usually implemented with route guards and an authentication service. After login,
          the user's role is stored — in a token, local storage, or app state — and a guard checks that role before
          allowing access to a route.</p>
        <p>Flow: login → get the user's role from the API/token → store the role → the guard checks the role →
          allow or block the route.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A guard checks the stored user role before allowing a route.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="wildcard-route" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Routing</span>
        <h3>Wildcard Route (**)</h3>
        <p>A wildcard route (<code>path: '**'</code>) catches any URL that doesn't match a defined route — typically
          used to show a 404 / Page Not Found component, or to redirect somewhere.</p>
        <pre><code>{ path: '**', component: PageNotFoundComponent }</code></pre>
        <p>It must be placed last in the route configuration, since Angular checks routes top to bottom — if
          <code>**</code> came first, it would match every URL, and the routes after it would never be reached.
        </p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> path: '**' catches any unmatched URL, usually for a 404 page.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="default-route" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Routing</span>
        <h3>Default Route</h3>
        <p>A default route defines which page opens when the user visits the app at an empty path
          (<code>''</code>). <code>pathMatch: 'full'</code> makes sure the redirect only happens when the URL is
          completely empty.</p>
        <pre><code>const routes: Routes = [
  { path: '', redirectTo: 'dashboard', pathMatch: 'full' },
  { path: 'dashboard', component: DashboardComponent },
  { path: '**', component: PageNotFoundComponent }
];</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Redirects an empty path ('') to a starting page, like a dashboard.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="route-data" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Routing</span>
        <h3>Storing data in routes</h3>
        <p>Angular routes have a <code>data</code> property, where we can store static data — like a page title, a
          role, or breadcrumbs.</p>
        <pre><code>const routes: Routes = [
  {
    path: 'dashboard',
    component: DashboardComponent,
    data: {
      title: 'Dashboard',
      role: 'Admin'
    }
  }
];</code></pre>
        <pre><code>// Access it in the component
this.route.snapshot.data['title'];</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A route's data property stores static values like a title or role.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= SERVICE & DI ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">13</span>
        <h2>Service &amp; Dependency Injection</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="dependency-injection" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Design pattern</span>
        <h3>What is Dependency Injection?</h3>
        <p>Dependency Injection (DI) is a design pattern used in Angular to provide services to components. Instead of
          creating objects manually, Angular automatically creates and injects the required service.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The component does not create the service itself — Angular creates it and injects it.</li>
          <li>Helps code reuse, and makes applications easier to test and maintain.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Real-world example:</b> Service = the pizza (the business logic). Dependency
          Injection = Swiggy or Zomato — the system that delivers the pizza to you. You don't cook the pizza yourself,
          so you don't create services yourself — Angular delivers it for you.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Angular creates and provides services to components, instead of components creating them.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="injectable" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Decorator</span>
        <h3>@Injectable()</h3>
        <p>@Injectable() is a decorator that marks a class as available for Angular's Dependency Injection system. It
          lets Angular create the service, inject its own dependencies — such as <code>HttpClient</code> or
          <code>Router</code> — and manage its lifecycle.
        </p>
        <pre><code>@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root'
})
export class UserService {}</code></pre>
        <p>With <code>providedIn: 'root'</code>, Angular registers the service in the root injector, creates one
          shared (singleton) instance for the whole application, and makes it tree-shakable — if the service is never
          used, Angular removes it from the production bundle.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Marks a class as available for Angular's Dependency Injection system.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="service-without-injectable" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Edge case</span>
        <h3>Can a service exist without @Injectable()?</h3>
        <p>Yes — but only if it has no constructor dependencies, and you don't expect Angular to inject anything into
          it.</p>
      </article>

      <article id="angular-service" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Building block</span>
        <h3>What is a Service in Angular?</h3>
        <p>A Service in Angular is a reusable TypeScript class that contains business logic, data handling, API calls,
          or other shared functionality. It is injected into components using Dependency Injection.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Helps avoid duplicate code.</li>
          <li>Keeps components focused on the UI.</li>
          <li>Improves maintainability and reusability.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A reusable class holding business logic, API calls, or shared functionality.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="providers" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">DI configuration</span>
        <h3>What are Providers in Angular?</h3>
        <p>A provider tells Angular how to create and supply a service. When a component needs a service, the provider
          creates the instance and injects it into the component.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Can be configured at the root, module, or component level.</li>
          <li>Support different strategies: <code>useClass</code>, <code>useValue</code>, and <code>useFactory</code>.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Tell Angular how to create and supply a service.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="singleton-service" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">One shared instance</span>
        <h3>What is a Singleton Service in Angular?</h3>
        <p>A singleton service means only one instance of the service is created and shared across the entire
          application — one instance used everywhere in the app.</p>
        <p>This is usually achieved with <code>providedIn: 'root'</code>, which registers the service in the root
          injector so it can be shared across all components.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> One shared instance of a service, used across the whole application.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="providedin-vs-providers" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Comparison</span>
        <h3>providedIn vs. providers</h3>
        <p>Both <code>providedIn</code> and <code>providers</code> are used for Dependency Injection in Angular. The
          main difference is where the service is registered, and how many instances get created.</p>
        <p><b>providedIn</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Added inside the service itself, using <code>@Injectable()</code>.</li>
          <li><code>providedIn: 'root'</code> creates one shared instance for the whole application (singleton).</li>
          <li>Supports tree-shaking — if the service is never used, Angular removes it from the final bundle.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root'
})
export class UserService {}</code></pre>
        <p><b>providers</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Added in a component, directive, route, or app configuration.</li>
          <li>Angular creates a new instance for that scope.</li>
          <li>Different components can have different instances of the same service.</li>
          <li>Not tree-shakable like <code>providedIn</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>@Component({
  selector: 'app-home',
  providers: [UserService]
})
export class HomeComponent {}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> providedIn gives one app-wide, tree-shakable instance; providers gives a new instance per scope.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="multi-provider-token" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Advanced DI</span>
        <h3>What is the Multi Provider Token?</h3>
        <p>Multi Provider Token means many services can be added under one token. Angular stores them in an array,
          instead of replacing the previous provider.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>multi: true</code> tells Angular to keep multiple providers together, instead of replacing the old
            one.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>providers: [
  {
    provide: HTTP_INTERCEPTORS,
    useClass: AuthInterceptor,
    multi: true
  },
]</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Lets several providers share one token instead of replacing each other.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= ADVANCED DI &amp; DOM APIS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">14</span>
        <h2>Advanced DI &amp; DOM APIs</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="di-decorators" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Dependency Injection</span>
        <h3>@Host(), @Self(), @SkipSelf(), @Optional()</h3>
        <p>These decorators fine-tune where Angular looks for a dependency in the injector tree.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>@Self()</b> — only look in this component's own injector, not any parent.</li>
          <li><b>@SkipSelf()</b> — skip this component's own injector, and look starting from the parent.</li>
          <li><b>@Host()</b> — stop searching at the host component, without going further up to the app root.</li>
          <li><b>@Optional()</b> — don't throw an error if the dependency isn't found; inject <code>null</code>
            instead.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>constructor(@Optional() @Self() private logger: LoggerService) {}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Fine-tune where Angular looks for a dependency in the injector tree.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hostbinding" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Directives</span>
        <h3>@HostBinding()</h3>
        <p>@HostBinding() binds a property of the host element — a class, style, or attribute — to a property on the
          directive or component class.</p>
        <pre><code>@HostBinding('class.active') isActive = false;
@HostBinding('style.color') textColor = 'blue';</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Binds a class, style, or attribute of the host element to a class property.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hostlistener" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Directives</span>
        <h3>@HostListener()</h3>
        <p>@HostListener() listens for an event on the host element — like <code>click</code>,
          <code>mouseenter</code>, or a window event — and runs a method when it fires. See the
          <a href="#custom-directive">custom directive example</a>.</p>
        <pre><code>@HostListener('window:resize', ['$event'])
onResize(event: Event) {
  console.log('window resized');
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Runs a method when an event fires on the host element.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="renderer2" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">DOM access</span>
        <h3>Renderer2 vs. ElementRef</h3>
        <p>ElementRef gives direct access to the native DOM element. Renderer2 is Angular's preferred way to change
          the DOM instead, because it works safely across platforms — browser, server-side rendering, web workers —
          and avoids touching the DOM directly.</p>
        <pre><code>constructor(private el: ElementRef, private renderer: Renderer2) {}

ngOnInit() {
  this.renderer.setStyle(this.el.nativeElement, 'color', 'red');
}</code></pre>
        <p>Direct DOM manipulation through <code>el.nativeElement.style...</code> works, but isn't safe outside the
          browser — e.g. under <a href="#ssr">SSR</a> — so Renderer2 is the recommended approach.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> The safe, cross-platform way to change the DOM, instead of touching it directly.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="exportas" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Directives</span>
        <h3>exportAs</h3>
        <p>exportAs gives a directive a name that can be referenced with a template reference variable, so a
          template can access the directive's public properties and methods directly.</p>
        <pre><code>@Directive({
  selector: '[appTooltip]',
  exportAs: 'appTooltip'
})
export class TooltipDirective {
  show() { /* ... */ }
}</code></pre>
        <pre><code>&lt;button appTooltip #t="appTooltip" (click)="t.show()"&gt;Hover me&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Names a directive so a template reference variable can access it directly.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

<!-- ================= HTTP, INTERCEPTORS & ERRORS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="chapter-divider">
        <span class="chapter-label">Part E · HTTP, performance, security &amp; testing</span>
        <div class="chapter-title">HTTP, performance, security &amp; testing</div>
        <p class="chapter-dek">Talking to a server, and keeping the app fast, safe, and tested.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">15</span>
        <h2>HTTP, interceptors &amp; errors</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="httpclient" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">HTTP</span>
        <h3>What is HttpClient?</h3>
        <p>HttpClient is an Angular service used to communicate with a backend server. It sends HTTP requests — GET,
          POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH — and returns the response as an RxJS Observable.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Angular's service for sending HTTP requests and getting an Observable back.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="call-api" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">HTTP</span>
        <h3>How to call an API in Angular</h3>
        <p>APIs are usually called inside a service, using HttpClient.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Provide <code>HttpClient</code> (or import <code>HttpClientModule</code>).</li>
          <li>Inject <code>HttpClient</code> into the service's constructor.</li>
          <li>Use methods like <code>get()</code>, <code>post()</code>, <code>put()</code>, or <code>delete()</code>
            — each returns an Observable.</li>
          <li>The component subscribes (or uses the <a href="#async-pipe">async pipe</a>) to get the data.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Inject HttpClient in a service, call get()/post()/etc., then subscribe in the component.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="interceptor" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">HTTP</span>
        <h3>What is an Interceptor?</h3>
        <p>An interceptor is a service that sits in the middle of every HTTP request and response, and can modify
          them globally — without changing every individual API call.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Adding a JWT token or headers automatically, instead of adding it to every request by hand.</li>
          <li>Global error handling — e.g. redirecting to login on a 401, in one place.</li>
          <li>Logging API calls.</li>
          <li>Caching a response, to avoid calling the same API repeatedly (e.g. shared master data).</li>
          <li>Showing or hiding a loading spinner.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Sits in the middle of every HTTP call, to add tokens, log, cache, or handle errors globally.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="api-error-handling" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Error handling</span>
        <h3>How to handle API errors in Angular</h3>
        <p>API errors are usually handled with the RxJS <a href="#catcherror">catchError</a> operator inside a
          service, and with HTTP interceptors for global cases like authentication errors or server issues. In the
          component, we display a user-friendly message based on the error.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> catchError in services, plus interceptors for global cases like auth failures.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="error-pages" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Error handling</span>
        <h3>Handling 404 / 500 error pages</h3>
        <p>For a <b>404</b> (page not found), Angular's <a href="#wildcard-route">wildcard route</a>
          (<code>**</code>) redirects to a Not Found page. For a <b>500</b> (server error) — an API failing, or the
          server crashing — an HTTP interceptor can catch it and navigate to a custom Server Error page.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A wildcard route for 404s; an interceptor to catch and route 500s.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= PERFORMANCE & MEMORY ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">16</span>
        <h2>Performance &amp; memory</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="memory-leaks" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Cleanup</span>
        <h3>How to avoid memory leaks in Angular</h3>
        <p>A memory leak happens when Angular removes a component from the screen, but subscriptions, timers, or
          listeners keep running in memory — RxJS subscriptions, <code>setInterval</code>/<code>setTimeout</code>,
          DOM event listeners, WebSocket connections. Over time, this increases memory use, can trigger duplicate API
          calls, and slows the app down.</p>
        <p><b>1. Unsubscribe in ngOnDestroy</b></p>
        <pre><code>subscription!: Subscription;

ngOnInit() {
  this.subscription = this.userService.getUsers().subscribe();
}

ngOnDestroy() {
  this.subscription.unsubscribe();
}</code></pre>
        <p><b>2. takeUntil</b> (a common enterprise pattern)</p>
        <pre><code>private destroy$ = new Subject&lt;void&gt;();

ngOnInit() {
  this.userService.getUsers()
    .pipe(takeUntil(this.destroy$))
    .subscribe();
}

ngOnDestroy() {
  this.destroy$.next();
  this.destroy$.complete();
}</code></pre>
        <p><b>3. takeUntilDestroyed()</b> (Angular 16+, recommended) — Angular automatically handles cleanup when the
          component is destroyed.</p>
        <pre><code>import { takeUntilDestroyed } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';
import { DestroyRef, inject } from '@angular/core';

destroyRef = inject(DestroyRef);

ngOnInit() {
  this.userService.getUsers()
    .pipe(takeUntilDestroyed(this.destroyRef))
    .subscribe();
}</code></pre>
        <p><b>4. Async pipe</b> (best when possible) — subscribes and unsubscribes automatically, no manual cleanup
          needed.</p>
        <pre><code>&lt;div&gt;{{ users$ | async | json }}&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
        <p><b>5. Angular Signals</b> — don't require manual subscriptions; Angular manages the lifecycle
          automatically.</p>
        <pre><code>users = toSignal(this.userService.getUsers());</code></pre>
        <p>In real projects, the <code>takeUntil</code> pattern is common wherever a component has multiple RxJS
          subscriptions — like API calls and form <code>valueChanges</code> — alongside the async pipe in templates,
          since Angular unsubscribes automatically there.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Clean up subscriptions and timers with unsubscribe(), takeUntil(), or the async pipe.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="check-performance" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Diagnostics</span>
        <h3>How to check Angular project performance</h3>
        <p>Performance is usually checked with browser developer tools, Angular DevTools, Lighthouse, and bundle
          analyzers — monitoring change detection cycles, bundle size, API calls, and rendering performance.</p>
        <p><b>Angular DevTools</b> (Chrome extension) shows component rendering time, change detection cycles, the
          component hierarchy, and performance bottlenecks — if one component re-renders 100 times unnecessarily, it
          shows up here.</p>
        <p><b>Chrome DevTools Performance tab:</b> press F12 → Performance → Record → use the app → stop recording.
          Check JavaScript execution time, rendering time, repaints, long tasks, and memory usage.</p>
        <p><b>Network tab:</b> F12 → Network. Check API response times, duplicate API calls, large payloads, and
          failed requests.</p>
        <p><b>Lighthouse audit:</b> F12 → Lighthouse → Analyze page load. Measures performance score, accessibility,
          best practices, and SEO.</p>
        <p><b>Bundle size:</b> build for production, then analyze the output.</p>
        <pre><code>ng build --configuration production
npm install -g source-map-explorer
source-map-explorer dist/*.js</code></pre>
        <p>This shows which libraries take up more space, large bundles, and unused code.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Angular DevTools, the Chrome Performance/Network tabs, Lighthouse, and bundle analyzers.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="optimize-performance" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Optimization</span>
        <h3>How to optimize Angular performance</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Use <a href="#default-vs-onpush">OnPush</a> change detection — checks only when input changes.</li>
          <li>Use <a href="#trackby">trackBy</a> in <code>*ngFor</code> — prevents re-rendering the full list.</li>
          <li>Use lazy loading — load modules or routes only when the user visits that page.</li>
          <li>Use the <a href="#async-pipe">async pipe</a> — Angular handles subscribe/unsubscribe automatically.</li>
          <li>Use pure pipes.</li>
          <li>Lazy-load images (<code>loading="lazy"</code>).</li>
          <li>Cache API responses to avoid unnecessary calls (see <a href="#sharereplay">shareReplay</a>).</li>
          <li>Split code into smaller, reusable pieces.</li>
          <li>Unsubscribe Observables with <a href="#takeuntil">takeUntil</a> or
            <a href="#takeuntildestroyed">takeUntilDestroyed</a>, and clear intervals.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> OnPush, trackBy, lazy loading, the async pipe, caching, and unsubscribing properly.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="trackby" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">*ngFor</span>
        <h3>trackBy in *ngFor</h3>
        <p>trackBy is a function used with <code>*ngFor</code> to improve performance. It tells Angular how to
          uniquely identify each item in a list, so when the list changes, Angular updates only the changed items
          instead of re-rendering the whole list.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Tells *ngFor how to identify items, so only changed ones re-render.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= SECURITY, ENVIRONMENTS &amp; TOOLING ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">17</span>
        <h2>Security, environments &amp; tooling</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="xss-sanitization" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Security</span>
        <h3>XSS protection &amp; DomSanitizer</h3>
        <p>Angular automatically sanitizes values bound into the DOM — HTML, styles, URLs, resource URLs — to
          protect against cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks. It strips out anything it considers unsafe before
          rendering.</p>
        <p>When we genuinely trust a value — like HTML from our own CMS — we can bypass sanitization for that one
          value using DomSanitizer.</p>
        <pre><code>constructor(private sanitizer: DomSanitizer) {}

trustedHtml = this.sanitizer.bypassSecurityTrustHtml(rawHtmlFromCms);</code></pre>
        <p>Use <code>bypassSecurityTrustHtml</code> / <code>Url</code> / <code>Style</code> / <code>Script</code>
          sparingly, and only for content you actually trust — it turns off Angular's protection for that value.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Angular auto-sanitizes bound values; DomSanitizer opts a trusted value out.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="environment-files" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Configuration</span>
        <h3>environment.ts files</h3>
        <p>Angular projects keep separate environment files — like <code>environment.ts</code> for development and
          <code>environment.prod.ts</code> for production — holding values that change between environments, such as
          an API base URL or feature flags.</p>
        <pre><code>// environment.ts
export const environment = {
  production: false,
  apiUrl: 'http://localhost:3000/api'
};</code></pre>
        <p><a href="#angular-json">angular.json</a>'s file replacements swap in the right file automatically for
          each build configuration, so the rest of the code just imports <code>environment</code> without caring
          which one it got.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Per-environment config, like the API URL, swapped in automatically at build time.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="angular-cli-commands" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Tooling</span>
        <h3>Common Angular CLI commands</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><code>ng generate component name</code> — scaffolds a new component (also <code>ng g c</code>).</li>
          <li><code>ng serve</code> — runs the app locally with live reload.</li>
          <li><code>ng build --configuration production</code> — builds an optimized production bundle.</li>
          <li><code>ng test</code> — runs unit tests.</li>
          <li><code>ng add &lt;package&gt;</code> — installs and configures a library, e.g. Angular Material.</li>
          <li><code>ng update</code> — upgrades Angular and its dependencies.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> ng generate, ng serve, ng build, ng test, ng add, ng update.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

<!-- ================= TESTING IN ANGULAR ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">18</span>
        <h2>Testing in Angular</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="testbed" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Unit testing</span>
        <h3>What is TestBed?</h3>
        <p>TestBed is Angular's testing utility that creates a mini Angular testing module — it lets us configure
          providers, imports, and declarations, then create components and inject services the same way Angular
          does at runtime.</p>
        <pre><code>TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  declarations: [UserCardComponent],
  providers: [UserService]
});</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Builds a mini Angular module for tests, so components/services work as in the app.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="test-component" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Unit testing</span>
        <h3>Testing a Component</h3>
        <p>A component test typically creates the component with TestBed, gets a ComponentFixture, and checks both
          the component instance and its rendered template.</p>
        <pre><code>let fixture: ComponentFixture&lt;UserCardComponent&gt;;
let component: UserCardComponent;

beforeEach(() => {
  fixture = TestBed.createComponent(UserCardComponent);
  component = fixture.componentInstance;
  fixture.detectChanges();
});

it('should display the user name', () => {
  component.user = { name: 'Saurabh' };
  fixture.detectChanges();
  const el: HTMLElement = fixture.nativeElement;
  expect(el.textContent).toContain('Saurabh');
});</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Create it with TestBed, then assert on the instance and the rendered template.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="test-service" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Unit testing</span>
        <h3>Testing a Service</h3>
        <p>Services are usually simpler to test — inject the service from TestBed, and call its methods directly,
          without needing a component or the DOM.</p>
        <pre><code>let service: CartService;

beforeEach(() => {
  TestBed.configureTestingModule({ providers: [CartService] });
  service = TestBed.inject(CartService);
});

it('should add an item', () => {
  service.addItem({ id: 1, name: 'Book' });
  expect(service.items.length).toBe(1);
});</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Inject it from TestBed and call its methods directly.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="httptestingcontroller" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Unit testing</span>
        <h3>HttpTestingController</h3>
        <p>HttpTestingController lets us test code that calls HttpClient, without hitting a real API. We trigger the
          call, then assert on and respond to the fake HTTP request ourselves.</p>
        <pre><code>TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  imports: [HttpClientTestingModule],
  providers: [UserService]
});
const httpMock = TestBed.inject(HttpTestingController);
const service = TestBed.inject(UserService);

it('should fetch users', () => {
  service.getUsers().subscribe(users => {
    expect(users.length).toBe(2);
  });

  const req = httpMock.expectOne('/api/users');
  req.flush([{ id: 1 }, { id: 2 }]);
});</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Tests HttpClient code against fake requests, without a real API.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="mocking-spies" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Unit testing</span>
        <h3>Mocking dependencies with spies</h3>
        <p>A spy replaces a real dependency's method with a fake one we control — so a component test doesn't need a
          real service, a real API call, or a real database.</p>
        <pre><code>const userServiceSpy = jasmine.createSpyObj('UserService', ['getUsers']);
userServiceSpy.getUsers.and.returnValue(of([{ id: 1, name: 'Ram' }]));

TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  providers: [{ provide: UserService, useValue: userServiceSpy }]
});</code></pre>
        <p>This keeps component tests fast and focused — they test the component's own logic, not the service's.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Replaces a real dependency's methods with fakes, to isolate what's under test.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

<!-- ================= SSR & ADVANCED DI ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">19</span>
        <h2>SSR &amp; advanced DI</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="ssr" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Rendering</span>
        <h3>SSR (Server-Side Rendering) in Angular</h3>
        <p>Normally, Angular renders the page in the browser. With SSR, Angular renders the page on the server
          first, and sends the ready HTML to the browser — so the user sees the page faster, and it's more
          SEO-friendly.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Without SSR:</b> open the site → the browser loads Angular → builds the page → shows the page.</li>
          <li><b>With SSR:</b> open the site → the server sends ready HTML → the user sees the page immediately →
            Angular then takes over in the browser (hydration).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>SSR in Angular is implemented using <b>Angular Universal</b>, which generates the HTML on the server
          before sending it to the browser.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Renders the page on the server first, for a faster, SEO-friendly first load.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hydration" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Rendering</span>
        <h3>Hydration</h3>
        <p>After SSR sends a ready-made HTML page, hydration is what makes that page "alive" — adding the
          JavaScript that makes buttons, forms, and events actually work.</p>
        <p>Without hydration, you can see the page, but can't interact with it — buttons don't work, forms can't
          submit. With hydration, everything works as normal.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Makes a server-rendered page interactive, by attaching Angular in the browser.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="injectiontoken" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Advanced DI</span>
        <h3>What is an InjectionToken?</h3>
        <p>InjectionToken is a unique key used to inject values that aren't classes — strings, objects, or
          configuration data — through Angular's Dependency Injection.</p>
<p>InjectionToken is used to create a unique DI token for values that don't have a class, such as strings, objects, or configuration. We can provide the value once and inject it wherever required.</p>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Example:</b> instead of hardcoding an API URL in every service, we create an
          <code>API_URL</code> InjectionToken, provide the URL once, and inject it wherever it's needed — making the
          app easy to reconfigure for different environments, like development, testing, and production.
        </div>
        <p><b>Step 1: create a token</b></p>
        <pre><code>// api.token.ts
import { InjectionToken } from '@angular/core';

export const API_URL = new InjectionToken&lt;string&gt;('API_URL');</code></pre>
        <p><b>Step 2: provide the value</b></p>
        <pre><code>// app.config.ts
import { ApplicationConfig } from '@angular/core';
import { API_URL } from './api.token';

export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [
    {
      provide: API_URL,
      useValue: 'https://api.company.com'
    }
  ]
};</code></pre>
        <p><b>Step 3: use it inside a service</b></p>
        <pre><code>// user.service.ts
import { Injectable, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { API_URL } from './api.token';

@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root'
})
export class UserService {
  private apiUrl = inject(API_URL);

  getUsers() {
    console.log(this.apiUrl);
  }
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A unique DI key for injecting non-class values, like a config string.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

  <!-- ================= FOUNDATIONS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="chapter-divider">
        <span class="chapter-label">Part F · RxJS &amp; reactive programming</span>
        <div class="chapter-title">RxJS &amp; reactive programming</div>
        <p class="chapter-dek">Handling data that arrives over time.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">20</span>
        <h2>Foundations</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="rxjs-intro" data-cat="core">
        <span class="eyebrow">Library</span>
        <h3>What is RxJS?</h3>
        <p>RxJS is a library used in Angular to handle asynchronous data. It is a reactive programming library based on
          Observables, and it helps us handle, transform, and control data that comes in over time.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Used for API calls, user input, events, and real-time data.</li>
          <li>Everything else in this handbook is either a type of Observable, or an operator that works on one.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article id="observable" data-cat="core">
        <span class="eyebrow">Foundation</span>
        <h3>Observable</h3>
        <p>Observable is a feature from RxJS, used to handle asynchronous data streams.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Used for API calls, user events, and real-time data.</li>
          <li>Can return multiple values over time — unlike a Promise, which resolves once.</li>
          <li>Can be cancelled mid-stream by calling <code>unsubscribe()</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>const numbers$ = new Observable&lt;number&gt;(observer => {
  observer.next(1);
  observer.next(2);
  observer.complete();
});</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Sends data over time. Nothing happens until something subscribes to it.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="observer" data-cat="core">
        <span class="eyebrow">Foundation</span>
        <h3>Observer</h3>
        <p>Observer = listener / receiver of data. It is a function or object that receives data from an Observable and
          reacts to it — the one that subscribes and handles the data, errors, and completion.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>next</code> — handles each emitted value.</li>
          <li><code>error</code> — handles a failure.</li>
          <li><code>complete</code> — runs once the stream finishes.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>observable.subscribe({
  next: (value) => console.log(value),
  error: (err) => console.log(err),
  complete: () => console.log('Done')
});</code></pre>
        <p>Everything inside that <code>subscribe()</code> call <em>is</em> the Observer.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> The next / error / complete functions you pass into subscribe().</div>
      </article>

      <article id="why-observables" data-cat="core">
        <span class="eyebrow">Design decision</span>
        <h3>Why does Angular use Observables instead of Promises?</h3>
        <p>Angular uses Observables because they support multiple values, cancellation, lazy execution, and powerful
          operators. These are important for handling HTTP calls, events, and real-time data.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Promises handle one-time async operations, and can't be cancelled.</li>
          <li>Observables handle continuous, cancellable data streams — that's why they suit Angular better.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article id="async-handling" data-cat="core">
        <span class="eyebrow">Practice</span>
        <h3>Handling asynchronous operations in Angular</h3>
        <p>In Angular, asynchronous operations are mainly handled using RxJS Observables together with
          <code>HttpClient</code>. We subscribe to Observables, or use the <a href="#async-pipe">async pipe</a> so
          subscribe and unsubscribe happen on their own.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>For simple cases, we can also use Promises with <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>.</li>
          <li>Observables are preferred because they support cancellation and multiple values.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= SUBJECTS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">21</span>
        <h2>Subjects</h2>
      </div>
      <p>A Subject is a special Observable that can both send data and receive data. The four types below all differ in
        how much old data they remember for a subscriber who joins late.</p>

      <article id="subject" data-cat="subject">
        <span class="eyebrow">Subject</span>
        <h3>Subject</h3>
        <p>Subject in RxJS is a special Observable that lets us both send data and receive data. It can have multiple
          subscribers, and it only sends values to the ones that are subscribed right now.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Does not store old values — only current subscribers get data.</li>
          <li>Used for event communication, like a button click, a refresh, or a modal opening or closing.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>private refresh$ = new Subject&lt;void&gt;();
refreshTriggered$ = this.refresh$.asObservable();

triggerRefresh() {
  this.refresh$.next();
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Sends data only to current subscribers. Does not store old values.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="behaviorsubject" data-cat="subject">
        <span class="eyebrow">Subject with memory</span>
        <h3>BehaviorSubject</h3>
        <p>BehaviorSubject stores the latest value and sends it immediately to new subscribers. It requires an initial
          value, which is what makes it ideal for state management — it always holds and emits the latest value.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Requires a starting value when it's created.</li>
          <li>Used for state management: logged-in user, theme, or cart item count.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>private userSubject = new BehaviorSubject&lt;User | null&gt;(null);
user$ = this.userSubject.asObservable();

setUser(user: User) {
  this.userSubject.next(user);
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Subject that stores the latest value and gives it to new subscribers right away.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="replaysubject" data-cat="subject">
        <span class="eyebrow">Subject with a buffer</span>
        <h3>ReplaySubject</h3>
        <p>ReplaySubject stores multiple previous values and sends them to new subscribers, not just the single latest
          one.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Stores multiple past values, not just one.</li>
          <li>New subscribers receive the previous values too.</li>
          <li>Useful for things like chat history or logs.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>private log$ = new ReplaySubject&lt;string&gt;(5); // keeps the last 5 entries

addLog(entry: string) {
  this.log$.next(entry);
}
// a component that subscribes later still receives the last 5 lines</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Subject that stores old values and replays them to new subscribers.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="asyncsubject" data-cat="subject">
        <span class="eyebrow">Final-value only</span>
        <h3>AsyncSubject</h3>
        <p>AsyncSubject emits only the last value, and only when the Observable completes. It stores the latest value
          and sends it to subscribers only after <code>complete()</code> is called.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Ignores every value in between — only the final one matters.</li>
          <li>Good for scenarios where we only need the final result after a process finishes.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>upload$ = new AsyncSubject&lt;string&gt;();

startUpload() {
  this.upload$.next('Uploading...');
  this.upload$.next('Almost Done...');
  this.upload$.next('Upload Completed');
  this.upload$.complete();
}
// subscribers only ever see: 'Upload Completed'</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Example:</b> suppose a file upload gives progress — 10%, 30%, 60%, 100% — but the UI
          only needs the final result. AsyncSubject is useful for exactly that.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Sends only the last value, and only after complete() is called.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= TRANSFORMATION ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">22</span>
        <h2>Transformation operators</h2>
      </div>
      <p>These four operators all switch to a new Observable when a new value comes in. They differ in what happens if a
        new value arrives before the old one has finished.</p>

      <article id="switchmap" data-cat="transform">
        <span class="eyebrow">Transformation</span>
        <h3>switchMap()</h3>
        <p>switchMap() is an RxJS operator used to switch to a new Observable and cancel the previous one. It cancels
          the previous Observable and subscribes to the latest one.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Useful when multiple API calls are triggered continuously, like in a search box, where we only care about
            the latest result.</li>
          <li>Helps avoid unnecessary calls and race conditions.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>this.searchControl.valueChanges.pipe(
  switchMap(term => this.api.search(term))
).subscribe(results => this.results = results);
// a new keystroke cancels the in-flight request for the previous one</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Use case:</b> a search box, where multiple calls fire in quick succession but only the
          latest result matters.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Cancels the old call and switches to the newest one.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="mergemap" data-cat="transform">
        <span class="eyebrow">Transformation</span>
        <h3>mergeMap()</h3>
        <p>mergeMap() is an RxJS operator used to handle multiple Observables in parallel. It subscribes to all inner
          Observables and runs them at the same time, without cancelling any of them.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Runs multiple API calls at the same time and processes every result.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>uploadClicks$.pipe(
  mergeMap(file => this.api.upload(file))
).subscribe(res => console.log('uploaded', res));
// every file uploads in parallel — nothing waits on anything else</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Use case:</b> uploading several files together.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Runs all the calls at the same time, in parallel.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="concatmap" data-cat="transform">
        <span class="eyebrow">Transformation</span>
        <h3>concatMap()</h3>
        <p>concatMap() is an RxJS operator used to run Observables one by one, in sequence. It waits for each one to
          complete before starting the next.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Keeps everything in order — nothing overlaps.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>this.saveForm().pipe(
  concatMap(() => this.api.getUpdatedList())
).subscribe(list => this.list = list);
// getUpdatedList only fires once the save has fully completed</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Use case:</b> save form data, then — only after it succeeds — fetch the refreshed list.
        </div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Runs the calls one by one, in order.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="exhaustmap" data-cat="transform">
        <span class="eyebrow">Transformation</span>
        <h3>exhaustMap()</h3>
        <p>exhaustMap() is an RxJS operator used to ignore new requests while one is already running.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Prevents duplicate API calls.</li>
          <li>Avoids multiple clicks or double form submissions.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>submitClicks$.pipe(
  exhaustMap(() => this.api.submitForm())
).subscribe();
// clicks while a submit is in flight are dropped, not queued</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Use case:</b> a user clicks Submit five times — only the first request is processed, the
          rest are ignored.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Ignores new calls while one is already running.</div>
      </article>

      <div class="usecase" style="margin-top:1rem">
        <b>In short:</b> switchMap cancels previous calls, mergeMap handles parallel requests, concatMap ensures
        execution in order, exhaustMap ignores new requests until the previous one completes, and <a
          href="#takeuntil">takeUntil</a> is used for automatic cleanup and to avoid memory leaks.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= FILTERING ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">23</span>
        <h2>Filtering &amp; cleanup</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="debouncetime" data-cat="filter">
        <span class="eyebrow">Timing</span>
        <h3>debounceTime()</h3>
        <p>debounceTime() waits for a pause before emitting the latest value. It is used to avoid unnecessary API calls
          and reduce how often frequent events fire.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Commonly used in search inputs to avoid multiple API calls and improve performance.</li>
          <li>Often paired with <code>distinctUntilChanged</code> and <code>switchMap</code> in a search box.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>this.searchControl.valueChanges.pipe(
  debounceTime(300),
  distinctUntilChanged(),
  switchMap(term => this.searchService.search(term))
).subscribe(results => this.results = results);</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Example:</b> user types quickly: a → ab → abc. Without debounce, that's 3 API calls.
          With debounce, only 1 API call fires — after the pause.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Waits for a pause before sending the latest value.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="throttletime" data-cat="filter">
        <span class="eyebrow">Timing</span>
        <h3>throttleTime()</h3>
        <p>throttleTime() is an RxJS operator that emits the first value immediately, and then ignores the next values
          for a specific time.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Use it to limit execution rate and control frequent events.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>fromEvent(window, 'scroll').pipe(
  throttleTime(200)
).subscribe(() => this.onScroll());</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Use case:</b> scroll or button click. User scrolls continuously — with throttle, we only
          get a limited number of events instead of every single one.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Sends the first value immediately, then blocks values for a fixed time.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="distinctuntilchanged" data-cat="filter">
        <span class="eyebrow">Filter</span>
        <h3>distinctUntilChanged()</h3>
        <p>distinctUntilChanged() emits a value only when it is different from the previous emitted value. In simple
          words — if the value is the same as the previous one, it is ignored.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Avoids duplicate API calls.</li>
          <li>Improves performance and reduces unnecessary processing.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Example:</b> user types in search: Angular, Angular, Angular, React. Without
          <code>distinctUntilChanged()</code>, the API is called 4 times. With it, the API is called only 2 times — for
          Angular and React — because the repeated "Angular" is the same as the value before it.
        </div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Skips a value if it's the same as the one right before it.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="takeuntil" data-cat="filter">
        <span class="eyebrow">Cleanup — classic</span>
        <h3>takeUntil()</h3>
        <p>takeUntil is used to automatically unsubscribe from an Observable when another Observable emits a value. It
          is commonly used in Angular to clean up subscriptions in <code>ngOnDestroy</code> and prevent memory leaks.
          This is the old way — you create your own Subject and complete it manually.</p>
        <pre><code>import { Subject, takeUntil } from 'rxjs';

export class UserComponent implements OnDestroy {
  private destroy$ = new Subject&lt;void&gt;();

  ngOnInit() {
    this.userService.getUsers()
      .pipe(takeUntil(this.destroy$))
      .subscribe();
  }

  ngOnDestroy() {
    this.destroy$.next();
    this.destroy$.complete();
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p>Problem with this approach: extra code — we need a Subject, we need <code>ngOnDestroy</code>, and it's easy
          to forget the cleanup.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Unsubscribes automatically when another Observable emits.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="takeuntildestroyed" data-cat="filter">
        <span class="eyebrow">Cleanup — modern</span>
        <h3>takeUntilDestroyed()</h3>
        <p>takeUntilDestroyed was introduced in Angular 16. It automatically unsubscribes when the component is
          destroyed, and needs less code than the old way.</p>
        <pre><code>import { takeUntilDestroyed } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';

export class UserComponent {
  private destroyRef = inject(DestroyRef);

  ngOnInit() {
    this.userService.getUsers()
      .pipe(takeUntilDestroyed(this.destroyRef))
      .subscribe();
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p>Benefits: no Subject, no ngOnDestroy, less code, automatic cleanup — this is the recommended approach in
          Angular 16+.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Auto-unsubscribes when the component is destroyed (Angular 16+, no manual Subject).</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= COMBINATION ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">24</span>
        <h2>Combination operators</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="combinelatest" data-cat="combine">
        <span class="eyebrow">Combination</span>
        <h3>combineLatest()</h3>
        <p>combineLatest() combines multiple Observables and emits the latest values whenever any one of them changes.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Used when the UI depends on multiple inputs and needs the latest values from all streams.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>combineLatest([searchText$, filter$]).subscribe(([text, filter]) => {
  this.results = applyFilter(text, filter);
});</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Use case:</b> search text plus a filter dropdown. When the user changes the search text
          or the filter, the UI updates using the latest values from both.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Sends the latest values from every source whenever any one of them changes.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="forkjoin" data-cat="combine">
        <span class="eyebrow">Combination</span>
        <h3>forkJoin()</h3>
        <p>forkJoin() is used to run multiple Observables in parallel and get the results only when all of them
          complete. It works like <code>Promise.all()</code>.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Use it when multiple API calls are needed and we need all results together.</li>
          <li>Returns the combined result as one object, once every source has finished.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>ngOnInit() {
  forkJoin({
    users: this.apiService.getUsers(),
    products: this.apiService.getProducts(),
    orders: this.apiService.getOrders()
  }).subscribe({
    next: (res) => {
      console.log(res.users);
      console.log(res.products);
      console.log(res.orders);
    },
    error: (err) => console.log(err)
  });
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Use case:</b> handling multiple API calls on page load. forkJoin is the most common
          choice here — it runs the calls in parallel and returns all responses together, once they've all finished.
        </div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Waits for every source to finish, then sends all the results together.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="zip" data-cat="combine">
        <span class="eyebrow">Combination</span>
        <h3>zip()</h3>
        <p>zip() combines multiple Observables by pairing values that are at the same index. It waits for each
          Observable to emit one value, then emits them together as a pair.</p>
        <pre><code>zip(questions$, answers$).subscribe(([question, answer]) => {
  console.log(question, '->', answer);
});</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Use case:</b> a quiz app with questions and answers, paired up one to one.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Pairs values from each source Observable by their position.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="withlatestfrom" data-cat="combine">
        <span class="eyebrow">Combination</span>
        <h3>withLatestFrom()</h3>
        <p>withLatestFrom is used when we want to get the latest value from another Observable, whenever the main
          Observable emits. The second stream doesn't trigger anything on its own — it just gives its latest value along
          for the ride.</p>
        <pre><code>loginClicked$.pipe(
  withLatestFrom(username$)
).subscribe(([_, username]) => {
  console.log('Login with', username);
});</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Example:</b> a Login button and a username field. Username typed: Ram, Saurabh, John.
          When the Login button is clicked, we want the latest username. Login button = main Observable, username =
          latest-value Observable. withLatestFrom says: when the login button is clicked, give me the latest username.
          Output: "Login with John".</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Takes the latest value from another Observable whenever the main one emits.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="race" data-cat="combine">
        <span class="eyebrow">Combination</span>
        <h3>race()</h3>
        <p>race is used to take the value from whichever Observable emits first. The fastest Observable wins.</p>
        <pre><code>import { race, timer } from 'rxjs';
import { mapTo } from 'rxjs/operators';

const obs1 = timer(2000).pipe(mapTo('API 1'));
const obs2 = timer(1000).pipe(mapTo('API 2'));

race(obs1, obs2).subscribe(data => console.log(data));
// logs 'API 2' — it resolved first</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Takes the value from whichever Observable emits first; ignores the rest.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= UTILITY ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">25</span>
        <h2>Utility &amp; error handling</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="tap" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">Utility</span>
        <h3>tap()</h3>
        <p>tap is used for side effects. It does <b>not</b> change the data — it just lets you look at it or react to it
          along the way.</p>
        <pre><code>import { of } from 'rxjs';
import { tap } from 'rxjs/operators';

of(10, 20).pipe(
  tap(value => console.log('Before:', value))
).subscribe(data => {
  console.log('Final:', data);
});</code></pre>
        <p>Mostly used for: console logging, debugging, and checking the API response. Also used to show a loader (often
          paired with <a href="#finalize">finalize</a> to hide it again).</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Runs a side effect — like logging or a loader — without changing the data.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="catcherror" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">Error handling</span>
        <h3>catchError()</h3>
        <p>catchError() is an RxJS operator used to catch and handle errors in an Observable. It lets us show an error
          message, log the error, or return fallback data, so the application keeps working instead of failing.</p>
        <pre><code>this.productService.getProducts().pipe(
  catchError(error => {
    this.toastService.show('Unable to load products');
    return of([]);
  })
).subscribe(products => {
  this.products = products;
});</code></pre>
        <p><code>of([])</code> creates an Observable that emits an empty array. So the component still receives valid
          data, and doesn't have to deal with <code>undefined</code>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Catches a failed Observable and returns fallback data instead.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="finalize" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">Cleanup</span>
        <h3>finalize()</h3>
        <p>finalize() is an RxJS operator that runs when an Observable finishes — whether it completes, fails, or is
          unsubscribed. It is commonly used for cleanup tasks, like hiding a loader after an API call.</p>
        <p>Yes — finalize() runs even if the API returns an error. That's exactly why it's the reliable place for
          cleanup.</p>
        <pre><code>this.loader.show();

this.http.get('/api/users').pipe(
  finalize(() => this.loader.hide())
).subscribe();</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>Hides a loader / spinner.</li>
          <li>Resets UI state.</li>
          <li>Stops a progress bar.</li>
          <li>Cleans up resources.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Runs cleanup code when the stream finishes, errors, or is unsubscribed.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="sharereplay" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">Caching</span>
        <h3>shareReplay() &amp; caching</h3>
        <p>In RxJS, caching means storing the API response so multiple subscribers can use the same data without calling
          the API again. The most common way to do this is <code>shareReplay()</code> — it stores the response and
          shares it with every subscriber.</p>
        <pre><code>private products$ = this.http.get&lt;Product[]&gt;('/api/products').pipe(
  shareReplay(1)
);

getProducts() {
  return this.products$; // the same cached response for every subscriber
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Typical uses:</b> master data, user profiles, and application configuration — anything
          fetched once and read from many places.</div>
        <p>Other caching approaches also exist: a <a href="#behaviorsubject">BehaviorSubject</a> or <a
            href="#replaysubject">ReplaySubject</a>, a plain HTTP service variable, browser storage
          (<code>localStorage</code> / <code>sessionStorage</code>), or a state-management layer like <a
            href="#ngrx">NgRx</a> or Signals. But for caching an API response, <code>shareReplay(1)</code> is the
          standard RxJS solution.</p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= PATTERNS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">26</span>
        <h2>Multicasting patterns</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="unicast-multicast" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Execution model</span>
        <h3>Unicast vs. Multicast</h3>
        <p><b>Unicast</b> — every subscriber gets its own separate execution. One Observable, multiple subscribers, and
          each one gets a separate data stream. Angular's <code>HttpClient</code> observables are unicast by default —
          that's why the API can get called multiple times when several components subscribe to it directly.</p>
        <pre><code>const observable = new Observable(observer => {
  console.log('API Called');
  observer.next(Math.random());
});

observable.subscribe(data => console.log('User 1', data));
observable.subscribe(data => console.log('User 2', data));
// "API Called" logs twice — each subscribe() triggers its own run</code></pre>
        <p><b>Multicast</b> — all subscribers share the same execution and the same data. One Observable, one execution,
          shared among multiple subscribers. In Angular, we usually get this with a <a href="#subject">Subject</a> or <a
            href="#behaviorsubject">BehaviorSubject</a> in a service — the API is called once, and the data is shared
          everywhere.</p>
        <pre><code>const subject = new Subject();
subject.subscribe(data => console.log('User 1', data));
subject.subscribe(data => console.log('User 2', data));
subject.next('Hello');
// both subscribers log 'Hello' from the same single emission</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Example:</b> multiple components need the logged-in user's data. Instead of calling the
          API in every component, we use a BehaviorSubject in a service — so the API is called once, and the data is
          shared everywhere.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hot-cold" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Timing model</span>
        <h3>Hot vs. Cold Observable</h3>
        <p>A <b>cold</b> Observable starts only when it is subscribed to, and each subscriber gets its own separate
          execution — this is how HTTP calls behave in Angular. A <b>hot</b> Observable starts even without a
          subscription, and it shares the same data among all subscribers — like DOM events or WebSocket streams.</p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= ANGULAR FEATURES ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="chapter-divider">
        <span class="chapter-label">Part G · Angular Signals</span>
        <div class="chapter-title">Angular Signals</div>
        <p class="chapter-dek">The newer, simpler way to manage state.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">27</span>
        <h2>Angular reactive features</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="signals" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Angular 16+</span>
        <h3>Signals</h3>
        <p>Signals are a reactive state-management feature introduced in Angular 16. They automatically track changes
          and update the UI whenever a signal's value changes.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Used for local UI state, and they work synchronously.</li>
          <li>Automatically update the template when the value changes — we don't need to subscribe or unsubscribe.</li>
          <li>Improve performance by reducing unnecessary change detection.</li>
          <li>Good for things like a sidebar toggle, theme change, or cart count.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>This is the short version. The full Signals toolkit — <code>effect()</code>, <code>computed()</code>, Signal
          Inputs, <code>toSignal()</code>, <code>toObservable()</code>, <code>linkedSignal()</code>,
          <code>resource()</code>, <code>httpResource</code>, and <code>model()</code> — is covered in
          <a href="#why-signals">Signals in depth</a> below.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article id="async-pipe" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Template feature</span>
        <h3>Async pipe</h3>
        <p>Async pipe automatically subscribes to an Observable and updates the UI, and also unsubscribes automatically
          to avoid memory leaks. It works with both Observables and Promises.</p>

        <p><b>Without async pipe</b> — manual subscribe, need to unsubscribe, risk of memory leaks:</p>
        <pre><code>ngOnInit() {
  this.userService.getUsers().subscribe(data => {
    this.users = data;
  });
}</code></pre>
        <pre><code>&lt;div *ngFor="let user of users"&gt;{{ user.name }}&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>

        <p><b>With async pipe</b> — no subscribe, no unsubscribe, clean code:</p>
        <pre><code>users$ = this.userService.getUsers();</code></pre>
        <pre><code>&lt;div *ngFor="let user of users$ | async"&gt;{{ user.name }}&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>

        <ul>
          <li><b>Auto subscription</b> — when the component loads, it starts listening.</li>
          <li><b>Auto unsubscription</b> — when the component is destroyed, it unsubscribes. This prevents memory leaks
            and unwanted API calls.</li>
          <li>Works with both Observables and Promises.</li>
          <li>Triggers change detection, even with <code>OnPush</code> — it marks the component for checking when new
            data arrives.</li>
          <li>Always shows the latest emitted value.</li>
          <li>Works well with Signals-based architecture and <code>OnPush</code>, for performant, reactive components
            without manual subscriptions.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article id="ngrx" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">State management</span>
        <h3>NgRx</h3>
        <p>NgRx is a library used in Angular to manage application state in a centralized way. It stores all app data in
          one place.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Components don't manage data directly.</li>
          <li>They just read the data and send actions.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= SIGNALS IN DEPTH ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">28</span>
        <h2>Signals in depth</h2>
      </div>
      <p>The rest of the Signals API — the parts that show up once you go past a simple sidebar toggle or theme
        switch.</p>

      <article id="why-signals" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Angular 16+</span>
        <h3>Why were Signals introduced?</h3>
        <p>Signals were introduced to improve Angular's performance and provide fine-grained reactivity. They reduce
          unnecessary change detection, simplify state management, and automatically update only the affected parts of
          the UI — not the whole component.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Better performance and fine-grained reactivity — only the affected UI parts update.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="effect" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Side effects</span>
        <h3>effect()</h3>
        <p>effect() is a function that runs automatically when a signal's value changes. It is used for side effects,
          not for producing a value. It automatically tracks which signals it depends on, and re-runs whenever those
          signals change.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Common uses: API calls, saving to localStorage, console.log, updating the DOM.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>count = signal(0);

constructor() {
  effect(() => {
    console.log('Count changed:', this.count());
  });
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Runs a side effect automatically whenever a signal it reads changes.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="computed" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Derived state</span>
        <h3>computed()</h3>
        <p>computed() is used to create a derived signal. It automatically recalculates its value whenever the signals
          it depends on change.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Used to derive values such as a full name, a total price, or filtered data.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>firstName = signal('Ram');
lastName = signal('Kumar');

fullName = computed(() => `${this.firstName()} ${this.lastName()}`);</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Creates a derived signal that recalculates when its dependencies change.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="signal-inputs" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Angular 17+</span>
        <h3>Signal Inputs</h3>
        <p>Signal Inputs are a new way for a component to receive input values as Signals, using the
          <code>input()</code> function instead of the <code>@Input()</code> decorator. Since the value is reactive, we
          can use <code>computed()</code> and <code>effect()</code> directly, without <code>ngOnChanges()</code> — less
          boilerplate, better performance.
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Optional input
name = input&lt;string&gt;();

// Required input
name = input.required&lt;string&gt;();</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Receive a component input as a signal with input(), instead of @Input() (Angular 17+).</div>
      </article>

      <article id="signals-vs-rxjs" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Signals vs. RxJS</span>
        <h3>Are Signals a replacement for RxJS?</h3>
        <p>No — they solve different problems. Signals are mainly used for local UI state management, like a sidebar
          toggle, a loader state, or a selected tab, because they update the UI automatically and improve performance.
          RxJS is still used for handling async operations like API calls, events, and complex reactive logic.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Signals handle local UI state; RxJS still handles async operations and events.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="tosignal" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Observable → Signal</span>
        <h3>toSignal()</h3>
        <p>toSignal() converts an RxJS Observable into a Signal. It automatically keeps the Signal updated whenever the
          Observable emits a new value — so we can keep using Observable-based services like <code>HttpClient</code>,
          but read the result like a normal signal.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>No manual <code>subscribe()</code>.</li>
          <li>No async pipe needed.</li>
          <li>UI updates automatically.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>export class UserComponent {
  users = toSignal(this.userService.getUsers(), { initialValue: [] });
}</code></pre>
        <pre><code>@for (user of users(); track user.id) {
  &lt;p&gt;{{ user.name }}&lt;/p&gt;
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Converts an Observable into a signal, kept in sync automatically.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="toobservable" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Signal → Observable</span>
        <h3>toObservable()</h3>
        <p>toObservable() converts a Signal into an RxJS Observable. Use it when state is stored in a Signal, but you
          need RxJS operators such as <code>debounceTime()</code> or <code>switchMap()</code>.</p>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Example:</b> a search box stores its text in a Signal. To wait 500ms
          (<code>debounceTime</code>), ignore duplicate values (<code>distinctUntilChanged</code>), and call the API
          (<code>switchMap</code>), first convert the Signal into an Observable.</div>
        <pre><code>import { toObservable } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';

searchText = signal('');
searchText$ = toObservable(this.searchText);

this.searchText$.pipe(
  debounceTime(500),
  distinctUntilChanged(),
  switchMap(text => this.productService.search(text))
).subscribe();</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Converts a signal into an Observable, so RxJS operators can be used on it.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="linkedsignal" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Read + write</span>
        <h3>linkedSignal()</h3>
        <p>linkedSignal() is a writable Signal whose value is linked to another Signal. It's writable, so we can update
          it manually, while it still recalculates automatically when the source Signal changes. Best for editable
          derived state.</p>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Example:</b> a warehouse list where the first warehouse is selected by default, the
          user can pick a different one, and if a new list arrives from the API, the default selection updates on its
          own.</div>
        <pre><code>warehouses = signal(['Nagpur', 'Pune', 'Mumbai']);
selectedWarehouse = linkedSignal(() => this.warehouses()[0]);

selectedWarehouse.set('Mumbai');</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A writable signal whose default value is linked to — and follows — another signal.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="resource" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Async data</span>
        <h3>resource()</h3>
        <p>resource() is used for handling asynchronous data, such as an HTTP request. It automatically manages the
          loading, success, and error states using Signals, so we don't need to manage loading or error flags
          manually.</p>
        <pre><code>products = resource({
  loader: () => this.productService.getProducts()
});</code></pre>
        <pre><code>@if (products.isLoading()) {
  &lt;p&gt;Loading...&lt;/p&gt;
}
@if (products.error()) {
  &lt;p&gt;Something went wrong&lt;/p&gt;
}
@if (products.value()) {
  @for (product of products.value(); track product.id) {
    &lt;p&gt;{{ product.name }}&lt;/p&gt;
  }
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Manages loading, success, and error state for async data using signals.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="httpresource" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Async data</span>
        <h3>httpResource</h3>
        <p>httpResource is a newer Angular feature for reactive HTTP data fetching using Signals. It automatically
          calls the API, manages loading and error states, and updates the UI reactively — without a manual
          <code>subscribe()</code>.
        </p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Reactive HTTP data fetching built on signals, without manual subscribe().</div>
      </article>

      <article id="model" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Two-way binding</span>
        <h3>model()</h3>
        <p>model() is a writable Signal used for two-way data binding between a parent and a child component. It
          replaces the need for <code>@Input()</code> and <code>@Output()</code> with a single API.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Before <code>model()</code>: <code>@Input()</code> to receive data, plus <code>@Output()</code> +
            <code>EventEmitter</code> to send updates back.
          </li>
          <li>With <code>model()</code>: one property does both jobs — no <code>EventEmitter</code>, no
            <code>@Output()</code>, no <code>.emit()</code>.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>// Parent
searchText = '';</code></pre>
        <pre><code>&lt;app-search [(search)]="searchText"&gt;&lt;/app-search&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Searching for: {{ searchText }}&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
        <pre><code>// Child
search = model('');
search.set('Angular');</code></pre>
        <pre><code>&lt;input
  [value]="search()"
  (input)="search.set(($event.target as HTMLInputElement).value)"&gt;</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase"><b>Use it when:</b> building reusable form-like components that need to both receive and
          update a value — a search box, dropdown, date picker, toggle switch, checkbox, or rating component.</div>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A writable signal for two-way binding between a parent and a child component.</div>
      </article>
    </section>


    <!-- ================= NEW TEMPLATE SYNTAX &AMP; SIGNALS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="chapter-divider">
        <span class="chapter-label">Part H · Angular in practice</span>
        <div class="chapter-title">Angular in practice</div>
        <p class="chapter-dek">The topics that come up after the basics: new syntax, routing, forms, HTTP, build tools, and architecture.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">29</span>
        <h2>New template syntax &amp; signals</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="ng-control-flow" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Angular 17+</span>
        <h3>@if, @for &amp; @switch</h3>
        <p>Angular 17 added control flow blocks to templates. They are faster, need no <code>CommonModule</code> import, and <code>@for</code> requires a <code>track</code> expression.</p>
        <pre><code>@if (user(); as u) {
  &lt;p&gt;Hello {{ u.name }}&lt;/p&gt;
} @else if (loading()) {
  &lt;p&gt;Loading...&lt;/p&gt;
} @else {
  &lt;p&gt;Please log in&lt;/p&gt;
}

@for (order of orders(); track order.id; let i = $index) {
  &lt;li&gt;{{ i + 1 }}. {{ order.number }}&lt;/li&gt;
} @empty {
  &lt;li&gt;No orders yet&lt;/li&gt;
}

@switch (status()) {
  @case ('paid') { &lt;span&gt;Paid&lt;/span&gt; }
  @case ('pending') { &lt;span&gt;Pending&lt;/span&gt; }
  @default { &lt;span&gt;Unknown&lt;/span&gt; }
}</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li><code>@for</code> gives <code>$index</code>, <code>$first</code>, <code>$last</code>, <code>$even</code>, <code>$odd</code>, and <code>$count</code>.</li>
          <li><code>track</code> replaces <code>trackBy</code>: <code>track item.id</code>, or <code>track $index</code> for simple lists.</li>
          <li>Run <code>ng generate @angular/core:control-flow</code> to migrate old templates.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Built-in control flow that replaces *ngIf, *ngFor, and *ngSwitch, with no imports needed.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-let" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Angular 18.1+</span>
        <h3>@let declarations</h3>
        <pre><code>@let fullName = user().first + ' ' + user().last;
@let total = price() * qty();

&lt;h2&gt;{{ fullName }}&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Total: {{ total | currency:'INR' }}&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
        <p>The variable is read-only, and visible in the template below the declaration. It replaces the old <code>*ngIf="x as y"</code> trick for storing values.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Store a value in a template variable once, and reuse it.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-inject-fn" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Angular 14+</span>
        <h3>inject() function</h3>
        <pre><code>@Component({ /* ... */ })
export class OrdersComponent {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
  private route = inject(ActivatedRoute);
}

export const authGuard: CanActivateFn = () =&gt; {
  const auth = inject(AuthService);        // works in functional guards and interceptors
  return auth.isLoggedIn() || inject(Router).createUrlTree(['/login']);
};</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li><code>inject()</code> only works in an <b>injection context</b>: field initializers, constructors, factory functions, and functional guards. It does not work inside <code>ngOnInit</code> or <code>subscribe</code> callbacks.</li>
          <li>Use <code>runInInjectionContext(injector, () =&gt; ...)</code> to call it elsewhere.</li>
          <li>Benefits: less boilerplate, works with inheritance, and enables functional guards, resolvers, and interceptors.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Get a dependency without a constructor, in classes, functions, and guards.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-output-fn" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Angular 17.3+</span>
        <h3>output(), viewChild() &amp; contentChild()</h3>
        <pre><code>@Component({ selector: 'app-search', /* ... */ })
export class SearchComponent {
  query = input('');                         // replaces @Input
  searched = output&lt;string&gt;();               // replaces @Output + EventEmitter
  box = viewChild&lt;ElementRef&gt;('box');        // replaces @ViewChild (a signal)
  items = viewChildren(ItemComponent);       // replaces @ViewChildren
  header = contentChild(HeaderDirective);    // replaces @ContentChild

  onEnter() {
    this.searched.emit(this.query());
    this.box()?.nativeElement.focus();
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p>Query signals update on their own, so you can read them inside <code>computed()</code> and <code>effect()</code> without <code>ngAfterViewInit</code>. See <a href="#signal-inputs">Signal Inputs</a> and <a href="#model">model()</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Signal-based replacements for @Output and the query decorators.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-effect-details" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Signals</span>
        <h3>effect() cleanup &amp; untracked()</h3>
        <pre><code>constructor() {
  effect((onCleanup) =&gt; {
    const id = this.userId();                    // tracked: the effect re-runs when this changes
    const sub = this.api.watch(id).subscribe();
    onCleanup(() =&gt; sub.unsubscribe());          // runs before the next run, and on destroy

    untracked(() =&gt; this.log.add(`Watching ${id}`));   // read or write without creating a dependency
  });
}</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>Effects run asynchronously, during change detection.</li>
          <li>Writing to signals inside an effect is blocked by default. Prefer <code>computed()</code> or <code>linkedSignal()</code> for derived values; use <code>effect()</code> only for side effects such as logging, localStorage, or DOM work.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Run cleanup before an effect re-runs, and read a signal without tracking it.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-destroyref" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Angular 16+</span>
        <h3>DestroyRef</h3>
        <pre><code>export class ChartComponent {
  private destroyRef = inject(DestroyRef);

  constructor() {
    const id = setInterval(() =&gt; this.refresh(), 5000);
    this.destroyRef.onDestroy(() =&gt; clearInterval(id));    // runs when the component is destroyed
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p><code>takeUntilDestroyed()</code> uses <code>DestroyRef</code> internally. See <a href="#takeuntildestroyed">takeUntilDestroyed</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Register cleanup code from anywhere, without implementing ngOnDestroy.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-content-select" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Templates</span>
        <h3>Content projection with ng-content select</h3>
        <pre><code>&lt;!-- card.component.html --&gt;
&lt;div class="card"&gt;
  &lt;header&gt;&lt;ng-content select="[card-title]"&gt;&lt;/ng-content&gt;&lt;/header&gt;
  &lt;div class="body"&gt;&lt;ng-content&gt;&lt;/ng-content&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;footer&gt;&lt;ng-content select="[card-actions]"&gt;&lt;/ng-content&gt;&lt;/footer&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;!-- usage --&gt;
&lt;app-card&gt;
  &lt;h3 card-title&gt;Q2 Return&lt;/h3&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Filed on 31 July&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;!-- goes to the default slot --&gt;
  &lt;button card-actions&gt;View&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/app-card&gt;</code></pre>
        <p>Use <code>ngProjectAs</code> to move an element into a slot with a different selector. Projected content is created by the parent, even when the child decides where to show it.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Project different content into different slots of a component.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-template-outlet" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Templates</span>
        <h3>ng-template &amp; ngTemplateOutlet</h3>
        <pre><code>&lt;ng-template #rowTpl let-order let-i="index"&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;{{ i + 1 }}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;{{ order.number }}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/ng-template&gt;

&lt;table&gt;
  @for (o of orders(); track o.id; let i = $index) {
    &lt;ng-container *ngTemplateOutlet="rowTpl; context: { $implicit: o, index: i }"&gt;&lt;/ng-container&gt;
  }
&lt;/table&gt;</code></pre>
        <p>A common use is a reusable table or list where the parent supplies the cell template, passed in with <code>@ContentChild(TemplateRef)</code> or <code>contentChild(TemplateRef)</code>. <code>ng-template</code> is also what <code>*ngIf</code> and <code>*ngFor</code> expand into. See <a href="#templateref">TemplateRef</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Define a template once, and render it with data wherever you need it.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-ngclass-ngstyle" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Templates</span>
        <h3>ngClass, ngStyle &amp; class/style bindings</h3>
        <pre><code>&lt;div [class.active]="isActive"&gt;...&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div [style.width.px]="width" [style.color]="color"&gt;...&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div [ngClass]="{ active: isActive, 'has-error': hasError }"&gt;...&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div [ngStyle]="{ 'font-size.px': size }"&gt;...&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div [class]="'card ' + extra"&gt;...&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>Single class or style: <code>[class.x]</code> and <code>[style.x]</code>. Simple and fast.</li>
          <li>Several at once or from an object: <code>ngClass</code> and <code>ngStyle</code>.</li>
          <li>Binding <code>[class]</code> replaces only the classes it manages, in recent Angular versions.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Three ways to apply classes and styles dynamically.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-host-directives" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Angular 15+</span>
        <h3>Directive composition (hostDirectives)</h3>
        <pre><code>@Component({
  selector: 'app-button',
  hostDirectives: [
    { directive: TooltipDirective, inputs: ['tooltip'] },
    HighlightDirective
  ],
  template: `&lt;ng-content /&gt;`
})
export class ButtonComponent {}</code></pre>
        <p>Every <code>&lt;app-button&gt;</code> now behaves as if it had both directives, and you choose which of their inputs and outputs are exposed. It replaces many cases of inheritance.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Attach directives to a component from inside its decorator.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-custom-pipe" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pipes</span>
        <h3>Writing a custom pipe</h3>
        <pre><code>@Pipe({ name: 'rupees', standalone: true })
export class RupeesPipe implements PipeTransform {
  transform(value: number | null, decimals = 2): string {
    if (value == null) return '-';
    return '₹' + value.toLocaleString('en-IN', { minimumFractionDigits: decimals });
  }
}</code></pre>
        <pre><code>&lt;p&gt;{{ amount | rupees }}&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;{{ amount | rupees:0 }}&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
        <p>Built-in pipes: <code>date</code>, <code>currency</code>, <code>number</code>, <code>percent</code>, <code>uppercase</code>, <code>lowercase</code>, <code>titlecase</code>, <code>json</code>, <code>slice</code>, <code>async</code>, <code>keyvalue</code>, <code>i18nPlural</code>. Prefer a pipe over a method call in the template: a pure pipe runs only when its input changes, while a method runs on every change detection. See <a href="#pure-impure-pipe">Pure vs. Impure Pipe</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A class with a transform method; keep it pure.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= CHANGE DETECTION &AMP; ERRORS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">30</span>
        <h2>Change detection &amp; errors</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="ng-expression-changed" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Common error</span>
        <h3>ExpressionChangedAfterItHasBeenChecked</h3>
        <p><b>NG0100</b> happens in development mode: Angular checks every binding twice, and a value was different the second time. It usually means a child changed something the parent had already rendered.</p>
        <pre><code>// causes the error: the parent value changes after the parent was rendered
ngAfterViewInit() {
  this.title = 'Loaded';      // the parent template already used the old value
}</code></pre>
        <p>Fixes:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Move the change earlier (constructor or <code>ngOnInit</code>), or compute the value instead of setting it later.</li>
          <li>Use signals, which are designed to avoid this.</li>
          <li>Last resort: <code>Promise.resolve().then(() =&gt; ...)</code>, <code>setTimeout</code>, or <code>this.cd.detectChanges()</code>.</li>
          <li>Never use a getter that returns a new object or random value on each call, and do not change state from a method called inside the template.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A binding changed during the same check that rendered it; change it earlier, or in the next tick.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-cd-detach" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Change detection</span>
        <h3>detach, reattach &amp; ApplicationRef.tick</h3>
        <pre><code>constructor(private cd: ChangeDetectorRef) {
  this.cd.detach();                   // this component and its children are no longer checked
}
refreshNow() {
  this.cd.detectChanges();            // check it once, manually
}
// this.cd.reattach() puts it back</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>Use it for a heavy component that updates rarely, like a big live-data table that refreshes every few seconds.</li>
          <li><code>ApplicationRef.tick()</code> runs change detection for the whole app.</li>
          <li>Prefer <a href="#default-vs-onpush">OnPush</a> and signals first; detaching is an advanced tool.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Take a component out of change detection, and run it manually.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-simplechanges" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Lifecycle</span>
        <h3>ngOnChanges &amp; SimpleChanges</h3>
        <pre><code>ngOnChanges(changes: SimpleChanges) {
  if (changes['userId'] &amp;&amp; !changes['userId'].firstChange) {
    this.load(changes['userId'].currentValue);
  }
  console.log(changes['userId'].previousValue);
}</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>It runs only when the <b>reference</b> of an input changes, so mutating an object does not trigger it.</li>
          <li>Each entry has <code>previousValue</code>, <code>currentValue</code>, and <code>firstChange</code>.</li>
          <li>With signal inputs you use <code>effect()</code> or <code>computed()</code> instead.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> See the previous and current value of each changed @Input.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-common-errors" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Debugging</span>
        <h3>Common Angular errors</h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Error</th><th>Meaning and fix</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>NullInjectorError: No provider for X</code></td><td>the service is not provided; add <code>providedIn: 'root'</code> or a provider</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>NG0100 ExpressionChangedAfterItHasBeenChecked</code></td><td>a binding changed during the check; see <a href="#ng-expression-changed">above</a></td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>NG0303 Can't bind to 'x' since it isn't a known property</code></td><td>the component or module is not imported, or a typo</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>NG0304 'app-x' is not a known element</code></td><td>import the standalone component, or the module that declares it</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>Cannot read properties of undefined</code></td><td>data has not arrived yet; use <code>?.</code>, <code>@if</code>, or <code>async</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>Circular dependency in DI</code></td><td>two services inject each other; extract a third service</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>Can't resolve all parameters</code></td><td>missing <code>@Injectable()</code> or wrong constructor types</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>Maximum call stack size exceeded</code></td><td>usually infinite recursion, or an effect that writes to its own signal</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Also: <code>ng build</code> fails with budget errors when a bundle is too big; see <a href="#ng-budgets">bundle budgets</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> NullInjectorError, NG0100, NG0303, NG0201, and what each one means.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= ROUTING IN DEPTH ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">31</span>
        <h2>Routing in depth</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="ng-router-outlet" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Routing</span>
        <h3>router-outlet, routerLink &amp; child routes</h3>
        <pre><code>export const routes: Routes = [
  { path: 'login', component: LoginComponent },
  {
    path: 'tds',
    component: TdsLayoutComponent,          // contains its own &lt;router-outlet&gt;
    children: [
      { path: '', redirectTo: 'dashboard', pathMatch: 'full' },
      { path: 'dashboard', component: DashboardComponent },
      { path: 'returns/:id', component: ReturnDetailComponent }
    ]
  },
  { path: '**', component: NotFoundComponent }
];</code></pre>
        <pre><code>&lt;a routerLink="/tds/dashboard" routerLinkActive="active"&gt;Dashboard&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;a [routerLink]="['/tds/returns', returnId]" [queryParams]="{ tab: 'errors' }"&gt;Open&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;router-outlet /&gt;</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>An absolute path starts with <code>/</code>; without it the link is relative to the current route.</li>
          <li><code>routerLinkActive</code> adds a class when the link matches the current URL. Add <code>[routerLinkActiveOptions]="{ exact: true }"</code> for exact matching.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Nested routes render into a router-outlet inside their parent component.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-route-params" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Routing</span>
        <h3>ActivatedRoute: snapshot vs. paramMap</h3>
        <pre><code>// snapshot: read once. Fine if the component is destroyed and re-created on every navigation
const id = this.route.snapshot.paramMap.get('id');

// observable: needed when /returns/1 changes to /returns/2 and the SAME component instance is reused
this.route.paramMap.pipe(
  map(p =&gt; p.get('id')!),
  switchMap(id =&gt; this.api.getReturn(id))
).subscribe(r =&gt; this.return = r);

// query params and fragment
this.route.queryParamMap.subscribe(q =&gt; q.get('tab'));</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>With <code>provideRouter(routes, withComponentInputBinding())</code>, route params, query params, and resolver data arrive as normal <code>@Input()</code> or <code>input()</code> values, so you do not need <code>ActivatedRoute</code>.</li>
          <li>The router unsubscribes from <code>ActivatedRoute</code> observables for you.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> snapshot reads the value once; paramMap emits again when the same component stays and the parameter changes.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-navigate" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Routing</span>
        <h3>router.navigate vs. navigateByUrl</h3>
        <pre><code>this.router.navigate(['/returns', id]);                              // commands array
this.router.navigate(['edit'], { relativeTo: this.route });         // relative to the current route
this.router.navigate(['/search'], { queryParams: { q: 'tds' }, queryParamsHandling: 'merge' });
this.router.navigateByUrl('/returns/42?tab=errors');                // absolute URL string
this.router.navigate(['/login'], { replaceUrl: true });             // do not leave a history entry</code></pre>
        <p><code>state</code> passes data without putting it in the URL: <code>navigate([...], { state: { from: 'dashboard' } })</code>, read with <code>history.state</code>. Use the <code>Location</code> service for <code>back()</code>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> navigate takes a commands array and is relative-aware; navigateByUrl takes a full URL string.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-router-events" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Routing</span>
        <h3>Router events &amp; loading indicator</h3>
        <pre><code>this.router.events.pipe(
  filter((e): e is NavigationStart | NavigationEnd | NavigationCancel | NavigationError =&gt;
    e instanceof NavigationStart || e instanceof NavigationEnd ||
    e instanceof NavigationCancel || e instanceof NavigationError),
  takeUntilDestroyed()
).subscribe(e =&gt; {
  this.loading = e instanceof NavigationStart;     // show a progress bar while a lazy route loads
});</code></pre>
        <p>Other events: <code>RoutesRecognized</code>, <code>ResolveStart</code>, <code>ResolveEnd</code>, <code>GuardsCheckStart</code>, <code>Scroll</code>. Use them for analytics page views, a global loader, or scroll handling.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Listen to navigation start, end, error, and cancel.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-preloading-strategy" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Routing</span>
        <h3>Preloading strategies</h3>
        <pre><code>provideRouter(routes, withPreloading(PreloadAllModules));

// custom: preload only routes marked in route data
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class SelectivePreload implements PreloadingStrategy {
  preload(route: Route, load: () =&gt; Observable&lt;any&gt;) {
    return route.data?.['preload'] ? load() : of(null);
  }
}
// { path: 'reports', loadComponent: ..., data: { preload: true } }</code></pre>
        <p>See <a href="#lazy-loading">Lazy loading</a>. <code>loadComponent</code> lazily loads a single standalone component; <code>loadChildren</code> loads a whole route tree.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> PreloadAllModules loads every lazy route in the background; a custom strategy chooses which.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-router-misc" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Routing</span>
        <h3>Router configuration options</h3>
        <pre><code>provideRouter(
  routes,
  withComponentInputBinding(),                         // route data as component inputs
  withInMemoryScrolling({ scrollPositionRestoration: 'enabled', anchorScrolling: 'enabled' }),
  withHashLocation(),                                  // /#/dashboard instead of /dashboard
  withRouterConfig({ onSameUrlNavigation: 'reload' })
)</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Hash location</b> (<code>/#/page</code>) works on any static host with no server rewrite rules; the default <b>path location</b> needs the server to return <code>index.html</code> for every route.</li>
          <li>Page title per route: <code>{ path: 'orders', component: X, title: 'Orders' }</code>, or a custom <code>TitleStrategy</code>.</li>
          <li><code>&lt;base href="/"&gt;</code> in <code>index.html</code> sets the root for all relative links; change it with <code>ng build --base-href /app/</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Scroll restoration, hash location, title, input binding, and relative links.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-functional-guard" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Routing</span>
        <h3>Functional guards with inject()</h3>
        <pre><code>export const authGuard: CanActivateFn = (route, state) =&gt; {
  const auth = inject(AuthService);
  const router = inject(Router);
  return auth.isLoggedIn()
    ? true
    : router.createUrlTree(['/login'], { queryParams: { returnUrl: state.url } });
};

export const unsavedGuard: CanDeactivateFn&lt;FormComponent&gt; = (component) =&gt;
  !component.form.dirty || confirm('Discard your changes?');

{ path: 'filing', component: FilingComponent, canActivate: [authGuard], canDeactivate: [unsavedGuard] }</code></pre>
        <p>Class-based guards are deprecated in favor of functions. Returning a <code>UrlTree</code> redirects, which is better than calling <code>navigate()</code> inside the guard. See <a href="#route-guards">Route Guards</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A guard is just a function that returns true, false, or a UrlTree.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= FORMS IN PRACTICE ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">32</span>
        <h2>Forms in practice</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="ng-typed-forms" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Angular 14+</span>
        <h3>Typed reactive forms</h3>
        <pre><code>form = new FormGroup({
  name: new FormControl('', { nonNullable: true, validators: [Validators.required] }),
  age: new FormControl&lt;number | null&gt;(null),
  tags: new FormArray&lt;FormControl&lt;string&gt;&gt;([])
});

const v = this.form.getRawValue();   // { name: string; age: number | null; tags: string[] }
this.form.controls.name.setValue('Ram');
this.form.controls.nme;              // compile error: typo caught</code></pre>
        <p>Without <code>nonNullable: true</code>, <code>reset()</code> sets the value to <code>null</code>, so the type becomes <code>string | null</code>. Use <code>FormBuilder.nonNullable.group({...})</code> as a shortcut.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> FormControl, FormGroup, and FormArray carry types, so value and controls are checked.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-patch-set" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Forms</span>
        <h3>setValue vs. patchValue</h3>
        <pre><code>this.form.setValue({ name: 'Ram', age: 30 });     // all fields required, or it throws
this.form.patchValue({ name: 'Ram' });            // only the fields you give
this.form.reset({ name: '', age: null });         // back to the initial state: pristine and untouched</code></pre>
        <p>Use <code>patchValue</code> when filling a form from an API response that may not contain every field.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> setValue needs every field; patchValue accepts a part of them.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-form-states" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Forms</span>
        <h3>Form states: touched, dirty &amp; valid</h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>State</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>pristine</code> / <code>dirty</code></td><td>the user has not / has changed the value</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>untouched</code> / <code>touched</code></td><td>the field has not / has been blurred</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>valid</code> / <code>invalid</code></td><td>validation passes / fails</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>pending</code></td><td>an <a href="#async-validators">async validator</a> is running</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>disabled</code></td><td>excluded from the value and from validation</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <pre><code>submit() {
  if (this.form.invalid) {
    this.form.markAllAsTouched();     // show every error message
    return;
  }
  this.api.save(this.form.getRawValue()).subscribe();
}</code></pre>
        <pre><code>@if (form.controls.name.touched &amp;&amp; form.controls.name.hasError('required')) {
  &lt;small class="error"&gt;Name is required&lt;/small&gt;
}</code></pre>
        <p><code>form.value</code> leaves out disabled controls; <code>getRawValue()</code> includes them.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> pristine/dirty track changes; touched/untouched track focus; valid/invalid/pending track validation.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-valuechanges" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Forms</span>
        <h3>valueChanges &amp; statusChanges</h3>
        <pre><code>this.form.controls.pan.valueChanges.pipe(
  debounceTime(300),
  distinctUntilChanged(),
  switchMap(pan =&gt; this.api.verifyPan(pan)),
  takeUntilDestroyed()
).subscribe(result =&gt; this.panStatus = result);

this.form.controls.type.valueChanges.subscribe(t =&gt; {
  const gst = this.form.controls.gst;
  t === 'company' ? gst.enable() : gst.disable();     // react to another field
});</code></pre>
        <p>Pass <code>{ emitEvent: false }</code> to <code>setValue</code> or <code>patchValue</code> to avoid triggering <code>valueChanges</code> and creating a loop. See <a href="#debouncetime">debounceTime</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> An Observable of every change in a control or whole form.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-form-updateon" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Forms</span>
        <h3>updateOn: blur &amp; submit</h3>
        <pre><code>name = new FormControl('', { validators: [Validators.required], updateOn: 'blur' });

form = new FormGroup({ /* ... */ }, { updateOn: 'submit' });    // validate only when submitted</code></pre>
        <p>It is a good choice for expensive validators, such as ones that call an API. Values are <code>change</code> (default), <code>blur</code>, or <code>submit</code>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Run validation on blur or submit instead of every keystroke.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-ngmodel-vs-reactive" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Forms</span>
        <h3>ngModel vs. formControlName</h3>
        <pre><code>&lt;!-- template-driven --&gt;
&lt;form #f="ngForm" (ngSubmit)="save(f)"&gt;
  &lt;input name="email" [(ngModel)]="email" required email #e="ngModel"&gt;
  @if (e.invalid &amp;&amp; e.touched) { &lt;small&gt;Invalid email&lt;/small&gt; }
&lt;/form&gt;

&lt;!-- reactive --&gt;
&lt;form [formGroup]="form" (ngSubmit)="save()"&gt;
  &lt;input formControlName="email"&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>
        <p>Reactive forms are easier to test, handle dynamic and complex forms, and are synchronous. Both need <code>FormsModule</code> or <code>ReactiveFormsModule</code> imported. Do not mix <code>ngModel</code> with <code>formControlName</code> on the same input.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Template-driven forms use ngModel; reactive forms bind controls you create in the class.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-formarray-example" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Forms</span>
        <h3>FormArray: add and remove rows</h3>
        <pre><code>form = this.fb.group({
  deductees: this.fb.array([this.newRow()])
});

get deductees() { return this.form.controls.deductees; }

newRow() {
  return this.fb.group({ name: ['', Validators.required], amount: [0, Validators.min(1)] });
}
add() { this.deductees.push(this.newRow()); }
remove(i: number) { this.deductees.removeAt(i); }</code></pre>
        <pre><code>&lt;div formArrayName="deductees"&gt;
  @for (row of deductees.controls; track row; let i = $index) {
    &lt;div [formGroupName]="i"&gt;
      &lt;input formControlName="name"&gt;
      &lt;input type="number" formControlName="amount"&gt;
      &lt;button type="button" (click)="remove(i)"&gt;Remove&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  }
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;button type="button" (click)="add()"&gt;Add row&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A dynamic list of controls or groups, with add and remove methods.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= HTTP, AUTH &AMP; SECURITY IN PRACTICE ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">33</span>
        <h2>HTTP, auth &amp; security in practice</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="ng-functional-interceptor" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Angular 15+</span>
        <h3>Functional interceptors</h3>
        <pre><code>export const authInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) =&gt; {
  const token = inject(AuthService).token();
  const authReq = token
    ? req.clone({ setHeaders: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } })   // requests are immutable: clone
    : req;
  return next(authReq);
};

export const errorInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) =&gt;
  next(req).pipe(
    catchError((err: HttpErrorResponse) =&gt; {
      if (err.status === 401) inject(Router).navigate(['/login']);
      return throwError(() =&gt; err);
    })
  );

// app.config.ts
provideHttpClient(withInterceptors([authInterceptor, errorInterceptor]))</code></pre>
        <p>Interceptors run in the order listed for the request, and in reverse for the response. Class-based interceptors still work with <code>withInterceptorsFromDi()</code>. See <a href="#interceptor">Interceptor</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Write an interceptor as a function and register it with withInterceptors.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-jwt-refresh" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Authentication</span>
        <h3>JWT auth &amp; refresh token flow</h3>
        <p><b>Flow:</b> login returns an access token (short-lived, minutes) and a refresh token (long-lived). Each request sends the access token. On a 401, call the refresh endpoint, store the new token, and replay the failed request.</p>
        <pre><code>export const refreshInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) =&gt; {
  const auth = inject(AuthService);
  return next(req).pipe(
    catchError(err =&gt; {
      if (err.status !== 401 || req.url.includes('/refresh')) return throwError(() =&gt; err);
      return auth.refresh().pipe(                       // POST /refresh
        switchMap(token =&gt; next(req.clone({ setHeaders: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } })))
      );
    })
  );
};</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>With several requests failing together, share one refresh call (<code>shareReplay</code> or a <code>BehaviorSubject</code> flag), so you do not refresh five times.</li>
          <li><b>Where to store tokens:</b> <code>localStorage</code> is simple but any XSS script can read it. An <code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>Secure</code>, <code>SameSite</code> cookie cannot be read by JavaScript. See <a href="#js-web-storage">localStorage vs. cookies</a>.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Attach the access token, and on a 401 refresh it once and retry the request.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-http-params" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">HTTP</span>
        <h3>HttpParams, headers &amp; request options</h3>
        <pre><code>const params = new HttpParams().set('page', 2).set('status', 'paid');   // HttpParams is immutable
const headers = new HttpHeaders({ 'X-Tenant': 'abc' });

this.http.get&lt;Order[]&gt;('/api/orders', { params, headers });             // /api/orders?page=2&amp;status=paid

this.http.get&lt;Order[]&gt;('/api/orders', { observe: 'response' })
  .subscribe(res =&gt; res.headers.get('X-Total-Count'));                  // full HttpResponse

this.http.get('/api/report', { responseType: 'blob' });                 // download a file
this.http.post('/api/orders', body);                                    // an object body is sent as JSON</code></pre>
        <p>Each <code>.set()</code> returns a <b>new</b> object; forgetting to use the return value is a common bug.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Build query strings and headers; use observe and responseType for more than the body.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-file-upload" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">HTTP</span>
        <h3>File upload with progress</h3>
        <pre><code>upload(file: File) {
  const body = new FormData();
  body.append('file', file);                       // do not set Content-Type: the browser adds the boundary

  return this.http.post('/api/upload', body, { reportProgress: true, observe: 'events' }).pipe(
    map(event =&gt; {
      if (event.type === HttpEventType.UploadProgress)
        return Math.round(100 * event.loaded / (event.total ?? 1));
      if (event.type === HttpEventType.Response) return 100;
      return 0;
    })
  );
}</code></pre>
        <pre><code>&lt;input type="file" accept=".xlsx,.csv" (change)="onFile($event)"&gt;</code></pre>
        <pre><code>onFile(e: Event) {
  const file = (e.target as HTMLInputElement).files?.[0];
  if (file) this.upload(file).subscribe(p =&gt; this.progress = p);
}</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Send FormData and listen to progress events.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-retry-timeout" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">HTTP</span>
        <h3>retry, timeout &amp; firstValueFrom</h3>
        <pre><code>this.http.get('/api/orders').pipe(
  timeout(10000),                                          // error after 10 seconds
  retry({ count: 3, delay: (err, n) =&gt; timer(n * 1000) }), // retry with a growing delay
  catchError(() =&gt; of([]))
);

const data = await firstValueFrom(this.http.get('/api/orders'));   // replaces toPromise()</code></pre>
        <p>Retry only safe calls such as GET; retrying a POST can create duplicates. <code>toPromise()</code> is deprecated; use <code>firstValueFrom</code> (first value) or <code>lastValueFrom</code> (last value before completion).</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Retry failed calls with a delay, give up after a time limit, and convert to a Promise.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-xsrf-csp" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Security</span>
        <h3>XSRF, CSP &amp; other security</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>XSS</b> — Angular escapes bound values; see <a href="#xss-sanitization">XSS &amp; DomSanitizer</a>. Avoid <code>innerHTML</code> with user data and <code>bypassSecurityTrust*</code>.</li>
          <li><b>CSRF/XSRF</b> — for cookie-based auth, the server sets a cookie named <code>XSRF-TOKEN</code>, and Angular copies it into the <code>X-XSRF-TOKEN</code> header on mutating requests (<code>withXsrfConfiguration()</code> changes the names). Bearer-token APIs are not affected.</li>
          <li><b>CSP</b> — a <code>Content-Security-Policy</code> header limits where scripts and styles can load from; Angular supports nonces with <code>ngCspNonce</code>.</li>
          <li><b>Other:</b> HTTPS only, <code>HttpOnly</code> cookies, validate on the server, never put secrets in the frontend bundle, keep dependencies updated (<code>npm audit</code>), and use route guards only for UX, since real authorization must be on the server.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Cookie-to-header CSRF protection, Content Security Policy, and trusted types.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-cors-proxy" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">HTTP</span>
        <h3>CORS &amp; proxy.conf.json</h3>
        <pre><code>{
  "/api": {
    "target": "http://localhost:3000",
    "secure": false,
    "changeOrigin": true,
    "pathRewrite": { "^/api": "" }
  }
}</code></pre>
        <pre><code>"serve": { "options": { "proxyConfig": "proxy.conf.json" } }</code></pre>
        <p>CORS is a browser rule, fixed by the server's headers (see <a href="#js-cors">CORS</a>). The proxy only works with <code>ng serve</code>; in production use a reverse proxy or CORS headers.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A dev proxy forwards API calls so the browser sees one origin.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= BUILD, TOOLING &AMP; SOURCE MAPS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">34</span>
        <h2>Build, tooling &amp; source maps</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="ng-source-maps" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Build</span>
        <h3>Source maps</h3>
        <p>A <b>source map</b> (<code>.map</code> file) links the minified, bundled JavaScript back to the original TypeScript, so DevTools and error trackers show your real file names and line numbers instead of <code>main.js:1:48213</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>"configurations": {
  "development": { "sourceMap": true, "optimization": false },
  "production":  { "sourceMap": false, "optimization": true }
}</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>Enabled in development by default and turned off in production, because they make the download bigger and expose your source code.</li>
          <li>Options: <code>"sourceMap": { "scripts": true, "styles": true, "hidden": true }</code>. <b>Hidden</b> source maps are generated but not referenced from the bundle, so you can upload them privately to Sentry, or similar tools, to read production errors.</li>
          <li>Browsers download the map only when DevTools is open, so it does not slow normal users.</li>
          <li>Used with <code>source-map-explorer</code> to see what makes the bundle big. See <a href="#check-performance">Checking performance</a>.</li>
          <li>In <code>tsconfig.json</code>, <code>"sourceMap": true</code> makes <code>tsc</code> emit them; see <a href="#ts-strict-config">tsconfig strict options</a>.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Files that map minified production code back to your original TypeScript for debugging.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-builders" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Build</span>
        <h3>Angular build system: esbuild vs. webpack</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>**<code>@angular-devkit/build-angular:browser</code><b> — the older </b>webpack** builder.</li>
          <li>**<code>@angular-devkit/build-angular:application</code><b> — the newer builder based on </b>esbuild<b> (and </b>Vite** for <code>ng serve</code>), the default since Angular 17. It builds much faster, and supports SSR and prerendering in one builder.</li>
          <li><b>Webpack</b> bundles by following imports; <b>esbuild</b> does the same, written in Go, so it is much faster.</li>
          <li>To migrate: <code>ng update @angular/cli --name use-application-builder</code>.</li>
          <li>Output has hashed file names (<code>main-A1B2C3.js</code>) for cache busting, and is split into <b>lazy chunks</b> per lazy route.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> The application builder uses esbuild and Vite for much faster builds than the older webpack builder.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-budgets" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Build</span>
        <h3>Bundle budgets</h3>
        <pre><code>"budgets": [
  { "type": "initial", "maximumWarning": "500kB", "maximumError": "1MB" },
  { "type": "anyComponentStyle", "maximumWarning": "2kB", "maximumError": "4kB" }
]</code></pre>
        <p>When a build exceeds the error limit it fails, which stops a heavy library from slipping into production. Fix it with lazy loading, removing unused imports, importing single functions instead of whole libraries, or analyzing with <code>source-map-explorer</code> or <code>ng build --stats-json</code> plus webpack-bundle-analyzer.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> angular.json can fail or warn the build when a bundle grows too large.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-prod-build" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Build</span>
        <h3>What ng build --configuration production does</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>AOT</b> compilation of templates.</li>
          <li><b>Minification</b> and dead-code removal (<b>tree shaking</b>); see <a href="#tree-shaking">Tree Shaking</a>.</li>
          <li><b>Bundling</b> and code splitting for lazy routes.</li>
          <li><b>Output hashing</b> for cache busting.</li>
          <li><b>Optimized fonts and CSS</b> (inlined critical CSS).</li>
          <li><b>Source maps off</b> and <code>environment</code> file replacement.</li>
          <li>Output goes to <code>dist/&lt;project&gt;/browser</code> and is deployed to any static host (Nginx, S3, Netlify, IIS).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>For a single-page app the server must serve <code>index.html</code> for unknown paths (<code>try_files $uri /index.html;</code> in Nginx).</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> AOT, minification, tree shaking, hashing, and no source maps.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-deploy" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Build</span>
        <h3>Deploying an Angular app</h3>
        <pre><code>server {
  listen 80;
  root /usr/share/nginx/html;
  location / {
    try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;       # deep links like /returns/42 work after refresh
  }
  location ~* \.(js|css|woff2)$ {
    add_header Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable";   # hashed files can be cached for a year
  }
}</code></pre>
        <pre><code>FROM node:20 AS build
WORKDIR /app
COPY . .
RUN npm ci &amp;&amp; npm run build

FROM nginx:alpine
COPY --from=build /app/dist/my-app/browser /usr/share/nginx/html
COPY nginx.conf /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>Do not cache <code>index.html</code>, so users get new deployments at once.</li>
          <li>For a sub-folder deployment: <code>ng build --base-href /erp/</code>.</li>
          <li>A 404 on refresh means the server is missing the rewrite rule.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Build, serve the static files, rewrite all routes to index.html, and set the base href.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-config-files" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Build</span>
        <h3>Runtime config &amp; multiple environments</h3>
        <p><code>environment.ts</code> values are replaced during the build, so one build per environment. To use <b>one build everywhere</b>, load a JSON file when the app starts:</p>
        <pre><code>// app.config.ts
provideAppInitializer(() =&gt; {
  const http = inject(HttpClient);
  const config = inject(ConfigService);
  return firstValueFrom(http.get&lt;AppConfig&gt;('assets/config.json')).then(c =&gt; config.set(c));
})</code></pre>
        <p>The older form is the <code>APP_INITIALIZER</code> token with <code>multi: true</code>. Angular waits for the Promise or Observable before the app starts, so the config is ready for every component. See <a href="#environment-files">environment.ts files</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Environment files are baked in at build time; load config.json at startup for true runtime config.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-lint-format" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Tooling</span>
        <h3>ESLint, Prettier &amp; code quality</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>ESLint</b> (<code>ng add @angular-eslint/schematics</code>, then <code>ng lint</code>) finds mistakes and bad patterns. TSLint is retired.</li>
          <li><b>Prettier</b> formats code, so reviews are about logic and not spacing.</li>
          <li><b>Husky</b> and <b>lint-staged</b> run both on <code>git commit</code>.</li>
          <li><b>Commit conventions:</b> Conventional Commits (<code>feat:</code>, <code>fix:</code>).</li>
          <li><b>CI:</b> run <code>lint</code>, <code>test --watch=false</code>, and <code>build</code> on each pull request.</li>
          <li>Enable <code>strict</code> mode in <code>tsconfig.json</code> and <code>strictTemplates</code> in Angular; see <a href="#ts-strict-config">tsconfig strict options</a>.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Lint for mistakes, format for style, and enforce both before commits.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-update-upgrade" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Tooling</span>
        <h3>Upgrading Angular versions</h3>
        <pre><code>ng update @angular/core@18 @angular/cli@18
ng update @angular/material@18</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>Upgrade <b>one major version at a time</b>; commit a clean git state first.</li>
          <li>Use update.angular.dev for the checklist between two versions.</li>
          <li>Migrations run automatically (for example to standalone, the new control flow, and the application builder).</li>
          <li>Check that third-party libraries support the new version.</li>
          <li>Angular releases a major version about every 6 months, and gives long-term support to each.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Use ng update one major version at a time, and read the update guide.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-schematics" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Tooling</span>
        <h3>Schematics &amp; ng generate</h3>
        <pre><code>ng generate component orders/order-list --standalone --change-detection OnPush
ng generate service core/auth
ng generate guard core/auth --functional
ng generate pipe shared/rupees
ng generate interface models/order
ng add @angular/material</code></pre>
        <p>Schematics are scripts that create or modify project files; <code>ng add</code> runs the schematic of a package to install and configure it. Teams write their own to generate feature folders in the project's standard layout. Set defaults in <code>angular.json</code> under <code>schematics</code>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Code generators that create files and edit existing ones.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-material-cdk" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">UI libraries</span>
        <h3>Angular Material &amp; CDK</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Angular Material</b> — buttons, forms, tables, dialogs, snackbars, date pickers, and theming.</li>
          <li><b>Angular CDK</b> — unstyled building blocks for your own UI: <code>Overlay</code>, <code>DragDrop</code>, <code>Clipboard</code>, <code>A11y</code> (focus trap, live announcer), <code>Portal</code>, <code>Table</code>, and <code>Virtual Scrolling</code>.</li>
          <li>Alternatives: PrimeNG, NG-ZORRO, Tailwind with Headless UI, Bootstrap with ng-bootstrap.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Material dialog example:</p>
        <pre><code>const ref = this.dialog.open(ConfirmComponent, { data: { text: 'Delete this challan?' }, width: '400px' });
ref.afterClosed().subscribe(ok =&gt; ok &amp;&amp; this.delete());</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Material gives ready components; the CDK gives behaviours like overlay, drag-drop, and virtual scroll.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-virtual-scroll" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Performance</span>
        <h3>Virtual scrolling</h3>
        <pre><code>&lt;cdk-virtual-scroll-viewport itemSize="48" class="viewport"&gt;
  &lt;div *cdkVirtualFor="let row of rows" class="row"&gt;{{ row.name }}&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/cdk-virtual-scroll-viewport&gt;</code></pre>
        <pre><code>.viewport { height: 400px; }</code></pre>
        <p>Only the visible items, plus a small buffer, exist in the DOM. Other large-list tools: server-side pagination, <code>trackBy</code>, <code>OnPush</code>, and <code>@defer</code>. Compare with <a href="#trackby">trackBy</a> and <a href="#optimize-performance">Optimizing performance</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Render only the rows visible on screen, so a list of 100,000 rows stays fast.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-i18n-pwa" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Features</span>
        <h3>i18n &amp; PWA</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>i18n:</b> mark text with the <code>i18n</code> attribute, run <code>ng extract-i18n</code>, translate the XLF file, and build one app per language. Libraries like <b>ngx-translate</b> and <b>Transloco</b> switch language at runtime instead.</li>
          <li><b>PWA:</b> <code>ng add @angular/pwa</code> adds a web manifest and the <b>service worker</b> (<code>ngsw-config.json</code>) that caches the app shell for offline use and enables install-to-home-screen. <code>SwUpdate</code> tells you when a new version is available.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>&lt;h1 i18n="@@title"&gt;Welcome&lt;/h1&gt;</code></pre>
        <p>Dates, numbers and currencies use the <code>LOCALE_ID</code>; for Indian formats register <code>en-IN</code> and use <code>provide: LOCALE_ID, useValue: 'en-IN'</code>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Translate the UI at build time, and add offline support with a service worker.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= ARCHITECTURE, STATE &AMP; TESTING ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">35</span>
        <h2>Architecture, state &amp; testing</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="ng-module-structure" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Architecture</span>
        <h3>Core, shared &amp; feature structure</h3>
        <pre><code class="plain">src/app/
  core/        singleton services, interceptors, guards, layout (loaded once)
  shared/      reusable components, pipes, directives (imported wherever needed)
  features/
    orders/    its own routes, pages, components, services (lazy loaded)
    returns/
  app.config.ts
  app.routes.ts</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>Features should not import each other; share through <code>shared</code> or <code>core</code>.</li>
          <li>Keep smart components (pages) in the feature, and dumb components in <code>shared</code> if reused. See <a href="#smart-dumb-components">Smart vs. Dumb components</a>.</li>
          <li>With standalone components, a "module" is just a folder plus a routes file.</li>
          <li><code>forRoot()</code> / <code>forChild()</code> (a <code>ModuleWithProviders</code>) let a module provide singletons once at root and routes or config in children; standalone code uses <code>provideX()</code> functions instead.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Singletons in core, reusable pieces in shared, and one folder per feature.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-facade-pattern" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Architecture</span>
        <h3>Facade &amp; service-layer pattern</h3>
        <pre><code>@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class OrdersFacade {
  private api = inject(OrdersApi);
  private state = signal&lt;Order[]&gt;([]);
  readonly orders = this.state.asReadonly();
  readonly total = computed(() =&gt; this.state().reduce((s, o) =&gt; s + o.amount, 0));

  load() { this.api.getAll().subscribe(o =&gt; this.state.set(o)); }
  add(o: Order) { this.api.create(o).subscribe(saved =&gt; this.state.update(list =&gt; [...list, saved])); }
}</code></pre>
        <p>Components talk only to the facade, and never to HTTP or the store directly. This makes the data source easy to replace and the components easy to test.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A facade service hides several services and state behind one simple API.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-ngrx-concepts" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">State management</span>
        <h3>NgRx: actions, reducers, effects &amp; selectors</h3>
        <pre><code>export const loadOrders = createAction('[Orders] Load');
export const loadOrdersSuccess = createAction('[Orders] Load Success', props&lt;{ orders: Order[] }&gt;());

export const reducer = createReducer(
  { orders: [], loading: false },
  on(loadOrders, s =&gt; ({ ...s, loading: true })),
  on(loadOrdersSuccess, (s, { orders }) =&gt; ({ ...s, orders, loading: false }))
);

loadOrders$ = createEffect(() =&gt; this.actions$.pipe(
  ofType(loadOrders),
  switchMap(() =&gt; this.api.getAll().pipe(
    map(orders =&gt; loadOrdersSuccess({ orders })),
    catchError(() =&gt; of(loadOrdersFailure()))
  ))
));

export const selectOrders = createSelector(selectOrdersState, s =&gt; s.orders);</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Store</b> — the single state object. <b>Action</b> — what happened. <b>Reducer</b> — a pure function that returns new state. <b>Effect</b> — async side effects (API calls). <b>Selector</b> — a memoized read of part of the state.</li>
          <li>Use it for large apps with a lot of shared state; for small apps a service with signals is enough. NgRx also has a lighter <b>SignalStore</b> and <b>ComponentStore</b>. See <a href="#state-management">State Management</a> and <a href="#ngrx">NgRx</a>.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> One-way flow: dispatch an action, a reducer makes the new state, effects handle side effects.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-microfrontends" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Architecture</span>
        <h3>Micro frontends &amp; monorepos</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Micro frontends:</b> separate teams build and deploy parts of the UI independently. In Angular, this is usually done with <b>Module Federation</b> (<code>@angular-architects/native-federation</code>), where a shell app loads remote apps at runtime.</li>
          <li><b>Monorepo:</b> one repository with many apps and shared libraries, managed by <b>Nx</b> or the Angular CLI workspace (<code>ng generate library</code>). Benefits are shared code, one version of dependencies, and affected-only builds and tests.</li>
          <li>Trade-offs: more complexity and shared-dependency version issues, so only use micro frontends when team size and deployment independence need them.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Split a big UI into independently deployed apps, or keep many apps and libraries in one repo.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-error-handler" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Error handling</span>
        <h3>Global ErrorHandler</h3>
        <pre><code>@Injectable()
export class GlobalErrorHandler implements ErrorHandler {
  private logger = inject(LoggerService);
  handleError(error: unknown) {
    this.logger.send(error);              // send to Sentry or your backend
    console.error(error);
  }
}

// app.config.ts
{ provide: ErrorHandler, useClass: GlobalErrorHandler }</code></pre>
        <p>It catches errors in templates, lifecycle hooks, and event handlers; HTTP errors are best handled in an <a href="#ng-functional-interceptor">interceptor</a>, and unhandled promise rejections also reach it through zone.js. See <a href="#api-error-handling">Handling API errors</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Catch every unhandled error in one place, to log and show a message.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-title-meta" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Services</span>
        <h3>Title &amp; Meta services</h3>
        <pre><code>constructor(private title: Title, private meta: Meta) {}

ngOnInit() {
  this.title.setTitle('Return 24Q - TDS Portal');
  this.meta.updateTag({ name: 'description', content: 'Quarterly TDS return summary' });
}</code></pre>
        <p>For route-level titles, use the <code>title</code> property of a route. Works with <a href="#ssr">SSR</a>, so crawlers see the tags.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Change the page title and meta tags from code.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-angular-elements" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Features</span>
        <h3>Angular Elements</h3>
        <pre><code>const el = createCustomElement(WidgetComponent, { injector: this.injector });
customElements.define('tds-widget', el);</code></pre>
        <pre><code>&lt;tds-widget user-id="42"&gt;&lt;/tds-widget&gt;</code></pre>
        <p>Used to embed an Angular widget in a non-Angular site (a CMS or a legacy page), or to share components between frameworks. See <a href="#html-web-components">Web Components</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Package a component as a standard Web Component for use in any page.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-test-async" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Testing</span>
        <h3>Testing async code: fakeAsync &amp; waitForAsync</h3>
        <pre><code>it('debounces the search', fakeAsync(() =&gt; {
  component.search.setValue('tds');
  tick(300);                                   // move the fake clock forward 300 ms
  expect(api.search).toHaveBeenCalledWith('tds');
}));

it('loads data', waitForAsync(() =&gt; {
  fixture.detectChanges();
  fixture.whenStable().then(() =&gt; {
    expect(component.orders.length).toBe(2);
  });
}));</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li><code>fakeAsync</code> + <code>tick()</code> / <code>flush()</code> for timers, debounce, and promises.</li>
          <li><code>waitForAsync</code> for real async work.</li>
          <li>Newer test runners (Jest, Vitest, Web Test Runner) are common alternatives to Karma.</li>
          <li>E2E tests: <b>Cypress</b> or <b>Playwright</b>. See <a href="#testbed">TestBed</a>.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Control time in tests with tick(), and wait for promises to settle.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-test-tools" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Testing</span>
        <h3>Karma, Jasmine, Jest &amp; coverage</h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th></th><th>Jasmine + Karma</th><th>Jest</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Runs in</td><td>a real browser</td><td>Node with jsdom</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Speed</td><td>slower</td><td>faster</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Mocks</td><td><code>jasmine.createSpy</code></td><td><code>jest.fn()</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Default in Angular</td><td>yes, for years</td><td>optional</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <pre><code>ng test --code-coverage --watch=false</code></pre>
        <p>Coverage shows which lines your tests run; a common target is 70–80%. Test behavior, not private methods, and mock HTTP with <code>HttpTestingController</code>. See <a href="#mocking-spies">Mocking with spies</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Jasmine is the assertion library, Karma the browser runner; Jest runs without a browser.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="ng-debugging" data-cat="angular">
        <span class="eyebrow">Debugging</span>
        <h3>Debugging an Angular app</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Open DevTools, go to <b>Sources</b>, find your <code>.ts</code> file thanks to <a href="#ng-source-maps">source maps</a>, and set breakpoints.</li>
          <li>Use <code>debugger;</code> for a breakpoint from code.</li>
          <li><b>Angular DevTools</b> shows the component tree, inputs, and change detection profile.</li>
          <li>In development, <code>ng.getComponent($0)</code> in the console returns the component of the selected element.</li>
          <li>Use <code>tap(console.log)</code> inside an RxJS <code>pipe</code> to see a stream's values; see <a href="#tap">tap</a>.</li>
          <li>The <b>Network</b> tab shows failed calls, and <b>Performance</b> shows slow rendering.</li>
          <li>VS Code can attach the Chrome debugger with a <code>launch.json</code> configuration.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Source maps, breakpoints, Angular DevTools, and the ng global.</div>
