    <section class="part">
<div class="chapter-divider"><span class="chapter-label">Part N · Engineering essentials</span><div class="chapter-title">Engineering essentials</div><p class="chapter-dek">The general knowledge interviewers expect at 3 to 5 years: HTTP, Git, tooling, principles, quality, and how a team ships software.</p></div><div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">82</span><h2>HTTP, REST &amp; APIs</h2></div>

<article id="eng-http-methods" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What are the HTTP methods, and which are safe or idempotent?</h3>
<p>A method tells the server what you want to do with a resource. Safe means it does not change data. Idempotent means calling it many times gives the same final state as calling it once.</p>
<ul>
<li>GET, HEAD, OPTIONS: safe and idempotent.</li>
<li>PUT and DELETE: idempotent, not safe.</li>
<li>POST: neither. Two POSTs can create two items.</li>
<li>PATCH: usually not idempotent (for example "add 1 to count").</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>PUT    /users/7  { "name": "Ana" }   // same result if retried
DELETE /users/7                      // 2nd call: 404 or 204, state is same
POST   /orders   { ... }             // retry may create a duplicate</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Safe = no change; idempotent = repeat is harmless; GET/PUT/DELETE can be retried, POST cannot without a key.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-status-codes" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>Which HTTP status codes must you know?</h3>
<p>Families: 1xx info, 2xx success, 3xx redirect, 4xx client error, 5xx server error. Learn the common ones and what the UI should do.</p>
<ul>
<li>200 OK, 201 Created (after POST), 204 No Content (success, empty body).</li>
<li>301 permanent redirect, 304 Not Modified (use your cache).</li>
<li>400 bad request, 401 not logged in, 403 logged in but not allowed, 404 not found.</li>
<li>409 conflict (duplicate, version clash), 422 validation failed, 429 too many requests.</li>
<li>500 server bug, 502 bad gateway, 503 unavailable (retry later).</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>// Angular: react by code
if (err.status === 401) this.auth.logout();
else if (err.status === 422) this.showFieldErrors(err.error.errors);
else if (err.status &gt;= 500) this.toast.error('Try again later');</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> 401 = who are you, 403 = not allowed, 422 = invalid data, 429 = slow down, 5xx = server problem, safe to retry GETs.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-rest-design" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What are REST principles and good URL design?</h3>
<p>REST models things as resources with URLs, and uses HTTP methods as the verbs. It is stateless: each request carries everything the server needs.</p>
<ul>
<li>Use plural nouns, not verbs: <code>/users</code>, not <code>/getUsers</code>.</li>
<li>Nest only for clear ownership: <code>/users/7/orders</code>.</li>
<li>Use the right method and status code.</li>
<li>Return the same JSON shape every time, errors included.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>GET    /users          list
GET    /users/7        one
POST   /users          create (201)
PATCH  /users/7        partial update
DELETE /users/7        remove (204)</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> REST = resources as nouns, HTTP methods as verbs, stateless requests, correct status codes.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-rest-graphql-grpc" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>REST vs GraphQL vs gRPC: when to choose which?</h3>
<p>All three move data between systems, with different trade-offs.</p>
<ul>
<li>REST: simple, cacheable with HTTP, best default for public and CRUD APIs. Can over-fetch or need many calls.</li>
<li>GraphQL: client asks for exact fields in one request. Good for many screens and mobile. Harder caching and server complexity.</li>
<li>gRPC: binary, fast, typed contracts. Great service to service; browsers need a proxy (grpc-web).</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>query { user(id: 7) { name orders { id total } } }  # one call, only needed fields</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> REST for simple and cacheable, GraphQL for flexible client queries, gRPC for fast internal service calls.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-http-versions" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 vs HTTP/3: what changes for a frontend developer?</h3>
<p>Newer versions send requests faster over the same connection.</p>
<ul>
<li>HTTP/1.1: about 6 connections per host, one request at a time each (head-of-line blocking). Old tricks: bundling, sprites, domain sharding.</li>
<li>HTTP/2: one connection, many parallel streams, header compression. Many small files are fine.</li>
<li>HTTP/3: runs on QUIC (UDP). Faster connect, no TCP blocking, better on bad mobile networks.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>Protocol column in DevTools Network: http/1.1, h2, h3</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> HTTP/2 multiplexes requests on one connection, HTTP/3 uses QUIC; sprites and domain sharding are no longer needed.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-http-caching" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>How does HTTP caching work (Cache-Control, ETag, stale-while-revalidate)?</h3>
<p>The server sends headers that tell the browser how long to reuse a response and how to check if it changed. See also <a href="#scn-caching-strategy">the caching strategy scenario</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li><code>Cache-Control: max-age=31536000, immutable</code> for hashed JS/CSS files.</li>
<li><code>no-cache</code> means "ask me before reuse"; <code>no-store</code> means never save.</li>
<li><code>ETag</code> plus <code>If-None-Match</code>: the server answers 304 if unchanged.</li>
<li><code>stale-while-revalidate</code>: show old copy now, refresh in background.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>index.html     -&gt; Cache-Control: no-cache
main.a1b2c3.js -&gt; Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
api/list       -&gt; ETag: "v42"   (next call: If-None-Match: "v42" -&gt; 304)</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Cache hashed assets forever, never cache index.html, and use ETag with 304 for data that may change.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-authn-authz" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What is the difference between authentication and authorization?</h3>
<p>Authentication (authn) proves who you are. Authorization (authz) decides what you may do. See <a href="#role-based-access">role based access</a> for the Angular side.</p>
<ul>
<li>Authn failure: 401. Authz failure: 403.</li>
<li>Frontend guards and hidden buttons are only UX. The server must enforce rules.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>Login with password     -&gt; authentication
Role "admin" can delete -&gt; authorization</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Authn = who are you (401), authz = what can you do (403); the real check always lives on the server.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-session-jwt-oauth" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>Session cookie vs JWT vs OAuth2/OIDC: how do they differ?</h3>
<p>They solve different parts of login.</p>
<ul>
<li>Session cookie: server stores the session, browser sends an HttpOnly cookie. Easy to revoke. See <a href="#js-cookies">cookies</a>.</li>
<li>JWT: signed token with claims, server needs no lookup. Hard to revoke; keep it short-lived. Refresh flow: <a href="#ng-jwt-refresh">JWT refresh</a>.</li>
<li>OAuth2: delegated access ("log in with Google"). OIDC adds identity (ID token) on top.</li>
<li>Access token: short life, sent to APIs. Refresh token: long life, only used to get new access tokens.</li>
<li>SPAs use Authorization Code flow with PKCE: a one-time code verifier proves the same app started the flow.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>GET /api/me
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOi...   // access token</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Cookie = server session, JWT = stateless token, OAuth2/OIDC = delegated login; SPAs use code flow with PKCE, never implicit.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-api-pagination" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What are common API conventions for pagination, filtering and sorting?</h3>
<p>Use query parameters, and never return unlimited lists.</p>
<ul>
<li>Offset: <code>?page=2&amp;limit=20</code>. Simple, but slow and unstable on big or changing data.</li>
<li>Cursor: <code>?after=abc123&amp;limit=20</code>. Stable and fast, good for infinite scroll.</li>
<li>Filter: <code>?status=active</code>. Sort: <code>?sort=-createdAt</code>.</li>
<li>Return total or <code>nextCursor</code> in the response.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>GET /orders?status=paid&amp;sort=-createdAt&amp;limit=20&amp;after=ord_91
{ "items": [...], "nextCursor": "ord_71" }</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Paginate every list; cursor pagination beats offset for large or live data.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-api-versioning" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>How do you handle API versioning and backward compatibility?</h3>
<p>Old clients keep working while the API grows. See also <a href="#scn-api-contract-change">API contract change</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Safe changes: add optional fields, add endpoints.</li>
<li>Breaking changes: remove or rename fields, change types, make a field required.</li>
<li>Version in URL (<code>/v2/users</code>) or header. Keep the old version until clients move.</li>
<li>Frontend: be tolerant, ignore unknown fields, map DTOs to your own models.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>GET /v1/users/7   { "name": "Ana" }
GET /v2/users/7   { "firstName": "Ana", "lastName": "Li" }  // breaking, new version</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Add, do not break; ship breaking changes under a new version and give clients time to migrate.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-rate-limit-429" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What is rate limiting and how do you handle a 429 in the frontend?</h3>
<p>The server limits requests per user or IP. When you exceed it, you get 429, often with a <code>Retry-After</code> header.</p>
<ul>
<li>Wait for Retry-After, then retry; use exponential backoff with jitter.</li>
<li>Reduce calls: debounce search, cache, batch.</li>
<li>Show a calm message, do not retry in a tight loop.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>source$.pipe(
  retry({ count: 3, delay: (e, n) =&gt;
    e.status === 429 ? timer(2 ** n * 1000 + Math.random() * 300) : throwError(() =&gt; e) })
)</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> On 429 honor Retry-After, back off with jitter, and cut needless requests.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-idempotency-key" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What is an idempotency key and why use it?</h3>
<p>It is a unique ID the client sends with a POST. If the same key arrives again (retry, double click), the server returns the first result instead of repeating the action. Related: <a href="#scn-double-submit">double submit</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Vital for payments and orders.</li>
<li>Generate the key once per user action, reuse it on retries.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>const key = crypto.randomUUID();
this.http.post('/payments', body, { headers: { 'Idempotency-Key': key } });</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> An idempotency key makes a POST safe to retry because the server treats the same key as the same request.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-websocket-handshake" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>How does a WebSocket connection start?</h3>
<p>It begins as a normal HTTP request asking to upgrade. After the server agrees, the same connection becomes a two-way channel. More in <a href="#js-websocket-sse">WebSocket and SSE</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Status 101 means the upgrade worked.</li>
<li>Browsers cannot set custom headers, so send auth by cookie, query token or first message.</li>
<li>Handle reconnect and heartbeat yourself.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>GET /chat HTTP/1.1
Upgrade: websocket
Connection: Upgrade
Sec-WebSocket-Key: x3JJ...

HTTP/1.1 101 Switching Protocols</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> WebSocket = HTTP Upgrade, 101 response, then full-duplex messages; you must code reconnects.</div>
</article>
</section>

    <section class="part">
<div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">83</span><h2>Git &amp; team workflow</h2></div>

<article id="eng-merge-rebase-squash" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>Merge vs rebase vs squash: what is the difference?</h3>
<p>All three combine work from one branch into another, with different history.</p>
<ul>
<li>Merge: keeps all commits and adds a merge commit. Safe, history is true but busy.</li>
<li>Rebase: replays your commits on top of the target. Linear history. Rewrites commits.</li>
<li>Squash: turns all commits into one. Clean main, loses small steps.</li>
<li>Rule: never rebase a branch others already use.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>git switch feature
git rebase main          # update my branch
git push --force-with-lease</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Rebase your own branch to stay current, squash or merge into main, and never rewrite shared history.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-git-commands" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>Which Git commands help in real life (stash, cherry-pick, reset, revert, reflog, bisect)?</h3>
<p>Know when to use each one.</p>
<ul>
<li><code>stash</code>: park unfinished work to switch branches.</li>
<li><code>cherry-pick &lt;sha&gt;</code>: copy one commit to another branch (hotfix).</li>
<li><code>reset --soft</code>: undo commit, keep changes staged. <code>--mixed</code>: keep changes unstaged. <code>--hard</code>: delete changes.</li>
<li><code>revert &lt;sha&gt;</code>: new commit that undoes an old one; safe on shared branches.</li>
<li><code>reflog</code>: history of where HEAD was; rescues lost commits.</li>
<li><code>bisect</code>: binary search for the commit that broke something.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>git stash push -m "wip"
git bisect start; git bisect bad; git bisect good v1.2.0</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Reset rewrites your local history, revert is safe on shared branches, reflog saves you after mistakes.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-merge-conflicts" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>How do you resolve a merge conflict?</h3>
<p>A conflict means two branches changed the same lines. Git cannot choose, so you do. Prevent them with small PRs and frequent updates from main.</p>
<ol>
<li>Run <code>git status</code> to see conflicted files.</li>
<li>Open the file, find the markers, and keep the right combination.</li>
<li>Remove <code>&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;</code>, <code>=======</code>, <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>.</li>
<li>Build and run tests, then <code>git add</code> and continue (<code>merge --continue</code> or <code>rebase --continue</code>).</li>
<li>Ask the other author when you are unsure.</li>
</ol>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Understand both changes, merge by hand, run tests, and keep branches short so conflicts stay small.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-branching-prs" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>GitFlow vs trunk-based development, and what makes a good PR?</h3>
<p>The branching model decides how long code lives outside main.</p>
<ul>
<li>GitFlow: develop, release and hotfix branches. Fits planned releases, but long branches cause pain.</li>
<li>Trunk-based: short branches into main, often with feature flags. Fits CI/CD and fast teams.</li>
<li>Good PR: small (under about 400 lines), one purpose, passing CI, screenshots for UI.</li>
<li>Description: what, why, how to test, linked ticket, risks. See <a href="#scn-code-review">code review</a>.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>## What / Why
Add coupon field to cart (JIRA-123)
## How to test
Cart page, enter SAVE10, total drops 10%
## Risk
Touches price calculation</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Prefer short-lived branches and small focused PRs with a clear what, why and how to test.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-semver-conventional" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What are semantic versioning and conventional commits?</h3>
<p>SemVer is MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH. Conventional commits are message prefixes that tools can read to pick the version and write the changelog.</p>
<ul>
<li>PATCH: bug fix. MINOR: new feature, compatible. MAJOR: breaking change.</li>
<li><code>fix:</code> gives patch, <code>feat:</code> gives minor, <code>feat!:</code> or BREAKING CHANGE gives major.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>feat(cart): add coupon field
fix(login): trim email before submit
1.4.2 -&gt; after a feat -&gt; 1.5.0</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> SemVer says what kind of change a version holds; conventional commits let tools automate versions and changelogs.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-scrum-role" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What are Scrum ceremonies and what is your role as a developer?</h3>
<p>Scrum works in sprints. Each ceremony has a goal, and developers take an active part.</p>
<ul>
<li>Sprint: fixed period (1 to 3 weeks) that delivers a working increment.</li>
<li>Refinement: clarify and split stories; ask questions about edge cases.</li>
<li>Planning: pick stories the team can finish. Story points show relative effort, not hours.</li>
<li>Daily: what I did, what I will do, blockers.</li>
<li>Review: demo to stakeholders. Retro: improve the process.</li>
<li>Definition of Done: code reviewed, tested, merged, deployed to test, docs updated.</li>
</ul>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> I refine stories early, estimate honestly, raise blockers daily, and call a story done only by the Definition of Done.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-jira-estimation" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>How do you keep Jira tickets clean and estimate work?</h3>
<p>Good tickets save meetings. Good estimates build trust. See also <a href="#scn-estimate-deadline">deadline estimation</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Keep status true: move the card when work really changes.</li>
<li>Write acceptance criteria; link PR, design and parent epic.</li>
<li>Split big stories; one story should fit in a few days.</li>
<li>Estimate by comparing with a past story; add buffer for review, tests, unknowns.</li>
<li>Give ranges and state assumptions; update early when the estimate changes.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>Estimate: 3 to 5 days
Assumes: API is ready, design is final
Risk: unclear validation rules</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Clean tickets have criteria, links and honest status; estimates are ranges with stated assumptions.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-ci-cd-stages" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What stages does a CI/CD pipeline have?</h3>
<p>CI checks every change automatically. CD delivers it to an environment.</p>
<ol>
<li>Install and cache dependencies.</li>
<li>Lint and format (<a href="#ng-lint-format">lint setup</a>).</li>
<li>Unit tests with coverage.</li>
<li>Build with budgets (<a href="#ng-budgets">budgets</a>).</li>
<li>Scan: <code>npm audit</code>, secrets, licenses.</li>
<li>E2E tests, deploy to an environment.</li>
<li>Smoke test, then promote or roll back.</li>
</ol>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Pipeline = lint, test, build, scan, deploy, verify, with a fast and tested rollback.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-environments-deploys" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What are environments, blue-green, canary and feature flags?</h3>
<p>They are ways to move code to users with less risk.</p>
<ul>
<li>Environments: dev (developers), QA (testers), UAT (business sign-off), prod (users). See <a href="#scn-multi-env">multi-env config</a>.</li>
<li>Blue-green: two identical prod copies; switch traffic to the new one, switch back to roll back.</li>
<li>Canary: send 5% of users to the new version, watch errors, then grow.</li>
<li>Feature flag: ship code turned off, enable per user or percent; deploy is not release.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>if (this.flags.isOn('new-checkout')) { /* new UI */ } else { /* old UI */ }</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Blue-green and canary cut deploy risk; feature flags separate deploying code from releasing it.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-docker-frontend" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>How do you use Docker for an Angular app?</h3>
<p>Use a multi-stage build: build with Node, serve static files with nginx. The final image has no Node or source.</p>
<pre><code>FROM node:20 AS build
WORKDIR /app
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm ci
COPY . .
RUN npm run build

FROM nginx:alpine
COPY --from=build /app/dist/app/browser /usr/share/nginx/html
COPY nginx.conf /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>nginx needs <code>try_files $uri /index.html</code> for SPA routes.</li>
<li>Load config at runtime (env file), not baked into the image.</li>
</ul>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Multi-stage Docker: Node builds, nginx serves a small image, and the same image runs in every environment.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-monitoring-logging" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>How do you monitor and log a frontend in production?</h3>
<p>You cannot fix what you cannot see. Collect errors, timings and logs from real users.</p>
<ul>
<li>Error tracking (Sentry): unhandled errors with stack trace, release and user context. Upload <a href="#ng-source-maps">source maps</a>.</li>
<li>RUM: real user timings and Core Web Vitals from real devices.</li>
<li>Logs: structured, with a correlation ID sent to the API; never log tokens or personal data.</li>
<li>Alerts: on error rate spikes and slow vitals, with an owner, not on every error.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>@Injectable()
export class AppErrorHandler implements ErrorHandler {
  handleError(e: unknown) { Sentry.captureException(e); }
}</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Track errors with source maps, measure real users, log safely, and alert on trends someone owns.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-production-ready" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What does "production ready" mean?</h3>
<p>It means the feature works, and also that you can run, watch and fix it safely.</p>
<ul>
<li>Meets acceptance criteria; reviewed and merged.</li>
<li>Tests pass; error, empty and loading states handled.</li>
<li>Accessible, secure, within performance budgets.</li>
<li>Monitoring, logs and a rollback or feature flag exist.</li>
<li>Docs updated; config per environment; no console errors.</li>
</ul>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Production ready = tested, secure, accessible, observable and reversible, not just "works on my machine".</div>
</article>
</section>

    <section class="part">
<div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">84</span><h2>Principles, patterns &amp; quality</h2></div>

<article id="eng-solid" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What is SOLID, with Angular examples?</h3>
<p>Five rules that keep classes easy to change.</p>
<ul>
<li>S, single responsibility: a component renders; a service fetches data.</li>
<li>O, open/closed: extend by a new directive or strategy, not by editing a long if chain.</li>
<li>L, Liskov: a subclass or implementation can replace its base without surprises.</li>
<li>I, interface segregation: small interfaces, not one huge service type.</li>
<li>D, dependency inversion: depend on an abstraction via a DI token, swap it in tests.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>export abstract class Logger { abstract log(m: string): void; }
providers: [{ provide: Logger, useClass: ConsoleLogger }]</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> SOLID = small focused classes, extend without editing, and depend on abstractions that Angular DI can swap.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-dry-kiss-yagni" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What do DRY, KISS and YAGNI mean, and why composition over inheritance?</h3>
<p>Simple habits that stop over-engineering.</p>
<ul>
<li>DRY: one source of truth for each piece of knowledge. But do not merge code that only looks alike.</li>
<li>KISS: choose the simplest solution that works.</li>
<li>YAGNI: do not build for needs you only imagine.</li>
<li>Composition over inheritance: build behavior from small parts (services, directives, pipes) instead of deep class trees. See <a href="#js-mixins-composition">mixins and composition</a>.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>// composition: inject what you need
export class CartComponent { private pricing = inject(PricingService); }</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Keep it simple, build only what is needed, avoid wrong duplication, and compose instead of inheriting.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-design-patterns" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>Which design patterns appear in Angular apps?</h3>
<p>One example for each common pattern.</p>
<ul>
<li>Singleton: <code>providedIn: 'root'</code> service has one instance.</li>
<li>Observer: RxJS Observable and subscribe.</li>
<li>Facade: a store service hiding HTTP and state from components.</li>
<li>Adapter: map API DTOs to view models.</li>
<li>Strategy: inject a different pricing or validator class by token.</li>
<li>Factory: a <code>useFactory</code> provider creates a service from config.</li>
<li>Decorator: <code>@Component</code>, or an HTTP interceptor wrapping requests.</li>
<li>Mediator: a shared service or store so components talk without knowing each other.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>providers: [{ provide: API_URL, useFactory: () =&gt; env.production ? '/api' : 'http://localhost:3000' }]</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Angular already gives singleton, observer, decorator and factory; you add facade, adapter and strategy yourself.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-clean-code" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What is clean code: naming and small functions?</h3>
<p>Code is read far more often than written, so write for the next reader.</p>
<ul>
<li>Names say intent: <code>isOrderPaid</code>, not <code>flag2</code>.</li>
<li>Functions do one thing, take few parameters, and have no hidden side effects.</li>
<li>Return early instead of deep nesting.</li>
<li>Comments explain why, not what.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>// before
if (u &amp;&amp; u.a &amp;&amp; u.a.r === 'x') { ... }
// after
const canEdit = (user?: User) =&gt; user?.role === 'editor';</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Clean code reads like prose: clear names, small single-purpose functions, early returns.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-layered-architecture" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What is separation of concerns and layered architecture in a frontend?</h3>
<p>Split code by job so each part changes for one reason.</p>
<ul>
<li>UI layer: components and templates, only display and events.</li>
<li>State layer: store or signals, holds app state and rules.</li>
<li>Data access: services that call APIs and map data.</li>
<li>Dependencies point one way: UI to state to data, never back. See <a href="#scn-state-choice">state choice</a>.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>OrdersPage (UI) -&gt; OrdersStore (state) -&gt; OrdersApi (HTTP)</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Keep UI, state and data access apart so you can test and change each without touching the others.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-test-pyramid" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What is the testing pyramid, and what are TDD and BDD?</h3>
<p>Write most tests where they are fast and cheap. Angular tools: <a href="#testbed">TestBed</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Many unit tests, fewer integration/component tests, few E2E tests (slow, flaky).</li>
<li>Test behavior users see, not private details. Test logic, edge cases, error paths; skip trivial getters.</li>
<li>TDD: write a failing test, make it pass, refactor.</li>
<li>BDD: describe behavior as Given / When / Then, shared with business people.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>it('shows error when email is empty', () =&gt; {
  // Given an empty form, When I submit, Then the error message appears
});</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Many fast unit tests, some integration, few E2E; TDD is red-green-refactor, BDD is Given-When-Then.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-mock-stub-spy" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>Mocking vs stubbing vs spying, and what does code coverage mean?</h3>
<p>They are test doubles that replace real dependencies.</p>
<ul>
<li>Stub: returns fixed data so the test is predictable.</li>
<li>Mock: a fake with expectations; you check it was called correctly.</li>
<li>Spy: wraps a real function and records calls.</li>
<li>Coverage: percent of lines or branches run by tests. It shows untested code, not good tests. 100% can still miss bugs.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>const api = jasmine.createSpyObj('Api', ['load']);
api.load.and.returnValue(of([{ id: 1 }]));  // stub
expect(api.load).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);  // spy/mock check</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Stubs feed data, spies and mocks verify calls, and coverage finds gaps but never proves quality.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-tech-debt" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What is technical debt and how do you handle refactoring?</h3>
<p>Tech debt is a shortcut that makes future change slower. Like money debt, it has interest. See <a href="#scn-legacy-refactor">legacy refactor</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Track it as tickets with impact, not as complaints.</li>
<li>Refactor in small steps, with tests, near code you already change (boy scout rule).</li>
<li>Do not mix big refactors with feature changes in one PR.</li>
<li>Reserve some capacity each sprint.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>Ticket: OrdersService has 900 lines
Impact: every change takes 2x longer, 3 bugs last month
Plan: extract PricingService first, add tests, then move on</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Make debt visible, pay it in small tested steps, and keep refactor PRs separate from features.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-performance-mindset" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What is a performance mindset?</h3>
<p>Measure first, then fix the biggest problem. Guessing wastes time.</p>
<ul>
<li>Set budgets (<a href="#ng-budgets">bundle budgets</a>) and fail CI when exceeded.</li>
<li>Track <a href="#html-core-web-vitals">Core Web Vitals</a> (LCP, INP, CLS) on real users.</li>
<li>Know the <a href="#html-critical-rendering-path">critical rendering path</a>.</li>
<li>Cheap wins: lazy loading, image sizing, OnPush, trackBy, fewer dependencies.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>1. Measure (Lighthouse, DevTools)  2. Find the bottleneck
3. Fix one thing                   4. Measure again</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Measure, find the bottleneck, fix it, measure again, and guard it with budgets.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-a11y-checklist" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What accessibility basics must you know (WCAG)?</h3>
<p>WCAG has levels A, AA and AAA; most teams and laws target AA. Details in <a href="#html-accessibility">accessibility</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Everything works with keyboard only, with a visible focus.</li>
<li>Text contrast at least 4.5:1 (3:1 for large text).</li>
<li>Use semantic HTML, labels for inputs, alt text for images.</li>
<li>Do not use color alone to show meaning; announce dynamic changes with aria-live.</li>
<li>Test with the Tab key, a screen reader, and axe or Lighthouse.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>&lt;button type="button" aria-label="Close dialog"&gt;X&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;div role="status" aria-live="polite"&gt;{{ message }}&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Aim for WCAG AA: semantic HTML, keyboard access, good contrast, and test with real tools.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-security-checklist" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>What is a frontend security checklist?</h3>
<p>Short list to run through before every release. Real case: <a href="#scn-xss-audit">XSS audit</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Never trust input; Angular escapes templates, so avoid <code>bypassSecurityTrust*</code> and <code>innerHTML</code> (<a href="#xss-sanitization">XSS</a>).</li>
<li>CSP and XSRF protection (<a href="#ng-xsrf-csp">CSP and XSRF</a>); correct <a href="#js-cors">CORS</a> on the server.</li>
<li>Tokens in HttpOnly cookies when possible, not in localStorage (<a href="#js-web-storage">web storage</a>).</li>
<li>No secrets in the bundle; everything in the browser is public.</li>
<li>Run <code>npm audit</code>, update dependencies; use HTTPS everywhere.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>npm audit --omit=dev
# fail CI on high severity</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Escape output, restrict scripts with CSP, protect tokens, hide no secrets in code, and audit dependencies.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-devtools-skills" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>Which browser DevTools skills do you need?</h3>
<p>DevTools is your main debugging tool; know one panel per type of problem.</p>
<ul>
<li>Network: check status, size, timing, headers; throttle to slow 3G; read the waterfall.</li>
<li>Performance: record, find long tasks and layout thrashing in the flame chart.</li>
<li>Memory: heap snapshots to find detached DOM and leaks (<a href="#scn-memory-leak">memory leak</a>).</li>
<li>Coverage: shows unused JS and CSS to cut bundle size.</li>
<li>Lighthouse: audit performance, accessibility, SEO; run it in an incognito window.</li>
<li>Sources: breakpoints, and source maps for original TypeScript.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>Slow page? Network (big files) -&gt; Coverage (unused code) -&gt; Performance (long tasks)</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Network for requests, Performance for slow rendering, Memory for leaks, Coverage for dead code, Lighthouse for audits.</div>
</article>

<article id="eng-documentation-adr" data-cat="pattern">
<span class="eyebrow">Engineering</span>
<h3>How do you document a project: README and ADR?</h3>
<p>Docs let new people start fast and explain old choices. Risks and open questions: <a href="#scn-unclear-requirements">unclear requirements</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li>README: what the app is, how to install, run, test, build, env vars, folder map.</li>
<li>ADR (architecture decision record): a short file per big decision, so future people know why.</li>
<li>ADR parts: title, status, context, decision, consequences.</li>
<li>Keep docs next to code and update them in the same PR.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code># ADR-007: Use NgRx SignalStore
Status: accepted
Context: many screens share order state.
Decision: use SignalStore per feature.
Consequences: less boilerplate; team must learn it.</code></pre>
<div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A README says how to run the project; an ADR says why a decision was made, and both live in the repo.</div>
</article>
</section>

    <section class="part">
      <div class="chapter-divider">
        <span class="chapter-label">Part O · Scenario-based questions</span>
        <div class="chapter-title">Scenario-based questions</div>
        <p class="chapter-dek">Real situations interviewers give to developers with 3 to 5 years of experience. Say how you would find the cause, what you would change, and why.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">85</span>
        <h2>Performance scenarios</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="scn-slow-table" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>A page with a 5,000-row table is slow. What do you do?</h3>
        <p><b>How to answer:</b> measure before changing anything, then reduce the work.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Profile with Angular DevTools and the Chrome Performance tab: is it slow to render, or slow on every change detection?</li>
          <li>Render less: server-side pagination or <a href="#ng-virtual-scroll">virtual scrolling</a>, so only visible rows exist in the DOM.</li>
          <li>Use <a href="#default-vs-onpush">OnPush</a> on the row component, and <a href="#trackby">trackBy</a> (<code>track row.id</code>) so rows are not re-created.</li>
          <li>Move calculations out of the template: no method calls in bindings; use a <a href="#pure-impure-pipe">pure pipe</a> or <code>computed()</code>.</li>
          <li>Debounce filters and sorting, and do heavy work on the server or in a <a href="#js-web-workers">Web Worker</a>.</li>
          <li>Re-measure and compare, and say the numbers.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Measure first, then cut what is rendered: pagination or virtual scroll, OnPush, trackBy, and fewer bindings.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-memory-leak" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>The app gets slower the longer a user keeps it open. How do you find the cause?</h3>
        <p><b>How to answer:</b> this is a memory leak until proven otherwise.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Chrome DevTools, Memory tab: take a heap snapshot, use the app (open and close a page several times), take another, and compare. Look for growing counts of components or detached DOM nodes.</li>
          <li>Usual suspects in Angular: Observables never unsubscribed (<code>valueChanges</code>, router events, <code>interval</code>, a <code>Subject</code>), <code>setInterval</code>, <code>addEventListener</code> on <code>window</code>, and services holding large arrays.</li>
          <li>Fix with <code>takeUntilDestroyed()</code>, the async pipe, and <code>ngOnDestroy</code> cleanup. See <a href="#memory-leaks">Avoiding memory leaks</a> and <a href="#js-memory-leaks">Memory leaks in JavaScript</a>.</li>
          <li>Prevent it: a lint rule for unsubscribed subscriptions, and a code review checklist.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Take heap snapshots to find what keeps growing, then fix subscriptions, timers, listeners, and detached DOM.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-bundle-size" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>The first load of the app takes 12 seconds. How do you reduce it?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Measure: Lighthouse and the Network tab, then <code>source-map-explorer</code> or the bundle analyzer to see what is big. See <a href="#check-performance">Checking performance</a>.</li>
          <li><b>Lazy load</b> feature routes with <code>loadComponent</code> or <code>loadChildren</code>, and preload the important ones.</li>
          <li>Replace heavy libraries (moment with date-fns, a full lodash with single imports), and import only what you use for better <a href="#tree-shaking">tree shaking</a>.</li>
          <li>Use <code>@defer</code> for heavy widgets such as charts. See <a href="#defer">@defer</a>.</li>
          <li>Serve with gzip or Brotli, cache hashed files for a year, and use a CDN.</li>
          <li>Optimize images (<code>NgOptimizedImage</code>, WebP) and fonts. Set <a href="#ng-budgets">bundle budgets</a> so it does not regress.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Analyze the bundle, lazy load routes, remove heavy libraries, enable compression and caching, and use @defer.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-duplicate-calls" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>The same API is called 5 times when a page loads. How do you fix it?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Open the Network tab and find which components call it. Each <code>subscribe()</code> on an HttpClient Observable makes a separate request (<a href="#unicast-multicast">cold, unicast</a>).</li>
          <li>Fix options: cache in a service with <a href="#sharereplay">shareReplay</a>, keep the result in a <code>BehaviorSubject</code> or a signal, or load it once in a <a href="#resolver">resolver</a>.</li>
          <li>Also check for the same stream used twice with the <code>async</code> pipe in a template (each pipe is a subscription); use <code>@if (data$ | async; as data)</code> once.</li>
          <li>For data that changes, add an invalidation rule (refresh after a save, or a time limit).</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Find who subscribes, then share one request with shareReplay, a service cache, or a signal-based store.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-search-race" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>The search box sometimes shows the results of an older search. Why, and what is the fix?</h3>
        <p>Responses can come back in a different order than the requests were sent, so a slow earlier request overwrites a newer one.</p>
        <pre><code>this.search.valueChanges.pipe(
  debounceTime(300),
  distinctUntilChanged(),
  switchMap(term =&gt; this.api.search(term).pipe(catchError(() =&gt; of([]))))
).subscribe(r =&gt; this.results = r);</code></pre>
        <p><a href="#switchmap">switchMap</a> cancels the previous request when a new term arrives, so only the latest result is shown. Debounce reduces the number of calls. Handle errors <b>inside</b> the <code>switchMap</code> so one failure does not end the stream. See <a href="#rx-error-handling-more">catchError placement</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> It is a race condition between responses; switchMap cancels the previous request.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-double-submit" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>Users click Save twice and get duplicate records. How do you stop it?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>UI: disable the button and show a loader while the request is running.</li>
          <li>Code: <a href="#exhaustmap">exhaustMap</a> ignores clicks while a request is in flight.</li>
          <li>Server: the real protection. Use an idempotency key or a unique constraint, because a user can double-click, retry from a bad network, or open two tabs.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre><code>this.saveClicks$.pipe(
  exhaustMap(() =&gt; this.api.save(this.form.getRawValue()).pipe(finalize(() =&gt; this.saving.set(false))))
).subscribe();</code></pre>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Disable the button while saving, use exhaustMap, and make the server idempotent.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-change-detection-loop" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>The browser freezes when a component renders, and the console shows NG0100 or an endless loop. What do you check?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>A method or getter in the template that returns a <b>new object or array each time</b> (<code>get items() { return this.list.filter(...) }</code> used with <code>@for</code>) makes Angular think the data changed on every check.</li>
          <li>A child changing a parent's value in <code>ngAfterViewInit</code> causes <a href="#ng-expression-changed">ExpressionChanged</a>.</li>
          <li>An <code>effect()</code> that writes to a signal it reads loops forever.</li>
          <li>A <code>setInterval</code> or <code>setTimeout</code> inside the zone triggers a check each tick; run it with <a href="#run-outside-angular">runOutsideAngular</a>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Fix: compute the value once (a <code>computed()</code> or pipe), keep objects stable, and use <a href="#default-vs-onpush">OnPush</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A binding is changing during change detection, or a getter or effect keeps changing its own input.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-large-upload" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>Users upload Excel files with 50,000 rows and the browser hangs. What do you do?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Best: send the file to the backend and process it there (queue plus a status endpoint); show progress by polling or <a href="#js-websocket-sse">WebSocket</a>.</li>
          <li>If it must be parsed in the browser, use a <a href="#js-web-workers">Web Worker</a>, so the UI stays responsive, and parse in chunks.</li>
          <li>Validate file type and size before upload, and show <a href="#ng-file-upload">upload progress</a>.</li>
          <li>Show results in a paginated or virtual table, never 50,000 DOM rows. Allow cancel with <code>AbortController</code> or <code>unsubscribe</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Do not parse on the main thread: upload to the server, or parse in a Web Worker and send in chunks, with progress.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= DATA, API &AMP; SECURITY SCENARIOS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">86</span>
        <h2>Data, API &amp; security scenarios</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="scn-token-expiry" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>A user is filling a long form and the token expires. What should happen?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>An <a href="#ng-jwt-refresh">interceptor</a> catches the 401, calls the refresh endpoint once (shared for parallel calls), and retries the failed request.</li>
          <li>If the refresh token has also expired, redirect to login with a <code>returnUrl</code>, and keep the unsaved form: save a draft in <code>sessionStorage</code> or the server, so the user returns to where they were.</li>
          <li>Also: warn before a session timeout with a countdown, and use a <a href="#ng-functional-guard">CanDeactivate</a> guard for unsaved changes.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Refresh the token silently in an interceptor, replay the request, and never lose the form data.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-role-based-ui" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>Different roles must see different menus, pages, and buttons. How do you design it?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>After login, get roles or permissions from the token or a <code>/me</code> call, and keep them in a service (signal).</li>
          <li><b>Routes:</b> a functional <a href="#role-based-access">guard</a> using route <code>data: { roles: [...] }</code>, plus <code>canMatch</code> so lazy modules are not even downloaded.</li>
          <li><b>UI:</b> a structural directive or <code>@if (auth.can('returns.edit'))</code> to hide buttons and menu items.</li>
          <li><b>Server:</b> always enforce permissions in the API. Hiding a button is only user experience, since anyone can call the API directly.</li>
          <li>Use permissions (<code>returns.edit</code>) and not role names in code, so roles can change without code changes.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Permissions from the token or API, a guard per route, a directive for UI, and the server as the real check.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-api-contract-change" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>The backend changes an API response and your screen breaks. How do you protect against it?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Keep API calls in a service and map the response to your own <b>model</b> in one place (an adapter), so a field rename changes one file, not twenty components.</li>
          <li>Type responses (<a href="#ts-interface-vs-type">interfaces</a>), and validate risky data at runtime (a schema library such as Zod).</li>
          <li>Agree on versioned endpoints (<code>/v2/</code>) and deprecation notices with the backend; use a shared OpenAPI spec and generate the client.</li>
          <li>Add tests with <code>HttpTestingController</code> for the mapping, and error monitoring for <code>undefined</code> access.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Map API data in one place, type it, use versioned endpoints, and add contract checks.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-xss-audit" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>A security audit found an XSS issue. How do you fix it and prevent more?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Find the cause: usually <code>innerHTML</code>, <code>[innerHTML]</code>, or <code>bypassSecurityTrustHtml</code> with user or API data. Angular escapes <code>{{ }}</code> by default. See <a href="#xss-sanitization">XSS &amp; DomSanitizer</a>.</li>
          <li>Fix: render as text; if HTML is needed, sanitize it (DOMPurify) on the server and the client, and never bypass for untrusted data.</li>
          <li>Add a <a href="#ng-xsrf-csp">Content Security Policy</a> and <code>HttpOnly</code> cookies for tokens.</li>
          <li>Prevent: an ESLint rule against <code>innerHTML</code>, a review checklist, <code>npm audit</code> in CI, and security tests.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Stop rendering untrusted HTML, sanitize, and add a CSP and review rules.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-offline-realtime" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>The business wants live notifications and offline support. What would you use?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Live data:</b> <a href="#js-websocket-sse">SSE</a> for server-to-client notifications (simple, auto-reconnect), or WebSocket for two-way. Wrap it in an RxJS stream with <code>retry</code> and backoff, and unsubscribe on destroy.</li>
          <li><b>Offline:</b> a PWA service worker (<a href="#ng-i18n-pwa">PWA</a>) caches the app shell and some GET responses; use IndexedDB for data.</li>
          <li><b>Offline writes:</b> store them in a queue and replay when <code>online</code> fires; handle conflicts (last write wins, or ask the user).</li>
          <li>Show connection status in the UI, and decide what must work offline before building.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> WebSocket or SSE for live data, a service worker for offline, and a queue for offline writes.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-caching-strategy" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>How would you cache API data in an Angular app?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Rarely changing master data</b> (states, tax sections): <a href="#sharereplay">shareReplay</a> in a service, or load once at startup.</li>
          <li><b>Per-user data that changes:</b> a signal or store with invalidation after writes, or a time limit.</li>
          <li><b>Generic:</b> an interceptor that caches GET responses by URL with a timeout.</li>
          <li><b>Browser and server:</b> <code>Cache-Control</code> and <code>ETag</code> headers, and a service worker for static assets.</li>
          <li>Always answer the hard part: <b>when does the cache become invalid</b> (after save, on logout, after a time limit)?</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Match the cache to how often data changes: in-memory with shareReplay, HTTP caching headers, an interceptor, or a store.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-multi-env" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>The same build must run in dev, QA, and production with different API URLs. How?</h3>
        <p>Environment files are baked in at build time, which means a separate build per environment. A better approach for deployment pipelines is to build once and load <code>assets/config.json</code> before the app starts, so the container or server supplies the values. See <a href="#ng-config-files">Runtime config</a> and <a href="#environment-files">environment.ts files</a>. Never put secrets in the frontend: everything shipped to the browser is public.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Load a config.json at startup (APP_INITIALIZER), so the build is not rebuilt per environment.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-users-see-old-version" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>After a deployment, some users still see the old version. Why?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><code>index.html</code> was cached by the browser, CDN, or service worker, so it still points to old file names. Serve it with <code>Cache-Control: no-cache</code>, and cache only the <b>hashed</b> JS and CSS files long-term. See <a href="#ng-deploy">Deploying</a>.</li>
          <li>With a PWA, the service worker serves the old version until it updates; use <code>SwUpdate</code> to tell the user "A new version is available, refresh".</li>
          <li>A lazy chunk of the old build may no longer exist after a deployment (a chunk load error); catch it and reload the page.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> index.html or the service worker was cached; fix cache headers and prompt for refresh.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= ARCHITECTURE &AMP; DESIGN SCENARIOS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">87</span>
        <h2>Architecture &amp; design scenarios</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="scn-state-choice" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>How do you decide between a service with signals, NgRx, and local component state?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Local state</b> (a form, a toggle): signals or plain fields in the component.</li>
          <li><b>Shared between a few components:</b> a service with signals or a <code>BehaviorSubject</code>, exposed read-only. See <a href="#ng-facade-pattern">Facade pattern</a>.</li>
          <li><b>Large app, many teams, complex async flows, need for time-travel debugging and strict patterns:</b> <a href="#ng-ngrx-concepts">NgRx</a> (or its SignalStore).</li>
          <li>Explain the trade-off: NgRx adds boilerplate, which is worth it only when the state is big and widely shared. Do not choose it by habit.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Start with the smallest tool; add NgRx only for large, shared, complex state.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-standalone-migration" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>Your project still uses NgModules. How would you move to standalone components?</h3>
        <pre><code>ng generate @angular/core:standalone      # run its three modes in order: convert, remove modules, bootstrap</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>Upgrade Angular first, and keep a clean git state and passing tests.</li>
          <li>Run the schematic in its steps: convert declarations to standalone, remove unneeded modules, then switch to <code>bootstrapApplication</code>.</li>
          <li>Move route by route, and lazy load with <code>loadComponent</code>. Keep shared modules until their parts are converted.</li>
          <li>Review the diff: imports, <code>providers</code>, and the testing setup (<code>imports</code> instead of <code>declarations</code>). See <a href="#standalone-components">Standalone Components</a>.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Use the CLI migration in steps, with tests passing after each step.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-big-form" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>You must build a form with 200 fields that changes by customer type. How?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Describe fields in a <b>configuration</b> (JSON from the API: type, label, validators, visibility rules), and render with a loop that creates a <code>FormControl</code> per field. See <a href="#dynamic-forms">Dynamic Forms</a>.</li>
          <li>Make small reusable field components with <a href="#control-value-accessor">ControlValueAccessor</a>.</li>
          <li>Split into steps or sections (lazy rendered), and use <code>FormArray</code> for repeating rows.</li>
          <li>Use <code>updateOn: 'blur'</code> for heavy validators, and save drafts automatically.</li>
          <li>Test the config logic separately from the UI.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A config-driven dynamic form, split into sections, with reusable field components and validators.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-reusable-component" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>How do you design a reusable table or dropdown that many teams will use?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Keep it <b>dumb</b>: data in through inputs, events out (<a href="#smart-dumb-components">Smart vs. Dumb</a>). No API calls inside it.</li>
          <li>Flexible with <a href="#ng-content-select">content projection</a> and template inputs for custom cells.</li>
          <li>Typed generics (<code>&lt;T&gt;</code>), <a href="#default-vs-onpush">OnPush</a>, and keyboard and screen reader support (<a href="#html-aria">ARIA</a>).</li>
          <li>Themeable with <a href="#css-variables">CSS variables</a>, and documented with Storybook examples and tests.</li>
          <li>Version it as a library in a monorepo, and avoid breaking changes.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A small, typed API with inputs and outputs, content projection, accessibility, and documentation.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-microfrontend-decision" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>Would you use micro frontends for this project?</h3>
        <p>Micro frontends solve an <b>organization</b> problem (many teams, independent releases), and they add complexity: shared dependency versions, consistent UI, routing, and performance. Mention <a href="#ng-microfrontends">Module Federation</a>. For a team of about ten, a well-structured monorepo with lazy-loaded feature areas gives most of the benefit with much less cost. State the decision criteria rather than a yes or no.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Only when separate teams must deploy independently; otherwise a modular monolith is simpler.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-seo-spa" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>The marketing pages in your Angular app must be found on Google. What do you do?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Add <a href="#ssr">SSR</a> with hydration, or prerender the static pages at build time.</li>
          <li>Set the title and meta description per route (<a href="#ng-title-meta">Title &amp; Meta services</a>), a canonical URL, a sitemap, and structured data.</li>
          <li>Make sure real links (<code>routerLink</code> renders <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code>) and semantic HTML are used. See <a href="#html-seo">SEO basics</a>.</li>
          <li>Avoid <code>window</code> and <code>document</code> in server code; use <code>isPlatformBrowser</code> or <code>afterNextRender</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Use SSR or prerendering, set titles and meta tags per route, and keep content out of client-only code.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= JAVASCRIPT, TYPESCRIPT &AMP; CSS SCENARIOS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">88</span>
        <h2>JavaScript, TypeScript &amp; CSS scenarios</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="scn-js-to-ts" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>You inherit a large JavaScript project. How do you move it to TypeScript?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Add TypeScript with <code>allowJs</code> and <code>checkJs: false</code>, so old and new files can live together.</li>
          <li>Convert <b>leaf files first</b> (utilities, models), then outward. Use JSDoc types for files you cannot convert yet.</li>
          <li>Start with <code>strict: false</code>, then enable <code>noImplicitAny</code>, then <code>strictNullChecks</code>, one at a time, fixing the errors. See <a href="#ts-strict-config">tsconfig strict options</a>.</li>
          <li>Avoid <code>any</code>; use <code>unknown</code> and <a href="#ts-type-narrowing">type guards</a>. Add the build and lint steps to CI so it does not slide back.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Move gradually: allowJs, then convert file by file, then tighten strictness.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-undefined-error" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>Production shows "Cannot read properties of undefined". How do you handle it?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Use error monitoring (Sentry) with <a href="#ng-source-maps">source maps</a> to see the real file and line, the user's steps, and the API response involved.</li>
          <li>Typical cause: data arrives later than the template renders it, or an API field is missing for some records. Fix with <code>@if</code>, <a href="#js-optional-chaining">optional chaining</a>, a default value, or a correct type that includes <code>undefined</code>.</li>
          <li>Add a unit test with the failing data, and a global <a href="#ng-error-handler">ErrorHandler</a> so errors are logged and the user sees a friendly message.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Reproduce with the logged data, find the missing guard, fix the cause, and add monitoring and a test.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-layout-mobile" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>The layout works on desktop, but breaks on phones. How do you debug and fix it?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Check the <a href="#html-meta-tags">viewport meta tag</a>, and use DevTools device mode on real device sizes.</li>
          <li>Find the overflow: a fixed <code>width</code> or a long unbroken word or table; outline all elements to see which one is too wide. Fix with <code>max-width: 100%</code>, <code>min-width: 0</code> on flex children, and <code>overflow-wrap: anywhere</code>.</li>
          <li>Use <a href="#css-mobile-first">mobile-first</a> styles, <a href="#css-grid">grid auto-fit</a>, <a href="#css-clamp">clamp()</a>, and let wide tables scroll inside their own container.</li>
          <li>Test touch target sizes, and the on-screen keyboard.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Check the viewport tag, find overflowing elements, and make layouts fluid with grid or flex and relative units.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-zindex-modal" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>A dropdown is hidden behind another element, even with a huge z-index. Why?</h3>
        <p><code>z-index</code> only competes <b>inside the same stacking context</b>. If an ancestor of the dropdown has <code>transform</code>, <code>opacity</code> below 1, <code>position</code> with <code>z-index</code>, <code>filter</code>, or <code>overflow: hidden</code>, the dropdown is trapped in it (or clipped). Fix: render overlays at the end of <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> (Angular CDK Overlay or a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>), or remove the property that creates the stacking context. See <a href="#css-z-index">z-index &amp; stacking context</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Stacking contexts: the dropdown's ancestor has a lower stacking order; render it at the body level.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-this-lost" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>A callback logs "undefined" for this.name, though the method worked a minute ago. What happened?</h3>
        <pre><code>class Timer {
  name = 'Report';
  start() { setTimeout(this.log, 100); }      // this is lost: this.name is undefined
  log() { console.log(this.name); }
}
// fixes: setTimeout(() =&gt; this.log(), 100)   or   setTimeout(this.log.bind(this), 100)   or  log = () =&gt; { ... }</code></pre>
        <p>A method called as <code>obj.method()</code> has <code>this = obj</code>; passed on its own (<code>const f = obj.method</code>) it loses it. See <a href="#js-this">The this keyword</a> and <a href="#js-call-apply-bind">call, apply &amp; bind</a>.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> The method was passed as a callback and lost its this; use an arrow function or bind.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= TEAM, PROCESS &AMP; DELIVERY SCENARIOS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">89</span>
        <h2>Team, process &amp; delivery scenarios</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="scn-prod-bug-triage" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>A critical bug is reported in production on a Friday evening. What do you do?</h3>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Acknowledge and confirm</b>: reproduce it or find it in the logs, and measure who is affected.</li>
          <li><b>Communicate</b>: tell your lead and the stakeholders what is known, and when the next update is.</li>
          <li><b>Mitigate first</b>: roll back the release, or switch off the feature flag, before searching for the perfect fix.</li>
          <li><b>Fix</b> on a branch with a test that fails first, get a quick review, and deploy with a smoke test.</li>
          <li><b>Follow up</b>: a short blameless post-mortem, and a prevention step (a test, an alert, a checklist).</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Show calm, ownership, and communication more than heroics.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Confirm and size the impact, mitigate first (rollback or flag), then fix properly and follow up.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-code-review" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>How do you give code review feedback, and how do you take it?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Giving:</b> review the logic, tests, naming, error handling, and performance risks before style (let tools handle style). Explain the <b>why</b>, suggest an alternative, and ask questions instead of giving orders. Mark must-fix against optional (<code>nit:</code>). Praise good work.</li>
          <li><b>Receiving:</b> do not take it personally; ask when unclear; explain your reasoning if you disagree, with evidence, and agree on a decision; thank the reviewer.</li>
          <li>Keep pull requests small and with a clear description.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Be specific and kind, separate must-fix from preferences, and treat feedback as learning.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-estimate-deadline" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>You are asked for an estimate, and later you will miss the deadline. What do you do?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>When estimating, split the work into small tasks, include testing and review, add a buffer for unknowns, and state assumptions.</li>
          <li>When you see a delay, tell the lead <b>as soon as you know</b>, not on the due date. Bring facts and options: reduce scope (ship the core first), add help, or move the date.</li>
          <li>Ask what matters most, and agree on a new plan. Learn for next time by tracking the estimates.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Break the work down when estimating, and raise risk early with options.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-legacy-refactor" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>You must change old, untested code that nobody understands. How do you do it safely?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>First understand it: read it, run it, and add logging. Ask people who know the history.</li>
          <li>Write <b>characterization tests</b> that record what the code does today, even if it looks wrong.</li>
          <li>Refactor in small steps behind those tests, and commit often. Use feature flags for risky changes.</li>
          <li>Leave the code a little better each time (the "boy scout rule"). A full rewrite is risky, slow, and usually repeats old mistakes; propose it only with a clear reason and a plan.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Add characterization tests first, make small safe changes, and avoid a big rewrite.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="scn-unclear-requirements" data-cat="pattern">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
        <h3>The requirements are unclear and the product owner is not available. What do you do?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Write down what you understand, and your open questions with a proposed default for each.</li>
          <li>Start with the parts that are clear, and design the unclear part so it is easy to change.</li>
          <li>Ask a teammate or the business analyst, and send the questions in writing so the answer is recorded.</li>
          <li>Show a small prototype early, because feedback on something visible is faster than a meeting.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> List assumptions and questions, work on what is clear, and confirm early with a small demo.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= ABOUT YOU ================= -->
    <section class="part">
  <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">90</span><h2>Upgrade, migration &amp; maintenance scenarios</h2></div>

  <article id="scn-upgrade-angular-major" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>The app is on Angular 12 and you must move to a current version. Third-party libraries block the upgrade. How do you plan it?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> upgrade one major at a time, keep the app shippable after each step, and treat blockers as separate small projects.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Inventory: list every dependency, its Angular peer range, and whether it is maintained. Decide per library: upgrade, replace, fork, or remove.</li>
      <li>Add a safety net first: green CI, a smoke e2e suite, and a bundle size baseline.</li>
      <li>Go 12 to 13 to 14 and so on, never skip. Per step run <code>ng update @angular/core@N @angular/cli@N</code> (see <a href="#ng-update-upgrade">ng update</a>), fix, test, merge, and deploy.</li>
      <li>For a blocking library, use <code>--legacy-peer-deps</code> only as a short bridge, with a ticket and an owner. Do not leave it.</li>
      <li>After the upgrade, run migrations in small PRs (control flow, standalone, signals). Do not mix them with the version bump.</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>ng update @angular/core@13 @angular/cli@13
ng update @angular/core@14 @angular/cli@14
# commit and release after each step</code></pre>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Upgrade one major at a time with tests in place, ship after each step, and deal with blocking libraries as separate tracked tasks.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-zone-to-zoneless" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>Management wants to move a big app from Zone.js to zoneless. How do you do it gradually?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> zoneless needs components that tell Angular when they change. So first make them OnPush and signal friendly, then switch.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Step 1: move components to <a href="#default-vs-onpush">OnPush</a>. If the app works with OnPush, it is close to zoneless.</li>
      <li>Step 2: replace mutable fields with <a href="#signals">signals</a>, or use the async pipe. Plain field changes after a timer or a promise will no longer refresh the view.</li>
      <li>Step 3: find code that relied on Zone.js (third-party libs, <code>setTimeout</code> updates) and wrap updates in signals or call <code>markForCheck()</code>.</li>
      <li>Step 4: enable it behind a branch or an environment, then test the key flows. See <a href="#zoneless">Zoneless</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [provideZonelessChangeDetection()]
});</code></pre>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Go OnPush first, then signals, then remove Zone.js; the risky part is old code and libraries that depended on zone triggers.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-replace-abandoned-library" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>A library used in 40 places is deprecated and no longer maintained. What do you do?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> do not rewrite 40 places at once. Hide the library behind your own interface, then swap the inside.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Assess the risk: security issues, blocks Angular upgrades, no fix for bugs? If low risk, schedule it; if high, do it now.</li>
      <li>Pick a replacement with a checklist (see <a href="#scn-choose-library">choosing a library</a>), and build a small proof of concept.</li>
      <li>Create a thin wrapper component or service. Migrate usages one feature at a time. Both can live together during the move.</li>
      <li>Add tests around the wrapper first, so behavior stays the same.</li>
      <li>Remove the old package and add a lint rule that bans importing it.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Wrap the library behind your own API, migrate step by step, then ban the old import so it cannot come back.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-break-shared-module" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>SharedModule has 300 declarations, everything imports it, and you have circular dependencies. How do you break it up?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> a huge shared module hurts bundle size and build time, and circular imports cause strange runtime errors.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Find the cycles with <code>madge --circular</code> or an ESLint rule (<code>import/no-cycle</code>). Fix the worst first.</li>
      <li>Make pieces <a href="#standalone-components">standalone</a>, so each component imports only what it needs and SharedModule can shrink.</li>
      <li>Split by purpose: a UI kit, pipes and directives, and feature code that is used by only one area goes back into that feature.</li>
      <li>Define the rule: features can import shared, shared never imports features. Enforce it with Nx tags or lint boundaries.</li>
      <li>Move cross-feature contracts (interfaces, tokens) to a small <code>core</code> or <code>data-access</code> library.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Standalone pieces plus a one-way dependency rule (features use shared, never the reverse) remove both the giant module and the cycles.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-reduce-build-time" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>The production build takes 15 minutes. How do you bring it down?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> measure which stage is slow (install, lint, tests, build), then fix the biggest one.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>CI: cache <code>node_modules</code> (or the package manager store) and the Angular build cache; use <code>npm ci</code>.</li>
      <li>Split jobs: lint, unit tests, and build run in parallel, not one after another.</li>
      <li>Build tool: move to the esbuild based <code>application</code> builder, which is much faster. See <a href="#ng-builders">builders</a>.</li>
      <li>In a monorepo, build only what changed (<code>nx affected</code>) and use a remote cache.</li>
      <li>Check the code: huge barrel files, giant modules, and many source maps in production slow it down.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Time each stage, then cache, parallelize, use the esbuild builder, and build only what changed.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-flaky-tests" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>Tests fail randomly in CI but pass on your machine. How do you handle flaky tests?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> flaky tests destroy trust, so treat them as bugs, not as noise. Do not just click "re-run".</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Find the cause: real timers and <code>setTimeout</code>, shared state between tests, test order, real HTTP, or dates and time zones.</li>
      <li>Use fake time: <code>fakeAsync</code> with <code>tick()</code>, or <code>jasmine.clock()</code>. See <a href="#ng-test-async">async testing</a>.</li>
      <li>Reset state in <code>beforeEach</code>, mock HTTP with <a href="#httptestingcontroller">HttpTestingController</a>, and fix the clock.</li>
      <li>For e2e, wait for conditions, not for fixed seconds.</li>
      <li>Short term: quarantine the test with a ticket and a deadline, so it does not block everyone. Run in random order to catch order bugs.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Flaky usually means time, shared state, or real network; fix the root cause, and quarantine with a deadline, never ignore.</div>
  </article>
</section>

    <section class="part">
  <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">91</span><h2>Product &amp; UX scenarios</h2></div>

  <article id="scn-autosave-drafts" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>A long form must autosave drafts, and warn users who leave with unsaved changes. How do you build it?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> two separate features: saving the draft, and protecting against leaving.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Autosave: debounce <code>valueChanges</code>, skip if the form is not dirty, and use <code>switchMap</code> or <code>concatMap</code> to save. Show "Saving..." and "Saved at 10:32".</li>
      <li>Save drafts to the server (so it works across devices), or to <code>localStorage</code> as a fallback.</li>
      <li>Leaving the page inside the app: a <a href="#candeactivate">canDeactivate guard</a> that asks for confirmation if the form is dirty.</li>
      <li>Closing the tab: a <code>beforeunload</code> listener.</li>
      <li>Handle failure: if autosave fails, show a clear message and keep the data in memory.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>this.form.valueChanges.pipe(
  debounceTime(1000),
  filter(() =&gt; this.form.dirty),
  switchMap(v =&gt; this.api.saveDraft(v)),
  takeUntilDestroyed()
).subscribe(() =&gt; this.lastSaved.set(new Date()));</code></pre>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Autosave with debounce plus a status label, protect navigation with canDeactivate and beforeunload, and plan for save failures.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-url-state" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>Users want filters, sort, and page to be kept in the URL, so they can share links and use the back button. How?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> make the URL the source of truth. The screen reads from the URL, and user actions only change the URL.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Use <a href="#query-params">query params</a>: <code>/orders?status=open&amp;sort=date&amp;page=2</code>.</li>
      <li>Read them as a stream (<code>route.queryParamMap</code>), and fetch data with <code>switchMap</code>.</li>
      <li>On change, navigate with <code>queryParamsHandling: 'merge'</code>. Use <code>replaceUrl: true</code> for small changes like typing, so history is not flooded.</li>
      <li>Validate and use defaults, because users can edit the URL by hand.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>this.router.navigate([], {
  queryParams: { status, page: 1 },
  queryParamsHandling: 'merge'
});</code></pre>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Keep list state in query params and load data from them; you get shareable links and a working back button for free.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-concurrent-edit" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>Two users edit the same record. The last save silently overwrites the first. How do you prevent it?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> use optimistic locking. The client proves which version it edited.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Server returns a version (an <code>ETag</code> header or a <code>version</code> field) with the record.</li>
      <li>On update, the client sends it back (<code>If-Match</code> header). If the record changed, the server answers <b>409 Conflict</b> (or 412).</li>
      <li>UI on 409: tell the user, load the latest version, and let them merge, overwrite, or cancel. Do not lose their text.</li>
      <li>Pessimistic locking (a lock while editing) is simpler to understand but needs timeouts and is worse when users close the tab.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>this.http.put(`/api/docs/${id}`, body, {
  headers: { 'If-Match': this.etag }
}).pipe(
  catchError(e =&gt; e.status === 409 ? this.showConflict() : throwError(() =&gt; e))
);</code></pre>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Send the version with every update, let the server reject stale writes with 409, and give the user a merge choice.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-export-report" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>Users need to export a large report (100,000 rows) to Excel or PDF. Server or client?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> small and simple exports can run on the client; large or sensitive ones should run on the server.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Client side: fine for what is already on screen (a few thousand rows). Cost: it uses user memory and can freeze the tab; move it to a <a href="#js-web-workers">Web Worker</a>.</li>
      <li>Server side: it can read the full data, respects permissions, and can stream the file. Better for 100,000 rows.</li>
      <li>For very long jobs: start an export job, return a job id, poll or push when ready, then download with a link. The UI stays responsive and shows progress.</li>
      <li>Download with a <a href="#js-file-blob">Blob</a>, and send the same filters the user sees on screen.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Export what is on screen on the client, but use a server job with progress and a download link for big or permissioned data.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-global-loading-error-ux" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>How do you design loading and error handling for the whole app, so every page is consistent?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> centralize the common cases, and keep page-specific cases local.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>An <a href="#ng-functional-interceptor">interceptor</a> for HTTP errors: 401 to login, 403 to a "no access" message, 5xx to a toast with a retry. Never show raw server text.</li>
      <li>A global <a href="#ng-error-handler">ErrorHandler</a> that logs unexpected errors to a monitoring tool.</li>
      <li>Loading: use skeletons for page content and a small spinner on buttons. A global top bar for background work is enough; a full-screen blocker is annoying.</li>
      <li>Each screen has four states: loading, empty, error (with retry), and data. Make a small reusable component for them.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Handle cross-cutting errors in one interceptor and handler, and design every screen for loading, empty, error, and data states.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-white-label-theming" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>One app must serve many customers, each with its own colors, logo, and name (multi-tenant white label). How?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> one build, many themes at runtime. Avoid one build per customer.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Design tokens as <a href="#css-variables">CSS variables</a>: <code>--brand</code>, <code>--bg</code>, <code>--radius</code>. Components use only variables.</li>
      <li>At startup, load tenant config (from the subdomain or a login response) with <code>provideAppInitializer</code>, and set variables on <code>:root</code>.</li>
      <li>Logo, texts, and feature switches also come from the tenant config.</li>
      <li>Test with two very different themes, and check color contrast for each one.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>const t = await this.http.get&lt;Theme&gt;('/api/tenant/theme').toPromise();
Object.entries(t.tokens).forEach(([k, v]) =&gt;
  document.documentElement.style.setProperty(`--${k}`, v));</code></pre>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> One build, CSS variable tokens, and tenant config loaded at startup give you white labeling without forking the app.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-timezone-bugs" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>Users in different countries see the wrong dates, sometimes one day off. How do you fix and prevent it?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> the usual cause is mixing a moment in time with a calendar date, and parsing strings without a time zone.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Store and send moments as <b>UTC ISO strings</b> (<code>2026-03-05T10:00:00Z</code>). Show them in the user's local zone (or the zone in their profile).</li>
      <li>A birthday or due date is a <b>calendar date</b>, not a moment. Send it as <code>2026-03-05</code> and never convert it. <code>new Date('2026-03-05')</code> is parsed as UTC, so a user in a negative zone sees March 4.</li>
      <li>Use the <code>date</code> pipe with a timezone argument, or Intl, instead of manual math. See <a href="#js-date">Date in JavaScript</a>.</li>
      <li>Test with a fixed time zone and around DST changes and midnight.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Store moments in UTC, show them local, and treat date-only values as plain strings that you never convert.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-realtime-table" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>A table gets 100 WebSocket messages per second, and the UI freezes. What do you do?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> the problem is too many renders, not too many messages. Batch the updates.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Buffer messages and apply them once per frame or every 250 ms: <code>bufferTime(250)</code>, or <code>auditTime</code> to keep the latest.</li>
      <li>Keep rows in a map by id and update in place; the view uses <a href="#trackby">trackBy</a> with OnPush and signals so only changed rows update.</li>
      <li>Render only visible rows with <a href="#ng-virtual-scroll">virtual scroll</a>.</li>
      <li>Run heavy parsing in a <a href="#js-web-workers">Web Worker</a>, and ask the server to send fewer, merged updates. See <a href="#js-websocket-sse">WebSocket</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>socket$.pipe(
  bufferTime(250),
  filter(batch =&gt; batch.length &gt; 0)
).subscribe(batch =&gt; this.store.applyBatch(batch));</code></pre>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Buffer messages into batches, update rows by id, and render only what is visible, so the UI redraws a few times a second, not 100.</div>
  </article>
</section>

    <section class="part">
  <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">92</span><h2>Quality, delivery &amp; architecture scenarios</h2></div>

  <article id="scn-feature-flags" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>You must release a big feature gradually, with the option to turn it off fast. How?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> separate deploying code from releasing a feature, using feature flags.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Flags come from a service (LaunchDarkly, Unleash, or your own API), loaded at startup, per user, role, or percentage.</li>
      <li>In Angular: a flag service with signals, a route guard (<a href="#route-guards">canMatch</a>) to hide whole routes, and <code>@if</code> in templates.</li>
      <li>Rollout plan: internal users, then 5 percent, then 50, then 100. Watch errors and metrics at each step.</li>
      <li>Keep the flag count low: remove each flag after full rollout, or it becomes debt.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>{
  path: 'new-checkout',
  canMatch: [() =&gt; inject(Flags).isOn('newCheckout')],
  loadComponent: () =&gt; import('./checkout.component')
}</code></pre>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Flags let you ship code dark, roll out by percentage, and switch off without a deploy; remove them after rollout.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-choose-library" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>Your team needs a grid or chart library. How do you choose a third-party library?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> give a checklist, not a favorite.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Health: last release, open issues, number of maintainers, and how fast the Angular version is supported.</li>
      <li>Fit: does it cover must-have features (virtual scroll, accessibility, i18n) without hacks? Test with a real use case.</li>
      <li>Cost: bundle size, tree shaking, license (and price for enterprise features), security audit.</li>
      <li>Exit: how hard is it to replace? Wrap it behind your own component.</li>
      <li>Decide with a short proof of concept and write down the choice (an ADR), so the team knows why.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Judge health, fit, cost, and exit plan, prove it with a small spike, and record the decision.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-lighthouse-score" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>The Lighthouse performance score is 45. How do you raise it?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> read the report, find the biggest metric problem (LCP, TBT, CLS), and fix in order of impact.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>LCP (slow main content): optimize the hero image (<code>NgOptimizedImage</code>, <code>priority</code>), preload fonts, and cut render-blocking CSS and JS. See <a href="#scn-bundle-size">bundle size</a>.</li>
      <li>TBT (blocked main thread): lazy load routes, <a href="#defer">@defer</a> heavy widgets, remove unused polyfills and libraries.</li>
      <li>CLS (jumping layout): set width and height on images and reserve space for late content.</li>
      <li>Consider SSR or prerender for the first paint.</li>
      <li>Test on a throttled mobile profile and add <a href="#ng-budgets">budgets</a> plus Lighthouse in CI.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Fix by metric: LCP with images and blocking resources, TBT with less JS, CLS with reserved space, and guard it in CI.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-accessibility-audit" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>An accessibility audit failed. Where do you start, and how do you stop it from coming back?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> fix the most common and highest impact issues first, then automate.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Keyboard: every control reachable with Tab, visible focus, no keyboard traps, and focus moves into and back from dialogs.</li>
      <li>Semantics: real <code>button</code> and <code>a</code> elements, labels on inputs, headings in order, <code>alt</code> text. See <a href="#html-accessibility">accessibility</a>.</li>
      <li>ARIA only where HTML is not enough; announce dynamic changes with <code>aria-live</code>.</li>
      <li>Color contrast and not using color alone. Test with a screen reader.</li>
      <li>Prevent: axe or Lighthouse in CI, Angular CDK a11y helpers, and a checklist in the PR template.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Start with keyboard, semantic HTML, labels, and contrast, then add automated checks to CI so it stays fixed.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-api-down-degradation" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>A backend API is slow or down. How should the Angular app behave?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> fail gracefully. One broken API should not break the whole app.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Timeout and retry only safe (GET) requests, with backoff and a limit. See <a href="#ng-retry-timeout">retry and timeout</a>.</li>
      <li>Show stale cached data with a "last updated" note when fresh data is not available.</li>
      <li>Circuit breaker idea: after several failures, stop calling for a short time instead of hammering a sick server, then try again.</li>
      <li>Isolate: only the affected widget shows an error with a Retry button; the rest keeps working.</li>
      <li>Tell the user clearly, and log the failure to monitoring.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>this.api.getRates().pipe(
  timeout(5000),
  retry({ count: 2, delay: (e, i) =&gt; timer(500 * 2 ** i) }),
  catchError(() =&gt; of(this.cache.lastRates()))
);</code></pre>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Use timeouts, limited retries, stale cache, and per-widget errors, so a failing API degrades one feature, not the app.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-dashboard-widgets" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>Design a dashboard with 15 widgets, each with its own data. How do you keep it fast and stable?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> treat each widget as an independent unit.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Each widget is a standalone OnPush component that loads its own data, with its own loading and error state. One failure does not block others.</li>
      <li>Load below-the-fold widgets lazily with <a href="#defer">@defer (on viewport)</a>; heavy chart libraries load only then.</li>
      <li>Do not fire 15 calls at once if the server is weak: prioritize the top widgets, or use one aggregated endpoint.</li>
      <li>Share data used by several widgets through a service with caching. See <a href="#scn-duplicate-calls">duplicate calls</a>.</li>
      <li>Let users refresh a single widget, and avoid polling everything every second.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>@defer (on viewport) {
  &lt;app-sales-chart /&gt;
} @placeholder { &lt;div class="skeleton"&gt;&lt;/div&gt; }
  @error { &lt;app-widget-error (retry)="reload()" /&gt; }</code></pre>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Independent widgets with their own state, deferred loading, shared caches, and isolated errors keep a busy dashboard fast.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-new-app-architecture" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>You start a new large Angular app from scratch. How do you structure the folders and architecture?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> structure by feature, with clear layers and rules, and keep it simple at first.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>core/</code>: app-wide singletons (auth, interceptors, config). <code>shared/</code>: dumb UI components, pipes, directives. <code>features/</code>: one folder per business area, lazy loaded with <a href="#lazy-loading">routes</a>.</li>
      <li>Inside a feature: pages (smart), components (dumb), data-access (services and state), models.</li>
      <li>Rules: features do not import each other; shared does not import features. Enforce with lint or Nx module boundaries.</li>
      <li>State: start with services and signals; add <a href="#ngrx">NgRx</a> only when complexity needs it.</li>
      <li>Set up early: strict TypeScript, ESLint and Prettier, <a href="#ng-budgets">budgets</a>, CI, environment config, and an error and logging strategy.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Organize by feature with core and shared layers, enforce import rules with tooling, and add heavy tools only when needed.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-testing-strategy" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>You join a new team that has no testing strategy. How do you set one up?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> aim for confidence per cost, not 100 percent coverage.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Many fast unit tests for logic: services, pipes, pure functions, and state. See <a href="#test-service">testing a service</a>.</li>
      <li>Fewer component tests with <a href="#testbed">TestBed</a> for key behavior (user sees, clicks), not internal details.</li>
      <li>A small set of e2e tests (Playwright or Cypress) for critical flows: login, checkout, main create flow.</li>
      <li>Rules: tests run in CI on every PR, a failing test blocks merge, new bugs get a regression test first, and flaky tests are fixed fast.</li>
      <li>Coverage is a signal, not a goal: set a minimum for new code only.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Use a testing pyramid (many unit, some component, few e2e), enforce it in CI, and add a regression test for every bug.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-secure-file-download" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>Files must be downloaded only by logged-in users. A plain link does not send the token. What do you do?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> an <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> cannot add an Authorization header, so choose another way.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Fetch with HttpClient (the <a href="#interceptor">interceptor</a> adds the token), using <code>responseType: 'blob'</code>, then create an object URL and click a hidden link. See <a href="#js-file-blob">Blob</a>.</li>
      <li>Good for small and medium files. Large files load fully into memory.</li>
      <li>For large files: ask the API for a short-lived signed URL (valid for a minute, one file) and open it. The token never appears in a long-lived URL.</li>
      <li>Never put the access token in the query string; it ends up in logs and history.</li>
      <li>Always check permission on the server, not only in the UI.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>this.http.get(`/api/files/${id}`, { responseType: 'blob' }).subscribe(blob =&gt; {
  const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
  const a = Object.assign(document.createElement('a'), { href: url, download: name });
  a.click(); URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
});</code></pre>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Download through HttpClient as a blob for normal files, or use a short-lived signed URL for big ones, and never put the token in a URL.</div>
  </article>
</section>

    <section class="part">
  <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">93</span><h2>Leadership scenarios (3 to 5 years)</h2></div>

  <article id="scn-mentor-junior" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>A junior developer's pull requests always have many issues. How do you help them?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> the goal is a developer who grows, not a perfect single PR.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Talk privately, ask how they work, and find the root cause: missing knowledge, unclear tasks, or rushing.</li>
      <li>Give feedback on the code, kindly and with reasons: "this leaks a subscription because..." plus a link or example. Praise what is good.</li>
      <li>Pair on one task, and ask them to explain their solution. Review before big work starts (a short design chat).</li>
      <li>Automate repeating issues: lint rules, a PR checklist, and a self-review before asking others.</li>
      <li>Set small goals and check progress every week or two.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Find the root cause, give kind and specific feedback, pair on real tasks, and automate repeated mistakes.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-push-back-scope" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>The product owner adds new features in the middle of the sprint and keeps the same deadline. How do you push back?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> do not just say no. Show the trade-off with data and offer options.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Show the facts: current scope, remaining capacity, and the estimate of the new work. "Adding this means moving X or moving the date by 4 days."</li>
      <li>Give options: cut something, move the date, add people (which rarely helps quickly), or deliver a smaller first version.</li>
      <li>Ask what matters most; let the business choose the priority, while you explain the risk (bugs, no tests, burnout).</li>
      <li>Put the agreement in writing in the ticket or chat. Be calm and on the team's side.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Do not refuse; show the cost in days, offer options, and let the owner choose the priority, then write it down.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-tech-debt-conversation" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>Your manager wants only new features, but tech debt is slowing the team. How do you make the case?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> speak in business terms: speed, risk, and cost, not "the code is ugly".</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Use numbers: "bugs in this module took 30 percent of our time last quarter", "builds take 15 minutes", "each feature here takes twice as long".</li>
      <li>Propose small, safe steps: reserve about 15 to 20 percent of each sprint, or fix debt in the area you are already changing (the boy scout rule).</li>
      <li>Link each debt item to a result: fewer incidents, faster delivery, easier hiring and onboarding.</li>
      <li>Prioritize: pick the 2 or 3 items with the biggest pain. Show progress after each one.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Sell tech debt as lost speed and higher risk with real numbers, and ask for a small fixed share of every sprint.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-onboarding-codebase" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>You join a company with a big unknown codebase. How do you spend your first two weeks?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> learn, deliver something small, and build relationships.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Day 1 to 3: set up the project, run it, read the README and architecture notes, learn the main user flow as a user, and meet your team.</li>
      <li>Week 1: read the routes, the main modules, and the state and API layers. Draw a simple map. Ask questions and write down answers as docs.</li>
      <li>Fix a small bug or a "good first issue" to learn the process: branch, PR, CI, review, deploy.</li>
      <li>Week 2: read recent PRs, learn the coding rules, and take a real small feature. Note confusing parts, and improve docs or tests as you go.</li>
      <li>Ask for a clear 30-60-90 day goal from your lead.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Run it, map it, ship a small fix early, and write down what you learn; do not start a big rewrite.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-own-unwanted-module" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>There is an old, fragile module that nobody wants to touch. You are asked to own it. What do you do?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> ownership means making it safe, visible, and better step by step.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>First understand it: read the code, find users and the business purpose, and check bug history.</li>
      <li>Add safety: write tests that capture current behavior before changing anything (characterization tests), plus monitoring.</li>
      <li>Document it: a short README with how it works, risks, and who to ask.</li>
      <li>Improve in small PRs, using the same approach as in <a href="#scn-legacy-refactor">refactoring legacy code</a> (an existing scenario); do not rewrite everything.</li>
      <li>Spread the knowledge: pair with a second person so there is no single point of failure.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Take ownership by adding tests and docs first, improving in small steps, and sharing the knowledge.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-design-vs-dev" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>The designer wants a pixel-perfect result, but the deadline is close. How do you handle it?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> remember you share a goal: a good product. Make the trade-off visible.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Separate what matters (spacing system, colors, typography, layout, states) from small details (a 1px shadow, an animation curve).</li>
      <li>Agree on design tokens and a shared component library, so most pixels are right by default.</li>
      <li>Show the cost: "this custom animation takes 2 days; the standard one takes 2 hours." Let the team decide.</li>
      <li>Do a quick review together in the browser. Ship the core, and add polish in a follow-up ticket.</li>
      <li>Explain technical limits calmly, and propose an alternative instead of only saying it is not possible.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Agree on tokens and what really matters, show the time cost of details, and schedule polish as a follow-up.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="scn-teammate-no-tests" data-cat="pattern">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scenario</span>
    <h3>A teammate keeps pushing code without tests. How do you deal with it?</h3>
    <p><b>How to answer:</b> change the system first, and the person second.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Talk to them one to one, with curiosity: do they lack time, skills, or see no value? Offer to pair on writing the first tests.</li>
      <li>Make it a team rule, not a personal attack: add a PR checklist, a coverage check on changed code, and "no merge on red CI".</li>
      <li>In review, ask for a test kindly and explain the risk: "this branch can break the checkout; can we cover it?"</li>
      <li>Make testing easy: good test helpers, fixtures, and examples in the repo.</li>
      <li>If it continues, bring it to the lead as a team process issue, with examples of bugs it caused.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Talk first and help, then fix it with team rules and CI checks, so quality does not depend on one person's habit.</div>
  </article>
</section>

    <section class="part">
      <div class="chapter-divider">
        <span class="chapter-label">Part P · HR &amp; behavioral questions</span>
        <div class="chapter-title">HR &amp; behavioral questions</div>
        <p class="chapter-dek">The non-technical round: how you talk about yourself, your work, and your choices. Prepare your own real stories, and use these as a frame.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">94</span>
        <h2>About you</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="hr-introduce" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>Tell me about yourself.</h3>
        <p><b>Structure (about 90 seconds):</b></p>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Present:</b> your current role, years of experience, and main stack. ("I am an Angular developer with 4 years of experience, working on a TDS compliance platform.")</li>
          <li><b>Past:</b> two or three achievements with a result. ("I cut the load time of the dashboard from 8 to 3 seconds by lazy loading and OnPush.")</li>
          <li><b>Future:</b> why this role fits you and what you want to learn.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Do not read your résumé or start with your childhood. Practice it aloud until it feels natural, and keep it relevant to the job.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Present, past, future in about 90 seconds: your role now, key experience, and why this role.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hr-strengths" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>What are your strengths?</h3>
        <p>Choose strengths the role needs (problem solving, ownership, learning fast, clear communication), and give a short proof for each: "I debug carefully. Last quarter I traced a memory leak in our grid to an unsubscribed stream and fixed it, which stopped the slowdown complaints." Avoid long lists and empty adjectives.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Pick two or three that match the job, and back each one with a real example.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hr-weakness" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>What is your weakness?</h3>
        <p>Pick a genuine weakness that is not essential to the job, and show progress. Example: "I used to take on too much and say yes to everything. Now I estimate my capacity, tell my lead early when I am full, and prioritize with them." Avoid fake weaknesses ("I work too hard") and serious ones (missing deadlines, poor teamwork).</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Name a real, fixable weakness, and show what you are doing about it.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hr-why-leaving" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>Why are you leaving your current company?</h3>
        <p>Be honest but positive and forward-looking: "I have learned a lot and delivered our main modules, and now I want bigger technical challenges, such as larger-scale architecture, and a team where I can grow into a senior role." Do not blame your manager, colleagues, or pay. Keep it short, and do not contradict what your references would say.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Talk about growth and what you are moving toward, and never criticize your employer.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hr-why-company" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>Why do you want to join us?</h3>
        <p>Mention something specific you learned (the product, the customers, the tech stack, the engineering culture), link it to your experience, and say what you want to contribute. Generic answers ("good company, good growth") show no preparation. Read the company website, the job description, and recent news before the interview.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Show you researched the company: product, tech, and how your skills fit.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hr-five-years" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>Where do you see yourself in five years?</h3>
        <p>"In five years I want to be a strong senior engineer: designing features end to end, mentoring juniors, and being trusted with technical decisions. I would like to grow here as the product grows." Show ambition without sounding like you will leave soon, or like you want the interviewer's job.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> A realistic path of growing skills and responsibility, in a way that fits the role.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= TEAMWORK &AMP; SITUATIONS ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">95</span>
        <h2>Teamwork &amp; situations</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="hr-star" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>What is the STAR method?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Situation:</b> the context, in one or two sentences.</li>
          <li><b>Task:</b> your responsibility or goal.</li>
          <li><b>Action:</b> what <b>you</b> did, step by step (spend most of your time here; use "I", not "we").</li>
          <li><b>Result:</b> the outcome, with numbers if possible, and what you learned.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Prepare four or five real stories (a conflict, a failure, a tight deadline, a technical win, a time you led) that you can adapt to many questions.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Situation, Task, Action, Result: a way to tell a short, clear story.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hr-conflict" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>Tell me about a conflict with a teammate, and how you handled it.</h3>
        <p>Use STAR. A good shape: two developers disagreed about an approach, you asked to understand their reasoning, compared the options against the project needs (performance, time, maintenance), maybe tried a quick proof of concept, and agreed on a decision with your lead if needed. End with the working relationship intact and a lesson. Do not blame the other person, and do not say "I never have conflicts".</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Stay calm and professional: listen, find the real issue, agree on a fix, and focus on the result.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hr-failure" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>Tell me about a mistake or a failure.</h3>
        <p>Pick a real mistake with limited damage (a bug released without testing an edge case, a wrong estimate). Say what happened, that you took responsibility, how you fixed it quickly, and <b>what process you changed</b> (a test, a checklist, an earlier conversation). Employers look for honesty and learning, not perfection.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Own it, explain the fix and what you changed so it does not happen again.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hr-pressure" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>How do you handle pressure and tight deadlines?</h3>
        <p>Show a method, not just "I work late": clarify priorities with your lead, split the work into small deliverables, deal with the riskiest part first, communicate blockers early, and cut scope or ask for help when needed. Give a real example with the result, such as delivering a compliance filing feature before a statutory due date.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Prioritize, communicate early, break down the work, and protect quality on the critical parts.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hr-difficult-colleague" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>How do you work with a difficult team member or manager?</h3>
        <p>Say you first try to understand the reason (pressure, a different goal, unclear communication), talk to them directly and privately, focus on the work and facts, and adjust your communication style (written updates for a manager who is busy). Involve your lead or HR only if it does not improve. Do not speak badly about people.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Understand their point of view, communicate directly and respectfully, and stay professional.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hr-learn-new" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>How do you keep your skills up to date?</h3>
        <p>Be concrete: "I follow the Angular blog and release notes, I tried signals in a small internal tool before suggesting them to the team, and I read RxJS and TypeScript documentation. I also learn from code reviews." Mention one thing you learned recently. This question is often a check on whether you know the current Angular version.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Name specific habits: official docs, side projects, courses, and applying new things at work.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hr-leadership" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>Have you led or mentored anyone?</h3>
        <p>At 3 to 5 years, interviewers look for early leadership: onboarding a new joiner, reviewing pull requests, writing a short guide, owning a module or release, running a knowledge-sharing session, or helping a junior debug. Describe what you did, how you helped them grow, and the result.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Even without a title: reviews, onboarding, knowledge sharing, and taking ownership of a module.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ================= SALARY &AMP; JOINING ================= -->
    <section class="part">
      <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">96</span>
        <h2>Salary &amp; joining</h2>
      </div>

      <article id="hr-salary" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>What are your salary expectations?</h3>
        <p>Research the market for the role, location, and experience, and know your current pay. Give a <b>range</b> that you would be happy with, based on your skills and the market, and show flexibility: "Based on my experience and the responsibilities, I expect something in the range of X to Y, and I am open to discussing the full package." If you can, ask for the company's budget first. Do not lie about your current salary.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Give a researched range, link it to the role and market, and stay flexible on the total package.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hr-notice-period" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>What is your notice period? Can you join earlier?</h3>
        <p>Tell the real notice period. If you are asked to join earlier, say you will talk to your current employer about a buyout or an early release, but that you also want to hand over your work properly. Companies value professional exits. Do not promise what you cannot deliver.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> State it honestly, and say what you can negotiate in a professional way.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hr-questions-to-ask" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>Do you have any questions for us?</h3>
        <p>Good questions: "What does the team's tech stack and release process look like?", "What would success look like in the first three months?", "How are code reviews and testing handled?", "What are the biggest technical challenges right now?", "How does the team support learning?" Do not ask about leave or pay first, and never say "No questions".</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Always ask two or three thoughtful questions about the work, team, and growth.</div>
      </article>

      <article id="hr-why-hire" data-cat="utility">
        <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
        <h3>Why should we hire you?</h3>
        <p>Match the job description: "You need someone who can build and maintain complex Angular modules. I have four years in Angular and RxJS, I have optimized slow screens, and I can work with the backend team on API design. I learn fast and take ownership, so I would be productive quickly." Use specifics, not superlatives.</p>
        <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Connect your skills and results to what the job needs, in a confident and short answer.</div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section class="part">
  <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">97</span><h2>Experience-level questions (3 to 5 years)</h2></div>

  <article id="hr-explain-project" data-cat="utility">
    <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
    <h3>Explain your current project and its architecture</h3>
    <p>This is the most common question for experienced candidates. Keep it to 2 to 3 minutes. Go from big picture to your part. Fill this template before the interview.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Product and users:</b> what it does, who uses it, how many (for example 50,000 monthly users).</li>
      <li><b>Modules:</b> the main screens or feature areas (dashboard, orders, reports).</li>
      <li><b>State:</b> services with RxJS, signals, or NgRx; why you chose it.</li>
      <li><b>Auth:</b> login method, JWT or SSO, route guards, interceptor, refresh.</li>
      <li><b>API:</b> REST or GraphQL, error handling, caching, who owns the backend.</li>
      <li><b>Testing and CI/CD:</b> Jasmine/Jest, Cypress, pipeline steps, deploy frequency.</li>
      <li><b>Your role and numbers:</b> modules you own, team size, one measurable result.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Sample answer: "I work on a B2B order management portal used by about 8,000 dealers. It is an Angular 17 app with lazy loaded feature modules: orders, invoices and reports. We use services with RxJS for state and signals for local UI state. Login is SSO with JWT; an interceptor adds the token and handles refresh, and route guards check roles. We talk to .NET REST APIs. We have Jest unit tests with about 75 percent coverage and Cypress for main flows. Azure DevOps builds and deploys every sprint. I own the orders and invoices modules, and I reduced the orders page load from 4 seconds to 1.8 seconds with lazy loading and OnPush."</p>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Go product, architecture, then your role with one number; practise it until it takes under three minutes.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="hr-daily-responsibilities" data-cat="utility">
    <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
    <h3>What are your daily responsibilities and what do you own?</h3>
    <p>They want to know your level: do you only take tickets, or do you own features? Show ownership from requirement to production.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Daily: stand-up, development, code reviews, bug fixing, helping teammates.</li>
      <li>Ownership: you take a feature end to end (clarify, design, build, test, release, monitor).</li>
      <li>Extra: documentation, performance, writing reusable components.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Sample answer: "My day starts with stand-up, then I work on my sprint stories, usually new features in the orders module. I review two or three pull requests a day and fix bugs raised by QA. I own the orders module: I talk to the product owner about requirements, plan the work, build it, write tests and watch it after release. About once a week I help juniors with Angular or RxJS doubts."</p>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Describe a normal day, then stress the parts you own from requirement to release.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="hr-biggest-achievement" data-cat="utility">
    <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
    <h3>What is your biggest technical achievement?</h3>
    <p>Pick one story with a clear problem and a number. Use <a href="#hr-star">STAR</a>. Use "I" for your actions, and show how the business gained.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Good topics: performance win, migration, big refactor, fixing a hard bug, building a reusable library.</li>
      <li>Always give before and after numbers.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Sample answer: "Situation: our reports page took about 9 seconds and users complained. Task: I was asked to fix it in one sprint. Action: I profiled it and found large tables re-rendering on every change detection, and one API call returning 40,000 rows. I moved to OnPush, added virtual scrolling, trackBy, and asked the backend team for server-side paging. I also cached filter lists with shareReplay. Result: load time dropped from 9 seconds to 1.5 seconds, support tickets on this page fell by about 60 percent, and I shared the approach with the team as a checklist."</p>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> One STAR story with the problem, your actions and before/after numbers beats a list of technologies.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="hr-disagree-lead" data-cat="utility">
    <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
    <h3>Tell me about a time you disagreed with your lead or architect</h3>
    <p>They test maturity. Show that you disagree with facts, respect the final decision, and keep the relationship good. Do not blame the person.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Present data or a small proof of concept, not opinions.</li>
      <li>Listen to their reasons; there may be context you did not know.</li>
      <li>If the decision stays, commit and support it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Sample answer: "Our architect wanted to put all state in NgRx. I felt it was too heavy for the small settings screens. I did not argue in the meeting. I wrote a short comparison with the boilerplate cost and showed a small prototype using services with signals. He agreed for local screens, but kept NgRx for shared data like the cart, with a good reason: easy debugging across teams. We documented both rules. I learned to bring examples and to understand the wider reason before pushing my view."</p>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Disagree with data and respect, accept the final call, and show a good result or learning.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="hr-production-incident" data-cat="utility">
    <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
    <h3>Tell me about a production incident you handled</h3>
    <p>They look for ownership under pressure and a calm process: stabilize first, then find the root cause, then prevent it.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Detect and tell the right people quickly.</li>
      <li>Reduce impact first (rollback, feature flag, hotfix).</li>
      <li>Find the root cause using logs, network tab, recent deployments.</li>
      <li>Fix, test and release; communicate status.</li>
      <li>Write a post-mortem and add a test or alert.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Sample answer: "After a release, users could not submit orders. A support alert reached me within ten minutes. I checked Sentry and saw a null error in a new form field. I first rolled back the release to restore service in 20 minutes, then reproduced it locally: the API returned null for old orders and my code did not handle it. I fixed it with a safe check, added a unit test, and released the next day. In the review we added a contract test and a canary release step. No data was lost, and I owned the update messages to the business team."</p>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Restore service first, then find the root cause, then add a test or process so it cannot repeat.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="hr-handle-feedback" data-cat="utility">
    <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
    <h3>How do you handle feedback?</h3>
    <p>Show that you listen, do not get defensive and act on it. Give a real example, including feedback you got in a code review.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Listen fully, ask for an example, thank the person.</li>
      <li>Make a small plan and follow up.</li>
      <li>You also give feedback kindly and specifically.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Sample answer: "I welcome feedback because it is the fastest way to grow. Once my lead said my pull requests were too large and hard to review. I asked for examples, then started splitting work into smaller PRs with a clear description. After a month, review time dropped from two days to a few hours, and he noticed. When I give feedback, I focus on the code, not the person, and offer a suggestion."</p>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Listen without defending, act on the feedback, and show the improvement it created.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="hr-explain-non-technical" data-cat="utility">
    <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
    <h3>How do you explain a technical issue to a non-technical person?</h3>
    <p>Use plain words, an everyday comparison, and focus on impact and next step. Skip the jargon.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Say what the user sees, why it happened (one line), what you are doing, and when it will be fixed.</li>
      <li>Use an analogy. Check if they understood.</li>
      <li>Offer options with simple trade-offs (time, cost, risk).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Sample answer: "Our manager asked why the page was slow. I said: 'The page asks the server for 40,000 rows at once, like asking a shop to carry the whole warehouse when you need one box. We will ask for 50 at a time. It takes two days and the page will open in about 2 seconds.' I then asked if it was clear and shared a short written summary."</p>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Explain impact, cause, fix and timeline in simple words with one analogy, and confirm they understood.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="hr-mentor-junior" data-cat="utility">
    <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
    <h3>How do you mentor a junior developer and review their pull request?</h3>
    <p>Show that you teach, not just correct. Be kind, clear and consistent.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Review for correctness, readability, tests, performance and security, in that order of importance.</li>
      <li>Explain why, give links or examples; ask questions instead of only giving orders.</li>
      <li>Praise good work. Mark small style points as optional (nit).</li>
      <li>Pair on hard tasks; let them try before you give the answer.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Sample answer: "When I review a junior's PR, I first say what is good. Then I comment on the important points, such as a missing unsubscribe, and explain the problem with a short example. For style issues I label them 'nit'. If the same mistake repeats, I do a 20 minute pairing session instead of more comments. One junior I mentored now reviews PRs on her own and ships features independently."</p>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Review to teach: praise first, explain the why, prioritise real issues, and pair when mistakes repeat.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="hr-estimation-changing-requirements" data-cat="utility">
    <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
    <h3>How do you estimate work and handle changing requirements?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Estimate:</b> break the story into small tasks, include testing, review and unknowns, add a buffer, and give a range when unsure.</li>
      <li><b>Clarify early:</b> ask questions and confirm the acceptance criteria before you start.</li>
      <li><b>Changes:</b> assess the impact, tell the owner what moves (scope, date or people), and agree in writing.</li>
      <li><b>Protect the code:</b> keep it modular and use feature flags so change is cheap.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Sample answer: "I split a story into tasks such as UI, API integration, tests and review, and estimate each. I add around 20 percent for unknowns and say '5 to 7 days' if risk is high. When requirements change midway, I explain the impact simply: 'This adds 2 days; we can move the date or drop the export feature.' The product owner picks, and we update the ticket. If I find I am going to be late, I say so early, not on the last day."</p>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Break work into tasks, give a range with a buffer, and treat changes as a visible trade-off, not a silent extra.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="hr-job-switches-tech-stack" data-cat="utility">
    <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
    <h3>Why did you switch jobs, have a gap, or choose this tech stack?</h3>
    <p>Be honest, short and positive. Show a clear story of growth. Never criticise past employers.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Job switch:</b> link each move to learning or responsibility.</li>
      <li><b>Career gap:</b> state the reason in one line (study, family, health, upskilling), and what you did and learned.</li>
      <li><b>Tech stack:</b> explain why you like Angular and TypeScript: structure, type safety, strong tools for large teams.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Sample answer: "My first job gave me a base in JavaScript and jQuery. I moved to my second company to work on a large Angular product, which grew my skills. I took a four month break for a family reason, and during that time I completed an RxJS and NgRx course and built a small project. I like Angular because TypeScript, DI and the CLI help big teams stay consistent, and I want to go deeper in it, which is why this role fits."</p>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Keep each switch or gap to a positive one-line reason and show how it moved your skills forward.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="hr-dont-know-answer" data-cat="utility">
    <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
    <h3>What do you do when you do not know the answer, in an interview or at work?</h3>
    <p>Honesty with a method is a strength. Do not bluff.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Interview: say what you know near the topic, reason out loud, and say how you would find the answer.</li>
      <li>Work: search docs, try a small prototype, ask a teammate after you have tried, and share what you learned.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Sample answer: "I have not used that API directly, but I know the related idea, and I would expect it to work like this. In a real project I would check the official docs, build a small test, and ask a senior colleague if I am still stuck. For example, when I first met NgRx effects, I read the docs, built a demo and then wrote a short note for my team."</p>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Say honestly what you do not know, show how you reason and learn, and never invent an answer.</div>
  </article>

  <article id="hr-quality-vs-speed" data-cat="utility">
    <span class="eyebrow">HR</span>
    <h3>How do you balance quality and speed, and handle technical debt?</h3>
    <p>There is no perfect answer, only conscious trade-offs. Show that you ship on time without ignoring the long-term cost.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Define a minimum bar: tests for critical paths, code review, no known security issues.</li>
      <li>Cut scope before cutting quality; ship in small steps with feature flags.</li>
      <li>Record shortcuts as tech-debt tickets and fix them with a fixed share of each sprint (for example 15 to 20 percent).</li>
      <li>Explain the business cost of debt: slower features and more bugs.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Sample answer: "Before a deadline I once used a quick workaround for a filter component. I told the product owner, added a ticket with the impact and fixed it in the next sprint using our debt time. I protect the basics, such as tests on the payment flow, but I will cut a nice-to-have feature to meet the date. I use feature flags to release half-finished work safely."</p>
    <div class="usecase know"><b>Know it:</b> Keep a non-negotiable quality bar, cut scope not quality, and track tech debt in tickets with planned time to repay it.</div>
  </article>
